    "yearRange": "2013-2016",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In facelift W212 form, the M274.910 gives the E 180 a noticeably more modern and more efficient character than the older naturally aspirated entry-level petrol engines that came before it. It was created to deliver respectable executive-car usability with lower taxation and better fuel economy on markets where engine displacement mattered heavily, which is why it appears most naturally in specific European and tax-sensitive regional configurations. On the road, it does not feel like a traditional large-capacity Mercedes engine, but it does feel clean, flexible, and sufficiently strong in normal driving, especially when paired with the smoother automatic calibration. Its personality is more about quiet efficiency and everyday refinement than emotional sound or effortless big-engine depth, but in a healthy car it suits the facelift W212 surprisingly well.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, adaptive ignition control, variable camshaft timing, and Mercedes/Bosch engine management. Compared with the older compressor or naturally aspirated four-cylinder Mercedes petrol engines, the M274.910 feels much more contemporary in the way it delivers torque early, responds cleanly in the middle of the rev range, and supports relaxed urban and motorway use without needing large displacement. The engine depends far more on calibration health, intake integrity, ignition quality, and clean oil than older simpler Mercedes petrol units, so condition matters much more than the modest output figure suggests. When everything is working properly, it feels refined, quiet, and efficient in a premium way; when neglected, it tends to feel thinner, less polished, and more hesitant than an E-Class should.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 320,000 km. The M274.910 can cover high mileage successfully, but it is not an engine that should be judged only by the badge or by the fact that it sits in an E-Class. A good example usually survives because it had frequent oil changes, proper cooling-system care, timely ignition service, and early diagnosis of timing, breather, turbo, or direct-injection-related issues before they became expensive. A neglected one often does not fail in one dramatic event, but instead deteriorates through timing noise, thermostat laziness, carbon buildup, oil leaks, rough cold starts, and a gradual loss of refinement. In practical terms, the engine is perfectly usable long-term when maintained properly, but it rewards disciplined ownership much more than the older, simpler Mercedes petrol engines did.",
    "turboType": "Single turbocharged direct-injection petrol inline-four engine. The E 180 does not try to feel sporty in an aggressive sense, but the turbocharged torque gives it a much more usable and flexible everyday character than a small naturally aspirated engine would have in a car this size. It feels strongest in the low and middle part of the rev range, where it can move the W212 with decent ease and much less effort than its displacement might suggest. Its appeal lies in efficiency, smooth low-end response, and tax-conscious market positioning rather than outright speed. In a healthy facelift W212, it works best as a quiet, modern, everyday executive drivetrain rather than as a performance engine.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz/Bosch engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock control, turbo boost coordination, and variable camshaft adjustment logic. This gives the M274.910 a polished factory calibration when healthy, allowing the engine to feel smoother and more substantial than its 1.6-liter size suggests. The downside is that the engine is more dependent on correct oil quality, clean intake and breather behavior, accurate ignition performance, and timely diagnostics than older Mercedes four-cylinders. Small issues in sensors, boost control, ignition, or crankcase ventilation can overlap and make the car feel weaker or rougher without immediately producing one obvious failure. That is why a properly maintained E 180 can feel genuinely refined, while a neglected one quickly feels more ordinary than the badge promises.",
