    "yearRange": "2009-2013",
    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain. The OM642 utilizes a single-row timing chain. While generally robust, it is known to stretch at higher mileages (250,000+ km), especially if oil change intervals are stretched. Listen carefully for cold-start rattle. The timing chain tensioner should be considered a wear item.",
    "injectionType": "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI) with Piezo injectors operating at up to 1600 bar. Extremely precise fuel atomization, but the injectors are sensitive to fuel quality. This specific version introduced early BlueTEC (SCR/AdBlue) to strictly meet Euro 6 emissions, which heavily restricted its power output to 211 hp.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The core mechanical block of the OM642 V6 is phenomenally strong. The true lifespan of the engine is entirely dictated by the maintenance of its complex peripherals: the infamous oil cooler seals, the BlueTEC AdBlue system, and the DPF/EGR components.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT). The Garrett variable geometry turbocharger provides massive low-end torque with zero lag. The electronic turbo actuator is prone to failure, often caused by heat soak or oil dripping from a failing PCV valve directly above it.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC17. A highly complex ECU managing not only the engine but the entire SCR (Selective Catalytic Reduction) AdBlue system. It is heavily restricted from the factory to keep emissions low but is incredibly receptive to software tuning.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "350+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and crankshaft are heavily over-engineered. The limitation is never the block, but rather the thermal limits of the turbocharger and the flow capacity of the DPF.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "8.5 - 10.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "5.5 - 6.8 L/100km",
      "mixed": "7.0 - 8.2 L/100km",
      "sporty": "9.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.0L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.52). Using MB 229.52 is strictly mandatory to prevent catastrophic DPF clogging and to protect the delicate internals of the BlueTEC system.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Oil Cooler Seals: The most infamous OM642 issue. The seals between the oil cooler and the block (in the 'V' of the engine) fail, leaking oil down the back of the engine. A $20 seal requires 10-12 hours of intensive labor to replace.",
      "BlueTEC AdBlue Heater Failure: The heater element inside the AdBlue tank burns out, triggering a countdown timer that will eventually prevent the car from starting.",
      "Intake Manifold Swirl Flap Motor (M55): Oil leaking from the turbo inlet seal drips directly onto the swirl flap motor, shorting it out and causing Limp Mode.",
      "NOx Sensor Failure: The BlueTEC system uses complex upstream and downstream NOx sensors that frequently fail and are highly expensive.",
      "PCV Valve Failure: The crankcase breather fails and dumps excessive oil into the turbocharger intake, destroying the swirl flap motor and fouling the intercooler."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Financial Ruin via Oil Cooler Seals: Ignoring the leak will cover the underside of the car in oil and destroy the serpentine belt, but the labor cost to fix it is immense.",
      "AdBlue Countdown to No-Start: If any BlueTEC component fails (heater, pump, NOx sensor), the ECU initiates a limited-starts countdown. Once it hits zero, the car becomes a brick.",
      "DPF Clogging: This engine MUST be driven on the highway. Consistent city driving will clog the DPF, leading to excessive backpressure and turbo failure.",
      "Turbo Actuator Short: A leaking PCV seal will literally drop oil into the turbo actuator's electronics, destroying the turbo system's brain.",
      "Timing Chain Stretch: Neglected oil changes lead to chain stretch, causing timing correlation faults and potential engine failure."
    ],
