    "valves": 16,
    "compression": "9.8:1",
    "yearRange": "2013-2016",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In facelift W212 form, the M274.920 gives the E 200 a more complete and more mature character than the earlier 1.8-liter M271-powered version, mainly because the stronger 300 Nm torque figure makes the car feel calmer and more substantial in ordinary driving. Mercedes used this newer 2.0-liter turbo petrol engine to give lower-displacement E-Class models a more convincing executive-car character without needing a six-cylinder layout. In real use, the extra torque matters more than the unchanged peak horsepower, because the car feels more relaxed in traffic, smoother on partial throttle, and better suited to the weight of the W212. When healthy, it delivers exactly the kind of quiet, modern, and efficient premium feel that a late petrol W212 should have.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition calibration, and Mercedes/Bosch engine management. Compared with the earlier M271 CGI engine, the M274.920 feels broader, more elastic, and more modern in its torque delivery, which makes the facelift E 200 easier to drive smoothly and more satisfying on normal roads. At the same time, it belongs to the modern Mercedes direct-injection turbo era, so it depends heavily on oil quality, cooling-system stability, ignition condition, intake cleanliness, and accurate diagnostics to stay refined. When all of those areas are in good shape, the engine feels much more premium than its displacement suggests. When neglected, it tends to feel thinner, rougher, and less impressive than the numbers on paper imply.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 340,000 km. The M274.920 can cover serious mileage, but long life depends much more on maintenance evidence than on simple confidence in the Mercedes badge. A strong example usually has regular oil service, timely spark plug replacement, sensible cooling-system care, and early diagnosis of timing-related behavior, breather problems, turbo irregularities, or deposit-related drivability changes. A weak example often declines through several medium-scale faults rather than one dramatic failure, which is why these engines can still feel acceptable during a short drive while hiding a larger deferred-maintenance story underneath. In practical ownership terms, the engine is fully capable of good durability, but only when it is maintained as a modern direct-injection turbo petrol engine rather than treated like an older, simpler Mercedes unit.",
    "turboType": "Single turbocharged direct-injection petrol inline-four engine. The facelift E 200 does not try to imitate the old big-capacity Mercedes feel, but the stronger torque output gives it enough depth to feel genuinely comfortable in a large executive sedan. It is strongest in the low and mid-range, where the engine can move the car with more ease than the older 1.8-liter E 200 and without needing constant revs. Its appeal lies in quiet usability, decent long-distance efficiency, and a more mature torque curve than the pre-facelift version. In a healthy late W212, it feels like a sensible and well-judged everyday premium drivetrain rather than a compromise.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz/Bosch engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock control, turbo boost coordination, and variable camshaft adjustment logic. This gives the M274.920 a cleaner and more polished factory calibration than older small Mercedes petrol engines, helping the facelift E 200 feel smoother and more cohesive in normal use. The downside is that the system depends on good maintenance discipline, because weak ignition components, breather issues, boost irregularities, oil-quality neglect, and cooling-system weakness can all overlap and reduce refinement without creating one obvious single failure. That subtle degradation is exactly why condition matters so much on used examples. The best cars feel quiet, strong enough, and very well judged, while weaker ones feel merely ordinary.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "205-220 hp. The M274.920 can accept mild software optimization, but in real ownership terms preserving factory smoothness, cooling stability, and gearbox quality usually matters more than chasing modest extra output.",
