    "yearRange": "2014-2016",
    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain. The OM642 utilizes a single-row timing chain. While generally robust, it stretches at higher mileages (250,000+ km), especially if oil change intervals are stretched. Listen carefully for cold-start rattle.",
    "injectionType": "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI) with highly advanced Piezo injectors operating at up to 1600 bar. This late-facelift iteration represents the final and most refined version of the OM642 in the W212, utilizing a highly complex SCR (AdBlue) system to meet strict Euro 6 regulations.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The core mechanical block of the OM642 V6 is phenomenally strong. The true lifespan is entirely dictated by the maintenance of its complex peripherals, specifically the oil cooler seals, the DPF, and the extremely fragile BlueTEC system.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT). The Garrett variable geometry turbocharger provides massive low-end torque with zero lag. The electronic turbo actuator is prone to failure, often caused by heat soak or oil dripping from a failing PCV valve.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC17. A highly complex ECU managing the fuel injection, DPF regenerations, and the SCR AdBlue dosing. It is incredibly receptive to software tuning, unlocking massive torque reserves.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "350+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and crankshaft are heavily over-engineered. The limitation is the thermal limit of the turbocharger and the flow capacity of the DPF.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "7.5 - 9.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "5.0 - 6.2 L/100km",
      "mixed": "6.0 - 7.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "8.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.0L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.52). Using MB 229.52 is strictly mandatory to prevent catastrophic DPF clogging and to protect the delicate internals of the BlueTEC system.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Oil Cooler Seals: The oil cooler seals located deep in the 'V' of the engine are notorious for failing, causing a massive oil leak. The original orange seals degrade from extreme heat, allowing oil to pool and run down the back of the engine block. Upgrading to the purple Viton seals is a mandatory fix for any OM642 owner.",
      "Turbo Inlet Seal: The orange 'batwing' seal connecting the intake tube to the turbocharger frequently deteriorates and leaks oil vapor. This oil drips directly onto the intake manifold swirl flap motor (M55) located directly below it. Once the motor is soaked, it shorts out and throws the car into Limp Mode.",
      "Timing Chain Stretch: Despite being a robust diesel, the OM642 uses a simplex (single-row) timing chain that stretches over high mileages. If the oil is not changed strictly at 10,000 km intervals, the chain elongates and the tensioner fails. This results in a loud rattle on cold startup and eventual timing correlation faults.",
      "PCV Valve Failure: The crankcase ventilation valve is highly prone to failing and passing excessive oil vapor into the intake tract. This exacerbates the turbo inlet seal leaking issue and coats the intercooler in thick oil sludge. Replacing it preventatively every 80,000 km is highly recommended to protect the turbocharger.",
      "EGR and Intake Clogging: The Exhaust Gas Recirculation system actively feeds soot back into the intake manifold to lower emissions. Over time, this mixes with PCV oil vapor to create a thick, tar-like sludge that chokes the intake ports. This completely jams the swirl flaps and drastically reduces engine performance.",
      "AdBlue Heater and NOx Sensors: The Euro 6 BlueTEC SCR system is notoriously fragile. The heater element inside the AdBlue tank frequently burns out, and the highly sensitive NOx sensors fail regularly. These components are extremely expensive to replace and will disable the vehicle if left broken."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Financial Ruin via Oil Cooler: While the replacement purple Viton seals cost less than twenty euros, the labor required to access them is monumental. The mechanic must remove the turbocharger, intake manifolds, and EGR system, resulting in 10 to 12 hours of highly expensive labor. Ignoring this leak will ultimately destroy the serpentine belt and coat the undercarriage in oil.",
      "DPF Blockage: Modern diesels like the OM642 absolutely require regular highway driving to perform high-temperature DPF regenerations. If the car is strictly used for short urban commutes, the Diesel Particulate Filter will fatally clog with excessive soot. Replacing a clogged DPF is a massive financial blow, often exceeding thousands of euros.",
      "Transmission Torque Overload: The massive torque output places immense strain on the automatic transmission clutch packs and torque converter over time. If the fluid is not changed strictly every 120,000 kilometers for the 9G or 60,000 for the 7G, the transmission will begin to shudder heavily under strong acceleration. Rebuilding a damaged transmission is catastrophically expensive and requires specialized programming.",
      "Swirl Flap Motor Destruction: As mentioned, oil dripping from the turbo inlet will instantly destroy the M55 swirl flap motor. However, the true vulnerability is that replacing this motor requires completely removing the turbocharger and intake manifolds. A simple, cheap plastic part failure cascades into a massive labor bill at the repair shop.",
      "Injector Seating (Black Death): While less common than on older inline-4 diesels, the copper crush washers under the Piezo injectors can fail. This allows combustion gases to escape and bake raw diesel fuel into a solid carbon mass on top of the cylinder head. Catching this early is critical to avoid destroying the expensive injector and the cylinder head itself.",
      "AdBlue No-Start Countdown: The SCR system relies on highly fragile NOx sensors and an AdBlue tank heater that frequently burns out. If the ECU detects an emissions fault, it will trigger a countdown timer allowing only a specific number of engine starts. Once this hits zero, the vehicle is completely immobilized until repaired at a dealership."
    ],
