    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.51 or 229.52). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is the only way to protect the vulnerable rear timing chain and twin-turbo bearings.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Timing Chain Placement and Stretch: The OM651 features a single-row timing chain notoriously mounted at the rear of the engine block. When it stretches, replacing it often requires pulling the entire engine or transmission out. Listen carefully for an aggressive metallic rattle upon cold starts, which indicates a failing tensioner.",
      "High-Voltage Battery Degradation: The compact lithium-ion hybrid battery situated in the engine bay is subjected to extreme heat cycles. Over time, the internal cells degrade, leading to hybrid system faults and a complete loss of electric propulsion. Replacing this specific battery pack at the dealer is an incredibly expensive endeavor.",
      "Delphi Piezo Injector Failure: Early iterations of the OM651 were equipped with Delphi piezo injectors that failed at an alarming rate. While many were replaced under warranty with updated solenoid injectors, some cars still run on older units. A failed injector will cause severe misfires, rough idling, and can leave the vehicle stranded.",
      "Coolant System Leaks: The engine utilizes a complex array of plastic coolant flanges and a water pump that become brittle with age and heat. The water pump housing frequently develops hairline cracks, leading to slow but persistent coolant loss. Catching these leaks early is vital to prevent severe engine overheating.",
      "Hybrid Stator and Transmission Integration: The electric motor is sandwiched between the engine and the 7G-Tronic transmission, replacing the traditional torque converter. This complex integration often leads to rough, jerky transitions between electric and diesel power. Additionally, internal stator cooling leaks can destroy the electric motor entirely.",
      "Intake Manifold Swirl Flap Wear: The plastic swirl flaps inside the intake manifold accumulate heavy soot and oil sludge from the EGR and PCV systems. Eventually, the actuator linkages break or the flaps seize completely. This drastically reduces performance and requires replacing the entire intake manifold assembly."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Repair Costs: Because the timing chain is positioned against the firewall, access is extraordinarily difficult. What is a standard maintenance task on other engines becomes a massive, high-labor financial burden. Neglecting the chain stretch will result in valves hitting the pistons, destroying the engine.",
      "Hybrid System Immobilization: If the high-voltage battery or the hybrid control module fails, the car does not simply revert to a standard diesel. The entire vehicle will refuse to start, leaving you completely stranded. The proprietary nature of these hybrid components means independent shops often cannot perform the repairs.",
      "DPF Blockage from Urban Commuting: Hybrid diesels are heavily marketed for city efficiency, but the OM651 still relies on high exhaust temperatures to regenerate the DPF. Frequent stop-and-go driving with the engine constantly shutting off prevents regenerations. This causes the filter to fatally clog, requiring an extremely expensive replacement.",
      "Aggressive Engine Mount Deterioration: The constant starting and stopping of the heavy diesel block places immense, repetitive strain on the hydraulic engine mounts. They collapse much faster than in standard non-hybrid models, transferring harsh vibrations directly into the cabin. Replacing them is a necessary but costly recurring maintenance item.",
      "Oil Dilution Risks: Interrupted DPF regenerations caused by the hybrid system shutting the engine off can lead to unburnt diesel fuel washing down the cylinder walls. This diesel heavily dilutes the engine oil, significantly reducing its lubricating properties. This drastically accelerates wear on the main bearings and the delicate rear timing chain.",
      "Electric Water Pump Failure: The hybrid system utilizes an auxiliary electric water pump to maintain cabin heating and battery cooling when the diesel engine is off. This pump is prone to electrical failure or internal seizing over time. If it fails, the high-voltage system can overheat, triggering a cascade of dashboard warning lights."
    ],
