    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain. The OM642 utilizes a single-row timing chain. While generally robust, it will stretch at higher mileages (250,000+ km) if oil change intervals are neglected. A sharp metallic rattle on cold starts indicates a failing tensioner and stretched chain.",
    "injectionType": "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI) with Piezo injectors operating at up to 1600 bar. This model acts as the entry-level V6 diesel, heavily detuned via software to 204 hp to offer a smoother, more refined alternative to the 4-cylinder diesels.",
    "engineLifespan": "350 000 - 600 000+ km. Because this engine is severely detuned from the factory, the internal mechanical stress is practically non-existent. The core block will last forever; the true lifespan is dictated by the maintenance of the oil cooler seals and DPF.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT). The Garrett turbocharger provides massive low-end torque. The electronic actuator is a known failure point, often ruined by oil dripping from the PCV valve.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC17. This complex ECU manages the detuned fueling map. It is incredibly receptive to software tuning, easily unlocking the true 260+ hp potential of the OM642 block.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "350+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and crankshaft are heavily over-engineered. The limitation is the thermal limit of the turbocharger and the flow capacity of the DPF.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "8.5 - 10.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "5.5 - 6.8 L/100km",
      "mixed": "6.8 - 8.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "9.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.0L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.51). Using Low-SAPS oil is strictly mandatory to prevent catastrophic DPF clogging.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Oil Cooler Seals Leak: The notorious orange Viton seals located deep in the engine valley eventually harden and fail. This causes a massive oil leak down the back of the engine block. Repairing this requires extensive labor to remove the turbo and intake system.",
      "Turbo Inlet Seal Degradation: The orange batwing seal connecting the intake to the turbocharger frequently deteriorates. Oil vapor leaks from this seal directly onto the swirl flap motor situated below it. This instantly shorts out the motor and triggers Limp Mode.",
      "Timing Chain Stretch: While generally robust, the simplex single-row timing chain will stretch on high-mileage engines. This is heavily accelerated if oil change intervals are neglected or stretched out. You will hear a distinct, sharp metallic rattle on cold starts when the tensioner fails.",
      "PCV Valve Failure: The crankcase ventilation valve is highly prone to failing and passing excessive oil vapor. This coats the entire intake tract and intercooler in thick oil sludge. Replacing it preventatively is crucial to protect the turbocharger's internals.",
      "EGR and Intake Clogging: The Exhaust Gas Recirculation system feeds soot back into the engine, mixing with oil vapor. This creates a thick, tar-like sludge that chokes the intake ports and jams the swirl flaps. It drastically reduces performance and fuel economy over time.",
      "Glow Plug and Module Failure: The solid-state glow plug control module is a frequent failure point, especially in winter. Furthermore, the glow plugs themselves seize in the aluminum cylinder head. Removing them requires immense care to prevent snapping them off inside the engine."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Immense Oil Cooler Labor Costs: The replacement purple Viton seals are incredibly cheap, but the access requires removing the entire top end of the engine. This transforms a minor seal failure into a 10 to 12-hour labor bill. Ignoring the leak will coat the undercarriage and destroy the serpentine belt.",
      "DPF Blockage from Urban Driving: The OM642 absolutely requires regular highway driving to achieve the high exhaust temperatures needed for DPF regeneration. Strict urban commuting will cause the filter to fatally clog with soot. A clogged DPF creates immense backpressure, ultimately destroying the turbocharger and requiring thousands in repairs.",
      "Transmission Torque Strain: Even heavily detuned to 204 horsepower, the OM642 produces a massive 500 Nm of torque. This places significant long-term strain on the 7G-Tronic transmission's torque converter and clutch packs. Ignoring the strict 60,000 km transmission fluid service will lead to aggressive shuddering and failure.",
      "Swirl Flap Motor Destruction: Oil dripping from the turbo inlet will instantly destroy the M55 swirl flap motor. The vulnerability lies in the fact that replacing this inexpensive motor requires completely removing the turbocharger and intake manifolds. It is a cascading failure that results in a massive labor bill.",
      "Injector 'Black Death': The copper crush washers under the Piezo injectors can fail, allowing combustion gases to escape. This bakes raw diesel fuel into a solid carbon mass on top of the cylinder head. Catching this early is critical to avoid destroying the expensive injectors and the head itself.",
      "Rear Subframe Corrosion: Early W212 models are highly susceptible to severe rear subframe corrosion caused by road salt. The metal rots from the inside out, potentially causing the rear control arms to tear off completely. This is a massive safety hazard requiring a complete subframe replacement."
    ],
