    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain. The OM642 utilizes a single-row timing chain. While generally robust, it will stretch at higher mileages (250,000+ km) if oil change intervals are neglected. A sharp metallic rattle on cold starts indicates a failing tensioner and stretched chain.",
    "injectionType": "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI) with Piezo injectors operating at up to 1600 bar. This model bridges the gap into the Euro 6 era by introducing the highly complex BlueTEC (SCR/AdBlue) system to clean exhaust emissions.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The core mechanical block of the OM642 V6 is phenomenally strong. The true lifespan is entirely dictated by the maintenance of its complex peripherals, specifically the oil cooler seals, the DPF, and the fragile BlueTEC system.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT). The Garrett turbocharger provides massive low-end torque. The electronic actuator is a known failure point, often ruined by oil dripping from the PCV valve.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC17. This complex ECU manages not only the engine fueling but the entire AdBlue dosing system. It is incredibly receptive to software tuning to remove the factory restrictions.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "350+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and crankshaft are heavily over-engineered. The limitation is the thermal limit of the turbocharger and the flow capacity of the DPF.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "8.5 - 10.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "5.5 - 6.8 L/100km",
      "mixed": "6.8 - 8.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "9.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.0L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.52). Using MB 229.52 is strictly mandatory to prevent catastrophic DPF clogging and to protect the SCR catalyst.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Oil Cooler Seals Leak: The notorious orange seals located deep in the engine valley eventually harden and fail. This causes a massive oil leak down the back of the engine block. Repairing this requires extensive labor to remove the turbo and intake system.",
      "Turbo Inlet Seal Degradation: The orange batwing seal connecting the intake to the turbocharger frequently deteriorates. Oil vapor leaks from this seal directly onto the swirl flap motor situated below it. This instantly shorts out the motor and triggers Limp Mode.",
      "Timing Chain Stretch: While generally robust, the simplex single-row timing chain will stretch on high-mileage engines. This is heavily accelerated if oil change intervals are neglected or stretched out. You will hear a distinct, sharp metallic rattle on cold starts when the tensioner fails.",
      "PCV Valve Failure: The crankcase ventilation valve is highly prone to failing and passing excessive oil vapor. This coats the entire intake tract and intercooler in thick oil sludge. Replacing it preventatively is crucial to protect the turbocharger's internals.",
      "EGR and Intake Clogging: The Exhaust Gas Recirculation system feeds soot back into the engine, mixing with oil vapor. This creates a thick, tar-like sludge that chokes the intake ports and jams the swirl flaps. It drastically reduces performance and fuel economy over time.",
      "AdBlue Tank Heater Burnout: The Euro 6 BlueTEC SCR system requires an internal heater in the AdBlue tank to prevent freezing. This heating element burns out with alarming regularity. It forces a complete replacement of the tank assembly or the internal pump unit.",
      "NOx Sensor Failures: The complex emissions system relies on upstream and downstream NOx sensors that are incredibly fragile. They frequently fail due to soot buildup or sensor degradation. They are exceptionally expensive to replace and will trigger an engine warning light."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Immense Oil Cooler Labor Costs: The replacement purple Viton seals are incredibly cheap, but access requires removing the entire top end of the engine. This transforms a minor seal failure into a 10 to 12-hour labor bill. Ignoring the leak will coat the undercarriage and destroy the serpentine belt.",
      "AdBlue No-Start Countdown: If the ECU detects an emissions fault from a failed heater or NOx sensor, it triggers a hard countdown timer. You will only be allowed a specific number of engine starts before the vehicle immobilizes itself. Once it hits zero, the car becomes a brick until repaired at the dealer.",
      "DPF Blockage from Urban Driving: The OM642 absolutely requires regular highway driving to achieve the high exhaust temperatures needed for DPF regeneration. Strict urban commuting will cause the filter to fatally clog with soot. A clogged DPF creates immense backpressure, ultimately destroying the turbocharger.",
      "Transmission Torque Strain: The 540 Nm of torque places significant long-term strain on the 7G-Tronic transmission. Ignoring the strict 60,000 km transmission fluid service will lead to aggressive shuddering and conductor plate failure. Rebuilding a damaged transmission is catastrophically expensive.",
      "Swirl Flap Motor Destruction: Oil dripping from the turbo inlet will instantly destroy the M55 swirl flap motor. The vulnerability lies in the fact that replacing this inexpensive motor requires completely removing the turbocharger and intake manifolds. It is a cascading failure that results in a massive labor bill.",
      "Injector 'Black Death': The copper crush washers under the Piezo injectors can fail, allowing combustion gases to escape. This bakes raw diesel fuel into a solid carbon mass on top of the cylinder head. Catching this early is critical to avoid destroying the expensive injectors.",
      "Rear Subframe Corrosion: Early W212 models are highly susceptible to severe rear subframe corrosion caused by road salt. The metal rots from the inside out, potentially causing the rear control arms to tear off completely. This is a massive safety hazard requiring a complete subframe replacement."
    ],
