    "compression": "9.3:1",
    "yearRange": "2011-2013",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven supercharged inline-four timing layout. In the W212 E 200 NGT BlueEFFICIENCY, the M271.958 is a niche bi-fuel version built mainly for high-mileage fleet, taxi, and cost-focused business use rather than private enthusiast demand. Its main appeal is not speed but fuel flexibility, because it can run on compressed natural gas as well as petrol, making it attractive in markets where CNG infrastructure and operating costs justified the complexity. Compared with ordinary petrol W212 E 200 models, it feels more utilitarian in concept, but still retains the calm rear-wheel-drive E-Class character. The drivetrain is best understood as a durability-and-running-cost package rather than a sporty or especially elegant powertrain solution.",
    "injectionType": "Multi-port manifold injection with supercharging, intercooling, and bi-fuel petrol/CNG operation. This setup is mechanically more old-school than later direct-injection turbo Mercedes four-cylinders, which can be a benefit for long-term simplicity in some areas, but the added natural-gas hardware introduces its own complexity. In real ownership, the engine itself is only one part of the story, because injectors, regulators, gas lines, tank equipment, switching logic, and fuel-system integration all matter. When the whole system is healthy, the car can be very cost-effective and smooth enough for daily use. When the gas side is neglected or poorly diagnosed, it can become much more troublesome than a standard petrol E 200.",
    "engineLifespan": "250,000 to 420,000 km. The M271.958 can cover very high mileage when used in the role it was designed for, especially steady-distance driving with disciplined servicing and proper attention to both petrol and CNG systems. Many of these cars were bought for commercial or fleet use, which means mileage itself is less alarming than poor maintenance records, repeated short-term fixes, or neglected gas-system servicing. The engine can last well, but ownership risk rises quickly when timing issues, supercharger-related wear, oil leaks, fuel switching faults, or CNG hardware ageing are ignored. A good one can be a durable workhorse; a neglected one can become complicated and expensive in several small ways at once.",
    "turboType": "Supercharged inline-four petrol/CNG bi-fuel engine with intercooling. This is not a torque-rich modern turbo setup, but a more linear, older-school forced-induction unit that suits steady driving and cost-conscious mileage accumulation. In the heavy W212 body, performance is adequate rather than impressive, and the drivetrain makes most sense for buyers who value range flexibility and lower fuel costs over effortless acceleration. The character is closer to dependable commercial transport than premium-petrol indulgence. When operating correctly on both fuels, it feels honest and usable rather than exciting.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz / Siemens bi-fuel engine management controlling petrol injection, supercharger operation, ignition strategy, and CNG system integration. This means the engine can be perfectly acceptable in daily use when both fuel systems are properly calibrated, but diagnosis becomes more demanding than on a normal petrol model. Many drivability complaints can come from fuel switching, pressure regulation, gas injectors, ignition weakness, or adaptation issues rather than one obvious mechanical failure. Because of that, service history and specialist knowledge matter more than usual. The best examples feel stable and predictable, while poor ones often suffer from repeated unresolved running faults.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "175-185 hp. Mild gains are possible, but on this version the smarter priority is preserving reliability, fuel-system integrity, and dual-fuel drivability rather than tuning.",
