    "yearRange": "2014-2016",
    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain located at the REAR of the engine block. The OM651 timing chain is highly controversial due to its placement. While it can last 200,000+ km with strict oil changes, chain stretch requires dropping the engine or transmission to replace. Listen closely for a metallic rattle on cold starts.",
    "injectionType": "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI) up to 2000 bar. This late facelift version uses highly reliable solenoid injectors and integrates a complex SCR (AdBlue) system to meet Euro 6 compliance. It is tuned specifically for maximum fuel efficiency and tax optimization.",
    "engineLifespan": "350 000 - 600 000+ km. Because this engine is severely detuned to 136 hp, the internal mechanical stress is practically non-existent. The core block will last forever; the true lifespan is dictated entirely by the maintenance of the rear timing chain and the complex BlueTEC emissions system.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT). Unlike the higher-output 220/250 CDI models, the 200 CDI uses a single turbocharger, which significantly improves long-term reliability. It lacks the complex vacuum systems of the bi-turbo setup.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch EDC17. A highly complex ECU managing the fuel injection, DPF regenerations, and the SCR AdBlue dosing. It is incredibly receptive to software tuning, safely unlocking the power reserves of the underlying OM651 block.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "220+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and crankshaft are heavily over-engineered for just 136 hp. The block itself handles increased boost effortlessly, limited only by the DPF and single turbo thermal limits.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "5.5 - 6.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "4.2 - 5.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "4.5 - 5.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "7.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.52). Using MB 229.52 is strictly mandatory to prevent catastrophic DPF clogging and to protect the delicate rear timing chain.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Timing Chain Stretch: The OM651 engine famously locates its simplex timing chain at the rear of the block, making access incredibly difficult. Due to its single-row design, it stretches over high mileages, leading to a failing tensioner. You will hear a distinct, sharp metallic rattle on cold starts when this occurs.",
      "BlueTEC SCR System Failures: To meet Euro 6 standards, this model relies heavily on the complex AdBlue injection system. The internal tank heater frequently burns out, and the delicate NOx sensors degrade rapidly from soot. These failures trigger immediate dashboard warnings and severe emission faults.",
      "Water Pump and Coolant Leaks: The engine utilizes a complex array of plastic coolant flanges and a water pump housing that become brittle with heat. These frequently develop hairline cracks, leading to slow but persistent coolant loss. Catching these leaks early is vital to prevent severe engine overheating.",
      "Intake Manifold Swirl Flaps: The plastic swirl flaps inside the intake manifold accumulate heavy soot and oil sludge from the EGR system. Eventually, the actuator linkages break or the flaps seize completely. This drastically reduces performance and throws the car into Limp Mode.",
      "DPF Pressure Sensor: The differential pressure sensor monitoring the Diesel Particulate Filter fails with alarming regularity on this chassis. When this happens, the ECU cannot trigger regenerations, leading to a rapidly clogging filter. It is a relatively cheap part that can cause massive peripheral damage if ignored.",
      "Fuel Injector Wear: While late models received updated solenoid injectors, they still operate at incredibly high pressures and are sensitive to fuel quality. Failure of these components leads to severe misfires and excessively rough idling. They must be monitored closely to prevent internal engine damage."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Engine-Out Timing Chain Repair: Because the timing chain is positioned practically against the firewall, what is a standard maintenance task becomes a massive financial burden. Mechanics often must drop the transmission or the entire engine just to replace the chain. Neglecting the chain stretch will result in pistons destroying the valves.",
      "AdBlue No-Start Countdown: If the ECU detects an emissions fault from a failed heater or NOx sensor, it triggers a hard countdown timer. You will only be allowed a specific number of engine starts before the vehicle immobilizes itself. Once it hits zero, the car becomes a brick until repaired at the dealer.",
      "Catastrophic Coolant Loss: The plastic water pump and coolant flanges can fail without much warning, dumping coolant rapidly. If the driver ignores the temperature gauge, the aluminum cylinder head will warp under the intense heat. This necessitates an incredibly expensive top-end engine rebuild.",
      "Intake Manifold Replacement: A simple, cheap plastic swirl flap linkage failure cannot be fixed with an OEM patch kit. Mercedes requires you to purchase an entirely new intake manifold assembly to solve the issue. This cascades a minor wear issue into a significant parts and labor bill.",
      "DPF Blockage from Urban Driving: The OM651 absolutely requires regular highway driving to achieve the high exhaust temperatures needed for DPF regeneration. Strict urban commuting will cause the filter to fatally clog with dense soot. A clogged DPF creates immense backpressure, ultimately destroying the turbocharger assembly.",
      "Injector 'Black Death': The copper crush washers under the high-pressure injectors can fail, allowing combustion gases to escape the cylinder. This bakes raw diesel fuel into a solid carbon mass on top of the cylinder head. Catching this early is critical to avoid destroying the expensive injectors and the head itself."
    ],
