    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In facelift W212 Natural Gas Drive form, the M274.920 is a more modern but also more system-dependent drivetrain than the older M271-based NGT version. It combines the newer-generation 2.0-liter turbo architecture with a factory CNG setup, so the result is more refined and lower-revving in character than the earlier supercharged 1.8-liter gas car. It still belongs to a niche fleet-style executive configuration, but it feels more mature and more torque-efficient in normal driving. The engine should be understood as a modern efficiency-focused bi-fuel unit rather than a simple low-cost taxi motor.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, and factory petrol/CNG bi-fuel integration. Compared with the earlier M271 NGT, the M274-based facelift car is technically more advanced and generally smoother in torque delivery, but it also depends more heavily on clean diagnostics, correct ignition behavior, cooling-system stability, and healthy gas-system calibration. The engine can feel very civilized when everything is working properly, yet it becomes more difficult to diagnose when petrol-side, turbo-side, and CNG-side problems overlap. In practice, this means workshop quality matters more than usual. A good one feels refined and efficient, while a neglected one can develop multiple medium-size drivability issues at once.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 360,000 km. The M274.920 Natural Gas Drive can cover serious mileage, but long life depends on disciplined oil changes, ignition quality, cooling-system health, clean intake operation, and proper servicing of the gas side. Mileage alone is not the key risk on these cars; the bigger problem is whether the dual-fuel system, turbo hardware, and engine management were maintained correctly over time. A strong example can remain smooth and economical for years, but a weak one often declines through timing wear, thermostat faults, carbon buildup, breather problems, turbo irregularities, and neglected CNG hardware. It is best treated as a modern specialist drivetrain that rewards evidence, not assumptions.",
    "turboType": "Single turbocharged direct-injection inline-four with factory bi-fuel petrol/CNG capability. This version is not fast, but it suits the W212 better than the older 1.8-liter NGT because the broader torque delivery gives the car a calmer and more settled feel in daily use. The drivetrain is geared toward efficient long-distance driving, tax-conscious operating costs, and dual-fuel flexibility rather than performance. Its character is smooth, restrained, and businesslike. In healthy condition it feels more complete than the older NGT setup.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz / Bosch engine management with direct-injection control, turbo boost coordination, knock regulation, adaptive fueling, variable cam timing, and factory CNG integration. This allows the engine to run with a cleaner and more modern calibration than the older supercharged NGT version, but it also means fault tracing can be more complex because petrol-side, boost-side, ignition-side, and gas-side problems can influence one another. Proper diagnosis is essential on these cars. Many weak examples are not catastrophically broken, but simply poorly maintained and badly interpreted. The best cars feel stable, clean-running, and very well integrated on both fuels.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "170-185 hp. Mild gains are possible, but the better priority on this version is preserving smooth dual-fuel operation, cooling stability, and long-term reliability rather than chasing extra output.",
