    "yearRange": "2013-2016",
    "timingType": "Double-roller chain. The M157 is infamous for timing chain stretch and rapid wear of the camshaft adjusters. Listen carefully for a sharp metallic rattle upon cold startup, which indicates failing tensioners and depleted adjusters. Preventative maintenance here is absolutely mandatory to prevent catastrophic engine failure.",
    "injectionType": "Direct Injection (CGI) with high-pressure Piezo injectors. Extremely efficient and capable of supplying immense fuel volumes under high boost. It completely abandons the port injection of the older M156, introducing the risk of carbon buildup on the intake valves.",
    "engineLifespan": "150 000 - 250 000+ km. The M157 block is immensely strong, but its lifespan is heavily dictated by rigorous maintenance. Neglecting timing chain rattles or ignoring failing fuel injectors will inevitably lead to fatal cylinder wall scoring or bent connecting rods.",
    "turboType": "Twin Garrett Honeywel Turbochargers. Nestled on the sides of the block, they provide instantaneous boost and massive mid-range torque. The plastic coolant lines feeding the turbos become extremely brittle from heat and are prone to sudden snapping.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MED17.7.3. A highly sophisticated engine control unit managing direct injection, twin turbos, and ignition. It is exceptionally tunable, easily allowing the 557 hp engine to produce well over 700 horsepower with a simple software flash.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "750+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods are robust but represent the engine's weak link when subjected to immense low-end torque. Pushing past 900 Nm of torque requires careful tuning to prevent bending a connecting rod.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "14.5 - 17.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "9.8 - 11.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "11.5 - 13.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "22.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.5L 5W-40 or 0W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Absolute adherence to strict 5,000-mile (8,000 km) oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the delicate twin-turbos and the highly stressed camshaft adjusters.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Timing Chain and Cam Adjusters: The M157 is infamous for premature timing chain stretch and rapidly degrading camshaft adjusters. You will typically hear a loud, sharp metallic rattle on cold starts lasting a few seconds before oil pressure builds. If left ignored, the timing will jump, causing pistons to strike the valves and destroying the engine.",
      "Turbocharger Coolant Line Leaks: The turbochargers are cooled by a network of thin plastic coolant lines that sit directly above the scorching hot exhaust manifolds. After a few years, these plastic lines become incredibly brittle and simply disintegrate. This leads to a sudden, massive loss of engine coolant and severe overheating if not caught immediately.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because the M157 utilizes direct injection, fuel no longer washes over the intake valves to keep them clean. Over time, thick layers of carbon sludge bake onto the intake valves and ports, severely restricting airflow. This causes rough idling, misfires, and a noticeable loss of top-end power, requiring professional walnut blasting.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Failure: The engine relies on two camshaft-driven high-pressure fuel pumps to supply the direct injectors. These pumps frequently wear out internally or leak fuel into the engine oil, leading to long cranking times and Limp Mode. A failing HPFP leans out the engine under boost, which can instantly melt a piston.",
      "MCT Wet Clutch Shudder: Instead of a traditional torque converter, the AMG Speedshift MCT transmission utilizes a multi-plate wet clutch pack for sharper shifts. This clutch pack requires religious fluid changes and frequent software adaptations to remain smooth. If neglected, taking off from a dead stop will feel extremely jerky and the clutch pack will eventually burn out.",
      "Ignition Coil Degradation: The twin-turbo V8 generates extreme under-hood temperatures that literally bake the ignition coils over time. As the internal resistance increases, the coils fail to provide a strong spark under heavy boost, leading to violent engine misfires. Replacing the coils alongside the spark plugs every 60,000 kilometers is highly recommended."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Fatal Cylinder Wall Scoring: The M157 utilizes a Silitec aluminum block coating that is highly susceptible to scoring if a fuel injector fails and washes the lubricating oil away. This results in severe oil consumption, aggressive misfires, and a heavy knocking sound from the bottom end. Repairing this requires pulling the engine, machining the block, and installing aftermarket steel sleeves, which is financially ruinous.",
      "Bent Connecting Rods: While the engine is incredibly strong, the connecting rods are the Achilles heel when adding aftermarket tuning software. If a tuner aggressively increases low-end boost, the resulting massive torque spike will physically bend the connecting rods. This catastrophic failure necessitates a complete bottom-end engine rebuild with forged aftermarket components.",
      "Camshaft Adjuster Failure: The internal locking pins within the variable camshaft adjusters wear down rapidly due to poor oil pressure on cold starts. When they fail completely, the camshaft timing deviates wildly from the crankshaft, causing severe internal engine collisions. Upgrading to aftermarket billet adjuster plates is the only permanent solution to this expensive design flaw.",
      "AirMatic Suspension Collapse: The heavy AMG V8 places immense stress on the standard AirMatic air suspension struts and the pneumatic compressor. The front struts frequently develop leaks in the airbags, causing the car to sag directly onto the tires overnight. Replacing the struts with quality aftermarket units like Arnott is mandatory, as OEM replacements are prohibitively expensive.",
      "Intercooler Pump Heat Soak: The air-to-water intercooler system relies on a small electric pump to circulate coolant and lower intake temperatures. This factory pump is drastically undersized and frequently fails, causing intake air temperatures to skyrocket after a single hard pull. The ECU will immediately pull engine timing and cut boost, leaving the car feeling severely underpowered.",
      "MCT Transmission Rebuilds: The sophisticated 7-speed MCT transmission is brilliant when functioning, but the internal wet clutch pack wears out just like a manual transmission clutch. If the vehicle has been subjected to frequent launch control starts, the friction plates will completely disintegrate. Replacing the wet clutch assembly is an intensely labor-heavy and costly procedure."
    ],
