    "compression": "11.3:1",
    "yearRange": "2009-2011",
    "timingType": "Double-roller chain. The M156 timing chain itself is highly robust. However, the catastrophic vulnerability lies in the camshafts and valve lifters (tappets). The soft camshaft lobes grind against oil-starved lifters, leading to severe valvetrain destruction. A pronounced ticking sound from the top of the engine is the death rattle of the camshafts.",
    "injectionType": "Multi-Point Sequential Fuel Injection (Port Injection). A massive blessing for reliability, as the fuel constantly washes over the intake valves, keeping them entirely free of the severe carbon buildup that plagues the later direct-injected M157 engines.",
    "engineLifespan": "150 000 - 250 000+ km. The lifespan of the legendary M156 is entirely dependent on whether the catastrophic factory head bolts have been replaced and if the valvetrain (camshafts/lifters) has been upgraded. If these are ignored, the engine will destroy itself well before 150,000 km.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. The glorious 6.2-liter V8 breathes entirely on its own, providing the most instantaneous, razor-sharp throttle response of any modern AMG engine. The auditory experience of this naturally aspirated giant revving past 7,000 RPM is legendary.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch ME 9.7. A highly sophisticated engine control unit managing the four variable camshafts and the dual-length intake manifold. It responds very well to tuning, though the gains are naturally less dramatic than on turbocharged engines.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "600+ hp naturally aspirated, 750+ hp supercharged. The forged internal rotating assembly is incredibly strong. Bolting a Weistec supercharger to the M156 is highly popular and easily pushes the block to immense power levels without opening the bottom end.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "16.0 - 19.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "11.0 - 13.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "13.5 - 15.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "25.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.5L 5W-40 or 0W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Absolute adherence to strict 5,000-mile (8,000 km) oil change intervals using high-zinc, high-moly oil is the only way to delay the inevitable camshaft and lifter wear.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Head Bolt Failure: The M156 is notorious for its factory head bolts stretching or snapping. This allows coolant to leak directly into the combustion chamber or mix with the engine oil. It is a catastrophic flaw that requires immediate preventative replacement using upgraded SLS-style head bolts.",
      "Camshaft and Lifter Wear: The factory valve lifters bleed down oil pressure, causing a harsh tapping noise on startup. The soft camshaft lobes rapidly wear against these starved lifters, creating deep scores and requiring complete valvetrain replacement. Upgrading to SLS Black Series valve buckets is highly recommended.",
      "Intake Manifold Leaks: The magnesium intake manifold utilizes an internal center plate that degrades and cracks over time. This creates a severe vacuum leak, causing rough idling, lean running conditions, and triggering the check engine light. Rebuilding the manifold with an aftermarket billet center plate is the only permanent solution.",
      "MCT Wet Clutch Shudder: Instead of a traditional torque converter, the AMG Speedshift MCT utilizes a multi-plate wet clutch pack. This clutch pack requires religious fluid changes and frequent software adaptations to remain smooth. If neglected, taking off from a dead stop will feel extremely jerky and the clutch pack will eventually burn out.",
      "PCV Valve Failure: The crankcase ventilation valve on the M156 is prone to sticking open or degrading over time. This allows excessive oil vapor to be sucked directly into the intake manifold, leading to heavy oil burning and smoking from the exhaust. It is positioned at the back of the engine and requires significant effort to replace.",
      "Fuel Injector Sticking: The factory fuel injectors can become stuck in the open position, especially on older, higher-mileage examples. When this happens, raw fuel pours continuously into the cylinder, washing away the lubricating oil. This can lead to fatal cylinder wall scoring or outright hydro-locking the engine."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Hydro-locking: If the head bolts fail and coolant floods the cylinder, or if a fuel injector sticks open, the engine will attempt to compress the liquid. Because liquids are incompressible, this instantly bends the connecting rods or blows a hole through the side of the engine block. Preventative maintenance on the injectors and head bolts is the only way to avoid this financial ruin.",
      "Valvetrain Destruction: Neglecting the ticking noise of a bleeding lifter will lead to the camshaft lobes physically grinding themselves flat. The metal shavings from the camshafts then circulate through the engine oil, scoring the cylinder walls and destroying the oil pump. A complete camshaft and lifter replacement is a massively expensive, labor-intensive repair.",
      "AirMatic Suspension Collapse: The heavy AMG V8 places immense stress on the standard AirMatic air suspension struts and the pneumatic compressor. The front struts frequently develop leaks in the airbags, causing the car to sag directly onto the tires overnight. Replacing the struts with quality aftermarket units like Arnott is mandatory, as OEM replacements are prohibitively expensive.",
      "Power Steering Reservoir Overheating: The hydraulic power steering system boils its fluid during aggressive driving or track days. This causes the fluid to overflow from the reservoir, making a mess of the engine bay and eventually starving the steering pump. Upgrading the fluid and installing a larger power steering cooler is necessary for spirited driving.",
      "Engine Mount Crushing: The sheer size and torque output of the 6.2L V8 places immense, repetitive strain on the fluid-filled engine mounts. They collapse and leak their hydraulic fluid much faster than standard models, transferring harsh vibrations directly into the cabin. Replacing them requires lifting the engine slightly and is a recurring maintenance tax.",
      "MCT Transmission Rebuilds: The sophisticated 7-speed MCT transmission is brilliant when functioning, but the internal wet clutch pack wears out just like a manual transmission clutch. If the vehicle has been subjected to frequent launch control starts, the friction plates will completely disintegrate. Replacing the wet clutch assembly is an intensely labor-heavy and costly procedure."
    ],
