    "yearRange": "2009-2013",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In the pre-facelift W212 E 250, the M271.860 is the stronger version of the 1.8-liter petrol four-cylinder and gives the car a more convincing and more effortless character than the smaller-output E 200. The extra boost and higher torque output make a noticeable difference in daily driving, especially in the mid-range where the W212 feels less heavy and more relaxed. It is still not a traditional large-capacity Mercedes engine, but it delivers enough low-end force to suit the chassis properly. In good condition, it feels like one of the more sensible pre-facelift petrol W212 options.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and Mercedes engine management. Compared with the lower-output M271 petrol versions, the E 250 tune gives the engine a stronger and more mature feel, but it also places greater importance on cooling stability, ignition quality, boost-system health, and clean oil service. This is still part of the M271 family, so its strengths and weaknesses remain familiar: good torque for the size, decent economy for the performance, but real sensitivity to deferred maintenance. When everything is working correctly, it feels smooth, quick enough, and well suited to the W212. When neglected, it can feel flat, noisy, or mechanically tired in a way that quickly removes the car's premium feel.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 340,000 km. The M271.860 can cover good mileage, but it only does so consistently when oil changes are frequent, timing-related symptoms are taken seriously, and ignition, boost, and breather issues are handled early. The problem with these engines is not usually that they fail without warning, but that they often give warnings that owners ignore because the car still runs reasonably well. Chain noise, roughness, oil seepage, weak cold starts, and small drivability faults can all build into a much more expensive ownership story over time. A strong car can be satisfying and durable, while a weak one often feels acceptable right up until repair costs begin to stack up.",
    "turboType": "Single turbocharged direct-injection petrol inline-four engine. The E 250 version is one of the better applications of the M271 in the W212 because the extra torque helps the car feel properly awake without needing to be driven hard all the time. It gives the large E-Class body enough flexibility to feel confident in traffic, calmer on the motorway, and less strained in overtaking than the lesser petrol versions. The character is still efficiency-focused rather than dramatic, but the added torque makes the whole drivetrain feel more complete. In a healthy example, it offers a good balance of usable performance and reasonable operating costs.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, turbo boost coordination, and variable camshaft timing logic. This gives the M271.860 a clean and responsive calibration when healthy, but it also means that several age-related faults can overlap without producing one dramatic failure pattern. Weak ignition components, timing wear, intake contamination, PCV issues, boost leaks, and oil-quality neglect can all slowly reduce how polished the car feels. Good diagnostics matter because these engines often degrade gradually rather than suddenly. The best examples feel smooth and strong enough; the tired ones feel less refined than their output figures suggest.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "220-235 hp. Mild software tuning is possible, but preserving timing health, cooling stability, and gearbox smoothness is usually the smarter ownership priority.",
