    "timingType": "Chain. The M278 utilizes timing chains that are known to rattle on cold starts due to failing secondary tensioners and a lack of check valves in the cylinder heads. Preventative upgrade of these tensioners and adding check valves is highly recommended to prevent chain stretch.",
    "injectionType": "Direct Injection. Unlike its predecessor, the M278 uses high-pressure direct injection. While this vastly improves fuel efficiency and power, it introduces the inevitable issue of carbon buildup on the intake valves, requiring professional cleaning at higher mileages.",
    "engineLifespan": "150 000 - 300 000+ km. The engine block is very strong, but the Silitec cylinder linings are highly sensitive to poor lubrication or excessive heat. A failing fuel injector or stretched oil intervals will quickly score the cylinder walls, destroying the engine.",
    "turboType": "Twin Turbochargers (BiTurbo). Nestled tightly alongside the block, they provide monstrous torque with minimal lag. The plastic coolant lines feeding the turbos are extremely fragile and will snap from heat cycling, dumping coolant rapidly.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MED17.7.3. A highly advanced ECU managing direct injection and boost pressure. The M278 is legendary in the tuning community, easily producing over 500 horsepower with a simple stage 1 software flash.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5 / Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "600+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods are strong but can bend if a tuner aggressively spikes the low-end torque. Smooth tuning is required to preserve the bottom end.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "13.0 - 16.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "8.0 - 10.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "10.0 - 12.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "16.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.5L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Absolute adherence to strict 5,000-mile (8,000 km) oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the delicate twin-turbos and the Silitec cylinder walls.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Timing Chain Tensioner Rattle: Early M278 engines suffer from premature timing chain tensioner wear and lack secondary check valves, allowing oil to drain back when parked. This causes a terrifying metallic rattle on cold starts as the loose chain slaps against the guides.",
      "Silitec Cylinder Wall Scoring: The aluminum cylinder walls are coated with a Silitec lining that is highly sensitive to poor lubrication or excessive heat. Fuel injector failure or stretched oil change intervals will cause the pistons to score the walls deeply.",
      "Camshaft Sensor Oil Leaking: Similar to the older engines, the camshaft position sensors are prone to internal seal failure. Oil pushes through the sensor body directly into the engine wiring harness.",
      "Turbocharger Coolant Line Brittleness: The twin turbochargers are supplied with coolant via incredibly thin plastic lines routed over the blistering hot exhaust manifolds. These lines bake in the heat, becoming so brittle they can snap simply from engine vibration.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes over the intake valves. Over time, thick, tar-like carbon sludge builds up on the valves, causing misfires and restricting airflow.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Wear: The twin camshaft-driven high-pressure fuel pumps work immensely hard and frequently fail internally. This leads to extended cranking times, severe power loss under heavy acceleration, and potential engine leaning.",
      "Ignition Coil Degradation: The extreme under-hood temperatures generated by the 'hot-V' adjacent turbos bake the ignition coils over time. As resistance increases, the coils fail to provide adequate spark under boost, causing aggressive misfires."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Engine Replacement via Cylinder Scoring: If the Silitec cylinder walls are scored, the engine will consume massive amounts of oil and lose compression. Repairing this requires a complete engine out teardown and aftermarket steel sleeving, which often totals the car financially.",
      "Wiring Harness and ECU Ruin: Oil leaking from the cam sensors will wick through the wiring harness straight into the Bosch ECU. This silent killer destroys the computer and requires thousands of euros to diagnose and replace.",
      "Catastrophic Coolant Loss: When the brittle plastic turbo coolant lines inevitably snap, they dump the engine's coolant supply almost instantly. If the driver does not pull over immediately, the aluminum engine block will warp from extreme overheating.",
      "Bent Connecting Rods from Tuning: The M278 is incredibly receptive to ECU tuning, easily making over 500 hp. However, aggressively tuned low-end torque will easily exceed the physical strength of the factory connecting rods, bending them and destroying the engine.",
      "AirMatic Collapse and Compressor Burnout: The heavy BiTurbo V8 accelerates the wear on the front AirMatic air struts. When a strut develops a micro-leak, the compressor runs continuously to compensate until it burns itself out, doubling the repair cost.",
      "Transmission Torque Overload: The massive 600 Nm of factory torque (and up to 800+ Nm tuned) places immense strain on the 7G-Tronic Plus transmission. Bypassing the strict 60,000 km fluid service interval guarantees premature torque converter and clutch pack failure."
    ],
