    "yearRange": "2013-2016",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In facelift W212 form, the M274.920 gives the E 250 a noticeably stronger and more complete character than the earlier 1.8-liter E 250 CGI. The extra torque and newer 2.0-liter architecture make the car feel calmer, fuller, and more naturally suited to the weight of the chassis. It is still a four-cylinder executive drivetrain, but in this output level it finally feels genuinely effortless in normal driving. When healthy, it is one of the more convincing non-six-cylinder petrol W212 setups.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and Mercedes / Bosch engine management. Compared with the older M271-powered E 250, this version feels more modern, more elastic, and more refined in the low and mid-range. At the same time, it belongs to the newer Mercedes turbo direct-injection era, so it depends heavily on oil quality, cooling-system stability, ignition quality, intake cleanliness, and precise diagnostics. When maintained properly, it feels smooth and quietly strong. When neglected, it can develop several overlapping drivability faults that make the car feel far less special than the output figure suggests.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 340,000 km. The M274.920 can cover strong mileage, but lifespan depends much more on maintenance evidence than on badge confidence. Regular oil changes, timely spark-plug service, stable cooling-system behavior, clean cold starts, and early diagnosis of timing, breather, boost, and carbon-related issues all matter. A weak example usually does not fail through one dramatic event, but through several medium-sized faults accumulating over time. A strong one can be very satisfying; a neglected one can become expensive in a surprisingly ordinary way.",
    "turboType": "Single turbocharged direct-injection petrol inline-four engine. In the E 250 tune, the M274 suits the W212 very well because the 350 Nm torque output gives the car proper low-end authority and relaxed overtaking ability. It does not imitate old-school big-engine Mercedes character, but it feels mature, capable, and genuinely premium in everyday use. The real strength of the setup is not drama, but its calm mid-range and easy long-distance usability. In healthy condition, it is one of the better-balanced petrol facelift W212 drivetrains.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz / Bosch engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, turbo boost coordination, and variable camshaft adjustment logic. This gives the E 250 a clean and polished factory calibration, but also means that ignition faults, boost leaks, breather issues, thermostat problems, and timing-related wear can overlap without creating one simple failure pattern. Because of that, proper diagnostics matter a lot more than guesswork. Many weak M274 cars are not catastrophically broken, just badly maintained and poorly interpreted. The best examples feel smooth, strong, and very cohesive.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "225-240 hp. Mild software optimization is possible, but preserving factory smoothness, cooling stability, and long-term engine health is usually the smarter ownership priority.",
