    "compression": "10.7:1",
    "yearRange": "2014-2016",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven V6 timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In this market-specific facelift W212 E 300 form, the M276.820 is best understood as a software-limited version of the 3.0-liter BiTurbo V6 rather than a fundamentally different mechanical package. That gives the car a smoother, fuller, and more effortless character than the four-cylinder petrol models, even in detuned form. It is not a loud or aggressive drivetrain, but it gives the W212 the kind of easy torque delivery and low-stress cruising feel that suits the platform very well. In healthy condition, it feels understated, refined, and properly executive.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with twin turbocharging, intercooling, variable cam timing, adaptive ignition control, and Mercedes-Benz engine management. Compared with the four-cylinder facelift engines, this setup feels more relaxed and less strained under load, but it also adds heat, packaging complexity, and more expensive component access. It is generally a more premium-feeling powertrain, yet it still depends heavily on oil quality, cooling-system health, intake cleanliness, and correct diagnostics. When maintained properly, it feels smooth, elastic, and very easygoing. When neglected, it becomes expensive in a broader and more premium-class way.",
    "engineLifespan": "250,000 to 420,000 km. The M276 biturbo V6 can cover strong mileage when it gets short oil intervals, good warm-up habits, proper cooling-system care, and timely attention to leaks, ignition wear, and boost-related issues. The major risk is not that it is inherently weak, but that owners often assume a refined V6 is healthy just because it still feels smooth. Small faults such as thermostat drift, charge-air leaks, breather issues, carbon buildup, or oil seepage can accumulate quietly over time. A well-kept example can feel excellent deep into high mileage; a neglected one can become a layered and costly project.",
    "turboType": "Twin-turbocharged direct-injection V6 petrol engine. Even in this detuned E 300 form, the engine gives the facelift W212 a far more natural and effortless character than the smaller petrol options, especially in motorway use and part-throttle acceleration. The appeal is not peak drama but calm strength, smoothness, and reserve torque. It feels like a drivetrain that is working well within itself rather than one that needs to be pushed. That is exactly why it suits the E-Class so well.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz / Bosch engine management with direct-injection control, twin-turbo boost coordination, knock regulation, adaptive fueling, and variable cam timing logic. This makes the engine very polished when healthy, but it also means that boost leaks, ignition weakness, cooling issues, intake contamination, and sensor-related faults can overlap without creating one obvious failure pattern. Proper diagnosis matters more than parts swapping. Many weak examples are not ruined, only badly interpreted. The best cars feel almost deceptively effortless.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "300-340 hp. Mild software optimization is usually possible because this version is already a reduced-output calibration, but preserving cooling stability, ignition quality, and gearbox behavior is the smarter priority on a road car.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "10.8-12.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "6.1-7.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "8.0-9.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "11.5-15.0 L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "Approx. 7.0L 5W-40 or 0W-40 fully synthetic oil meeting suitable Mercedes-Benz petrol-engine approvals.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Thermostat and cooling-system ageing can cause unstable warm-up behavior, weak temperature control, or general loss of drivetrain refinement.",
      "Oil leaks from upper-engine sealing points, breather-related areas, turbo oil-feed or return zones, and surrounding components can appear with age.",
      "PCV / crankcase ventilation faults can lead to rough idle, oil-consumption changes, mixture instability, and increased contamination in the intake path.",
      "Carbon buildup on the intake side is a normal direct-injection ageing issue and can gradually affect cold-start quality, response, and smoothness.",
      "Ignition-coil and spark-plug wear can produce hesitation, misfires, reduced load tolerance, and a general loss of the engine's polished feel.",
      "Charge-air leaks, boost-control irregularities, diverter-related behavior, or ageing intercooler plumbing can reduce response and mid-range consistency.",
      "Turbocharger heat management and surrounding hoses or seals deserve attention because age and heat cycles matter in a tightly packaged V6 twin-turbo engine.",
      "7G-TRONIC PLUS issues such as harsh shifts, delayed engagement, connector-area seepage, or conductor-plate-related behavior remain relevant on ageing examples."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "The biggest vulnerability is owner complacency: refined V6 engines can hide moderate faults better than rougher four-cylinders.",
      "Long oil intervals are especially harmful because they increase risk for timing wear, turbo-related deterioration, and internal deposit formation.",
      "Short-trip use accelerates carbon buildup, incomplete warm-up wear, and gradual decline in smoothness.",
      "Cooling-system neglect is more expensive here than on simpler engines because the drivetrain depends on stable thermal control.",
      "Cheap ignition parts, low-quality sensors, or poor leak repairs can quickly ruin the calm factory behavior of the engine.",
      "Boost, ignition, and breather-related problems can overlap and imitate one another, so weak diagnostics waste money fast.",
      "A short test drive often flatters the car, because a slightly tired M276 can still feel subjectively 'good enough' at first impression."
    ],
