    "timingType": "Double-roller chain. The M276 engine utilizes a robust timing chain system, but these early models lack secondary tensioner check valves. This allows oil to drain back, causing a harsh metallic rattle on cold starts. Upgrading the tensioners and adding the check valves is a mandatory preventative service.",
    "injectionType": "Direct Injection (CGI) with Piezo injectors. Extremely efficient high-pressure system operating at up to 200 bar. While it provides excellent fuel economy and power, it inherently introduces the risk of severe carbon buildup on the intake valves over time.",
    "engineLifespan": "150 000 - 300 000+ km. The 3.5L BiTurbo V6 block is mechanically exceptionally reliable. The true lifespan of the vehicle is largely dictated by strict adherence to oil change intervals, preventing cylinder scoring, and maintaining the twin-turbo cooling system.",
    "turboType": "Twin Turbochargers (BiTurbo). Nestled tightly alongside the block, they provide monstrous torque with minimal lag. This is the rarer 3.5L BiTurbo setup. The plastic coolant lines feeding the turbos are extremely fragile and will snap from heat cycling, dumping coolant rapidly.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MED17.7.3. A highly advanced ECU managing direct injection and boost pressure. The M276 is legendary in the tuning community, easily producing over 420 horsepower with a simple stage 1 software flash.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "500+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and crankshaft are heavily over-engineered. The block itself handles increased boost effortlessly, limited only by the thermal capacity of the factory turbos.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "10.5 - 12.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "7.5 - 9.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "8.5 - 10.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "13.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the delicate twin-turbos and the highly stressed timing chain tensioners.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Timing Chain Tensioner Rattle: The M276 engine lacks secondary chain tensioner check valves from the factory, allowing oil to drain back when parked. This causes a terrifying metallic rattle on cold starts as the loose chain slaps the guides. Installing the updated tensioners and check valves is critical to prevent chain stretch.",
      "Camshaft Sensor Oil Leaking: The camshaft position sensors are prone to internal seal failure over time. Oil pushes through the sensor body directly into the engine wiring harness. If ignored, the oil travels straight to the ECU.",
      "Turbocharger Coolant Line Brittleness: The twin turbochargers are supplied with coolant via incredibly thin plastic lines routed over the blistering hot exhaust manifolds. These lines bake in the heat, becoming so brittle they can snap simply from engine vibration.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes over the intake valves. Over time, thick, tar-like carbon sludge builds up on the valves, causing misfires and restricting airflow. Professional walnut blasting is required at higher mileages.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Wear: The twin camshaft-driven high-pressure fuel pumps work immensely hard and frequently fail internally. This leads to extended cranking times, severe power loss under heavy acceleration, and potential engine leaning.",
      "Ignition Coil Degradation: The extreme under-hood temperatures generated by the adjacent turbos bake the ignition coils over time. As resistance increases, the coils fail to provide adequate spark under boost, causing aggressive misfires."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Jump: If the cold-start chain rattle is ignored for too long, the timing chain will physically stretch beyond the tensioner's limits. The chain will eventually jump teeth on the camshaft sprockets, causing the pistons to strike the open valves. This instantly destroys the engine and necessitates a complete replacement.",
      "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: If the leaking camshaft sensors are ignored, capillary action will draw the oil through the wiring harness directly into the main engine control unit. Once the ECU is flooded with oil, it shorts out and requires complete, highly expensive replacement.",
      "Catastrophic Coolant Loss: When the brittle plastic turbo coolant lines inevitably snap, they dump the engine's coolant supply almost instantly. If the driver does not pull over immediately, the aluminum engine block will warp from extreme overheating. Billet replacement lines are highly recommended.",
      "Fatal Cylinder Wall Scoring: The M276 utilizes a Nanoslide aluminum block coating that is highly susceptible to scoring if a fuel injector fails and washes the lubricating oil away. This results in severe oil consumption, aggressive misfires, and a heavy knocking sound from the bottom end.",
      "Transmission Torque Overload: The massive 480 Nm of factory torque (and up to 600+ Nm tuned) places immense strain on the 7G-Tronic Plus transmission. Bypassing the strict 60,000 km fluid service interval guarantees premature torque converter and clutch pack failure.",
      "Intercooler Pump Heat Soak: The air-to-water intercooler system relies on a small electric pump to circulate coolant. This factory pump is frequently overwhelmed during spirited driving, causing intake air temperatures to skyrocket. The ECU will immediately pull engine timing, leaving the car feeling severely underpowered."
    ],
