    "yearRange": "2009-2011",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven naturally aspirated V6 timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In the pre-facelift W212 E 300, the M272 3.0-liter V6 gives the car a more traditional Mercedes character than the smaller turbocharged four-cylinders. The engine delivers its power in a smooth, linear, and mechanically relaxed way, which suits the weight and refinement goals of the E-Class very well. It does not feel especially aggressive, but it feels expensive in the right way because it works without obvious strain. In healthy condition, it is one of the most naturally balanced petrol options in the early W212 range.",
    "injectionType": "Naturally aspirated port-injected petrol V6 with variable cam timing and conventional Mercedes engine management. Compared with later direct-injection turbo engines, this setup is mechanically simpler in some areas and often easier to live with when properly maintained. It lacks the low-rpm punch of the later turbo units, but it makes up for that with smoothness, predictability, and a more classic premium-engine feel. The overall ownership story is less about tuning potential and more about preserving refinement, sealing integrity, cooling health, and intake-system condition.",
    "engineLifespan": "280,000 to 450,000 km. A good M272 E 300 can cover very strong mileage when oil changes are regular, cooling-system health is maintained, and age-related leaks or intake faults are fixed early. These engines usually do not fail because of one small neglected item alone, but because several medium-size issues are allowed to accumulate over time. A strong example can remain smooth and satisfying for many years, while a neglected one becomes expensive through oil leaks, intake problems, cooling wear, ignition faults, and general ageing. Service history matters more than mileage alone.",
    "turboType": "Naturally aspirated V6 petrol engine. This is not a turbocharged setup, so the driving experience is smoother and more progressive rather than boost-heavy or torque-spiky. In the W212, that gives the E 300 a calm and mature character, with decent response and a more traditional executive-car feel. It rewards drivers who value refinement and predictable power delivery more than headline numbers.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz engine management with sequential injection, adaptive fueling, knock control, and variable camshaft adjustment logic. The calibration is usually smooth and unobtrusive when the engine is healthy, but ageing sensors, intake faults, ignition issues, and vacuum-related problems can gradually reduce that polished feel. Diagnosis is still important, because a tired naturally aspirated V6 can mask medium-size issues better than a rougher four-cylinder. The best examples feel seamless and composed, while poor ones feel older than they should.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
