    "yearRange": "2009-2011",
    "timingType": "Double-roller chain. By the time the W212 was introduced, Mercedes had completely resolved the disastrous M272 balance shaft soft-metal sprocket issue. The timing chain and balance shaft in this generation are highly robust and generally last the lifetime of the engine.",
    "injectionType": "Multi-Point Sequential Fuel Injection (ME-SFI). Because the bulky early 4MATIC transfer case didn't fit alongside the new CGI direct injection system, Mercedes utilized the older, port-injected M272 for early AWD models. This is a MASSIVE blessing for reliability, as it completely avoids HPFP failures and intake valve carbon buildup.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The core aluminum block is virtually indestructible. Free from the complexities of direct injection, this specific 4MATIC variant is arguably one of the most reliable gasoline engines ever fitted to the W212 chassis. The true lifespan is dictated by the maintenance of the 4MATIC transfer case.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. The 3.5L V6 provides a very smooth, linear power delivery. It lacks the sharp punch of the CGI model but makes up for it with bulletproof reliability and endless smooth cruising.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch ME 9.7. A highly advanced ECU that controls the continuous variable valve timing and the variable intake manifold. It provides extensive diagnostic capabilities.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "350+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is very strong. However, naturally aspirated tuning yields very modest gains, usually capped around 290 hp with software and exhaust modifications.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "13.0 - 15.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "8.5 - 10.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "10.0 - 11.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "16.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.0L 5W-40 or 0W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Strict adherence to 10,000 km oil change intervals is highly recommended to protect the variable valve timing phasers.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Intake Manifold Tumble Flap Breakage: The plastic levers that actuate the variable intake runners become brittle from engine heat and snap off completely. This causes a poor, erratic idle and drastically reduces low-end torque. Mercedes only sells the entire manifold.",
      "Camshaft Adjuster Solenoid Leaks: The seals on the four camshaft magnets at the front of the engine frequently leak oil over time. This oil can wick directly into the engine wiring harness, traveling all the way to the main ECU.",
      "4MATIC Transfer Case Wear: The transfer case integrated into the 7G-Tronic transmission requires specific fluid changes. If neglected, the internal bearings will whine and the clutches will shudder during tight turns.",
      "Front CV Axle Boot Tearing: The 4MATIC system utilizes front CV axles that are prone to tearing their rubber boots. Once torn, grease escapes and grit enters, quickly destroying the expensive CV joints.",
      "Thermostat Failure: The electronically mapped thermostat frequently fails in the open position, preventing the engine from reaching its optimal 90°C operating temperature. This leads to poor fuel economy and accelerated engine wear.",
      "Engine Mount Collapse: The weight of the V6 engine and the 4MATIC system crushes the fluid-filled engine mounts surprisingly fast. When they fail, harsh vibrations are transferred directly into the cabin."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Intake Manifold Replacement Cost: Because Mercedes does not sell the external plastic actuator lever separately, a broken flap linkage usually dictates replacing the entire intake manifold assembly. This turns a cheap plastic failure into a massive parts and labor bill.",
      "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: If the leaking camshaft magnets are ignored, capillary action will draw the oil through the wiring harness directly into the main engine control unit. Once the ECU is flooded with oil, it shorts out and requires complete replacement.",
      "Transfer Case Destruction: Ignoring the 4MATIC fluid service intervals will cause the internal bearings in the transfer case to fail. Rebuilding or replacing the transfer case is a major, highly expensive transmission-out repair.",
      "Transmission Conductor Plate Failure: The internal speed sensors on the 722.9 transmission conductor plate are prone to failing. This forces the transmission into Limp Mode, locking it in second gear until the car is restarted.",
      "Rear Subframe Corrosion: Early W212 models suffer from a notorious factory defect where the rear subframe rusts violently from the inside out. In severe cases, the rear suspension control arms can literally tear away from the chassis while driving."
    ],
