    "timingType": "Double-roller chain. The M276 engine utilizes a robust timing chain system, but early models lack secondary tensioner check valves. This allows oil to drain back, causing a harsh metallic rattle on cold starts. Upgrading the tensioners and adding the check valves is a mandatory preventative service.",
    "injectionType": "Direct Injection (CGI) with Piezo injectors. Extremely efficient high-pressure system operating at up to 200 bar. While it provides excellent fuel economy and crisp throttle response, it inherently introduces the risk of severe carbon buildup on the intake valves over time.",
    "engineLifespan": "250 000 - 400 000+ km. The 3.5L naturally aspirated V6 block is mechanically exceptionally reliable and unstressed. The true lifespan of the vehicle is largely dictated by strict adherence to oil change intervals and preventative maintenance on the timing chain tensioners.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. The 3.5-liter V6 breathes entirely on its own, providing instant, lag-free throttle response. It delivers a remarkably smooth, linear power curve that perfectly suits a luxury executive sedan.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MED17.7.3. A highly advanced ECU managing the direct injection and variable valve timing. It provides incredible fuel efficiency via lean-burn mapping during highway cruising.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5 / Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "400+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and crankshaft are heavily over-engineered for the naturally aspirated power output. The block itself can handle significant stress, though naturally aspirated tuning yields modest gains.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "11.0 - 13.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "7.5 - 9.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "8.5 - 10.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "14.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the delicate high-pressure fuel pump and timing chain tensioners.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Timing Chain Tensioner Rattle: The M276 engine lacks secondary chain tensioner check valves from the factory, allowing oil to drain back when parked. This causes a terrifying metallic rattle on cold starts as the loose chain slaps the guides. Installing the updated tensioners and check valves is critical to prevent chain stretch.",
      "Camshaft Sensor Oil Leaking: The camshaft position sensors are prone to internal seal failure over time. Oil pushes through the sensor body directly into the engine wiring harness. If ignored, the oil travels straight to the ECU.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes over the intake valves. Over time, thick, tar-like carbon sludge builds up on the valves, causing misfires and restricting airflow. Professional walnut blasting is required at higher mileages.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Wear: The engine relies on a camshaft-driven high-pressure fuel pump to supply the direct injectors with immense pressure. This pump frequently wears out internally, leading to long cranking times, severe power loss, and Limp Mode. A failing HPFP can also leak raw fuel into the engine oil.",
      "PCV Valve / Oil Separator Failure: The crankcase ventilation valve is highly prone to failing and passing excessive oil vapor. This coats the entire intake tract in oil, massively exacerbating the direct injection carbon buildup problem. Replacing it preventatively is crucial to protect the engine's breathing efficiency.",
      "Thermostat Failure: The electronically mapped thermostat frequently fails in the open position, preventing the engine from reaching its optimal operating temperature. This leads to poor fuel economy, increased emissions, and a constant check engine light. It must be replaced as an entire assembly."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Jump: If the cold-start chain rattle is ignored for too long, the timing chain will physically stretch beyond the tensioner's limits. The chain will eventually jump teeth on the camshaft sprockets, causing the pistons to strike the open valves. This instantly destroys the engine and necessitates a complete replacement.",
      "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: If the leaking camshaft sensors are ignored, capillary action will draw the oil through the wiring harness directly into the main engine control unit. Once the ECU is flooded with oil, it shorts out and requires complete, highly expensive replacement. Installing pigtail harnesses prevents this completely.",
      "Fatal Cylinder Wall Scoring: The M276 utilizes a Nanoslide aluminum block coating that is highly susceptible to scoring if a fuel injector fails and washes the lubricating oil away. This results in severe oil consumption, aggressive misfires, and a heavy knocking sound from the bottom end. Repairing this requires pulling the engine and installing aftermarket steel sleeves.",
      "Transmission Torque Converter Glazing: The 7G-Tronic Plus automatic transmission requires absolute religious adherence to 60,000 km fluid change intervals. If neglected, the torque converter clutch will glaze and begin to shudder violently under moderate acceleration. Rebuilding the transmission is a massive financial burden.",
      "Engine Mount Collapse: The sheer weight of the V6 engine crushes the fluid-filled engine mounts surprisingly fast. When they fail, harsh vibrations are transferred directly into the cabin, especially noticeable when idling in gear. Replacing them restores the classic Mercedes isolation.",
      "Rear Subframe Corrosion: Early W212 models suffer from a notorious factory defect where the rear subframe rusts violently from the inside out due to road salt. In severe cases, the rear suspension control arms can literally tear away from the chassis while driving. It requires a massive subframe replacement procedure."
    ],
