    "yearRange": "2011-2014",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven naturally aspirated V6 timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In this E 300 application, the M276.952 is best understood as a softer-calibrated version of the newer-generation 3.5-liter direct-injection V6 rather than as a fundamentally weak or compromised drivetrain. It gives the W212 a more modern, cleaner, and more responsive character than the older M272, while still keeping the smooth naturally aspirated V6 feel that suits the E-Class very well. Power delivery is linear, refined, and easy to modulate, with less mechanical heaviness than the previous 3.0-liter V6. In healthy condition, it feels like one of the most elegant petrol setups available in the mid-range W212 lineup.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with naturally aspirated induction, variable cam timing, adaptive ignition control, and Mercedes engine management. Compared with the earlier port-injected V6, this engine is technically more advanced, more efficient, and more responsive, but it also depends more heavily on clean oil, stable cooling performance, healthy ignition parts, and accurate diagnostics. It does not have turbocharger complexity, which helps long-term ownership in some areas, yet the direct-injection system and newer emissions-era hardware make it less old-school simple than the M272. When maintained properly, it feels smooth, precise, and very mature. When neglected, it can become expensive through overlapping medium-sized faults rather than one dramatic failure.",
    "engineLifespan": "260,000 to 420,000 km. The M276.952 can cover strong mileage when oil changes are frequent, warm-up habits are sensible, and cooling, ignition, and sealing issues are handled before they spread into larger problems. Mileage by itself is less important than evidence of careful ownership, because this engine often stays smooth even when it is beginning to develop leaks, intake deposits, or timing-related wear. A good one can remain refined and dependable for a very long time, while a neglected one becomes expensive through thermostat faults, oil leaks, misfires, carbon buildup, and age-related sensor or breather problems. Service history is more valuable than headline mileage.",
    "turboType": "Naturally aspirated V6 petrol engine. This is not a turbocharged setup, so the engine delivers its power progressively and without the sudden torque surge of the later small forced-induction petrol units. In the W212, that gives the E 300 a calm and premium character, with strong smoothness and natural throttle response rather than boost-heavy urgency. It rewards drivers who value mechanical refinement and predictable delivery over peak shove. In normal use, it feels balanced and expensive in the right way.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, and variable camshaft adjustment logic. The calibration is usually polished and unobtrusive when the engine is healthy, but ageing sensors, intake deposits, ignition weakness, vacuum-related faults, and timing deviations can gradually reduce that seamless factory feel. Proper diagnosis still matters because a tired naturally aspirated V6 can hide its problems better than a rougher four-cylinder. The best examples feel clean, quiet, and cohesive. Weak ones usually feel merely older rather than obviously broken, which is why neglected cars can fool buyers.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "280-305 hp. If this version is genuinely software-limited relative to higher-output M276 applications, useful gains are possible through calibration, but preserving reliability, smoothness, and thermal stability is still the smarter ownership priority.",
