    "yearRange": "2020-2023",
    "timingType": "Chain. The OM656 features a complex timing chain system located at the REAR of the engine block. While generally highly robust compared to older 4-cylinders, stretching can occur at high mileages if oil changes are neglected, requiring a massive engine-out repair.",
    "injectionType": "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI) operating at an immense 2500 bar. Utilizes highly precise Piezo injectors. This late facelift version features an incredibly strict software map and enhanced dual-dosing SCR hardware to comply with draconian Euro 6d-ISC-FCM emissions regulations.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The inline-6 aluminum block with steel pistons is exceptionally durable. The true lifespan of the vehicle is largely dictated by the maintenance of the highly complex, hypersensitive emissions systems (EGR, DPF, dual-SCR) and the rear timing chain.",
    "turboType": "Sequential Twin-Turbo (BiTurbo). Features a smaller high-pressure turbocharger for instant response and a larger low-pressure turbo for massive top-end power. The transition is managed electronically, providing seamless torque delivery without any noticeable lag.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MD1. An incredibly advanced engine control unit that manages the fuel injection, twin turbos, Camtronic system, and the ultra-strict Euro 6d emissions monitoring. It is heavily locked down but remains receptive to high-end ECU tuning.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6d-ISC-FCM",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "450+ hp. The internal forged rotating assembly is incredibly strong. The block can handle significant boost increases, with the primary limitation being the hypersensitive DPF/SCR monitoring systems and the 9G-Tronic transmission clutches.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "7.5 - 9.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "5.0 - 6.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "6.0 - 7.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "9.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.52). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the rear timing chain, the delicate twin-turbos, and prevent DPF clogging.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Rear Timing Chain Stretch: The OM656 retains the rear-mounted timing chain architecture. Prolonged oil change intervals can lead to chain elongation and tensioner wear. A metallic rattle on cold start is a critical warning sign that requires immediate attention.",
      "Camtronic Variable Valve Timing Faults: The advanced Camtronic system allows variable exhaust valve lift to heat up the SCR catalyst faster. The electronic actuators for this system can stick or fail due to intense heat cycles. This leads to erratic idling, reduced power, and severe emission control faults.",
      "EGR and Intake Manifold Soot Buildup: Modern emissions standards force the engine to ingest a significant amount of exhaust gases via high and low-pressure EGR systems. This soot mixes with oil vapor from the PCV to form a thick sludge inside the intake manifold. Over time, it restricts airflow and causes the engine to throw Limp Mode codes.",
      "Complex AdBlue (SCR) System Failures: The dual-dosing AdBlue system is highly effective but incredibly complex and prone to sensor failure. The NOx sensors frequently degrade from soot, and the AdBlue injector can crystalize and block. This triggers dashboard countdowns that will eventually prevent the car from starting.",
      "Tandem Oil/Vacuum Pump Leaks: The engine uses a combined oil and vacuum pump located at the rear of the block. The internal seals can fail, leading to oil leaking down the bellhousing, often misdiagnosed as a rear main seal leak. It requires significant labor to access and replace.",
      "Switchable Water Pump Issues: The OM656 uses a switchable water pump to bring the engine up to temperature rapidly. The internal shroud mechanism can jam over time due to coolant degradation. This leads to localized overheating or extremely slow warm-up times, triggering thermostat faults.",
      "Exhaust Gas Temperature (EGT) Sensor Failures: The DPF and SCR systems rely on incredibly accurate EGT sensors to trigger active regenerations. These sensors bake in the exhaust heat and fail randomly over time. A failed sensor throws the car into limp mode to protect the DPF from melting."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Engine-Out Timing Chain Repair: Because the timing chain is located at the absolute rear of the engine block against the firewall, access is practically impossible. Replacing a stretched chain requires dropping the entire engine and transmission assembly. This turns a standard wear item into a massive, financially crippling labor bill.",
      "AdBlue No-Start Countdown: If the ECU detects any fault in the incredibly sensitive SCR system, it initiates a hard countdown. Once the permitted number of miles or starts reaches zero, the ECU completely immobilizes the vehicle. You will be stranded until a dealer replaces the hardware and resets the encrypted software.",
      "DPF Blockage from Urban Commuting: The OM656 relies on extreme exhaust temperatures to actively burn off trapped soot in the DPF. If the car is used strictly for short city trips, these regenerations are constantly aborted. The filter will fatally clog, creating massive backpressure that can destroy the twin turbochargers.",
      "Camtronic Mechanical Seizing: If the Camtronic sliding cam pieces seize due to poor oil quality or actuator failure, the engine's breathing is severely compromised. Fixing the mechanical side of the Camtronic system requires removing the valve cover and dealing with the complex camshaft assembly. It is a highly specialized and expensive repair.",
      "Oil Dilution via Aborted Regenerations: During DPF regeneration, extra diesel is injected late in the combustion cycle. If the regeneration is aborted by shutting the car off, this raw diesel washes down the cylinder walls and dilutes the engine oil. This massively reduces oil viscosity, rapidly accelerating wear on the main bearings.",
      "Tandem Pump Labor Tax: An oil leak from the tandem oil/vacuum pump is common, but because it sits at the back of the engine, it is notoriously difficult to access. Mechanics often quote massive labor hours just to replace a simple internal O-ring. Ignoring the leak will coat the transmission bellhousing and undercarriage in oil."
    ],
