    "yearRange": "2016-2019",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In the pre-facelift W213 E 200, the M274.920 gives the car a more natural executive character than the smaller tax-oriented versions because the extra displacement and torque reserve suit the size of the chassis better. It is still not a large-capacity luxury engine in the old Mercedes sense, but it feels calm, modern, and properly matched to the platform in normal use. The power delivery is smooth, broad, and easy to access, which helps the car feel refined in traffic and reasonably relaxed on the motorway. In healthy condition, it is one of the more convincing four-cylinder petrol pairings for the early W213.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and Mercedes engine management. Compared with naturally aspirated V6 Mercedes engines, this setup feels more modern and more torque-efficient, but it also relies more heavily on clean oil, stable cooling, healthy ignition components, and careful diagnostics. It has enough low-end torque to move the car without strain in ordinary driving, yet it belongs to the newer direct-injection turbo era where several medium-size faults can overlap. When maintained properly, it feels smooth and polished. When neglected, it becomes ordinary and expensive in a very contemporary way.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 340,000 km. The M274.920 can cover strong mileage when oil changes are conservative, cooling-system health is monitored, and ignition, breather, carbon, and timing-related symptoms are not ignored. These engines often continue to feel acceptable even after moderate deterioration has started, which is why service history matters more than first impressions. A strong example can remain refined and economical for a long time, while a neglected one tends to accumulate thermostat problems, oil leaks, carbon buildup, timing wear, misfires, and small boost-related issues. Long-term ownership depends much more on maintenance quality than on the Mercedes badge alone.",
    "turboType": "Single turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine. In the standard E 200 tune, the M274.920 gives the W213 enough torque to feel properly usable and composed without pretending to be a performance model. Its strongest quality is not excitement but balanced everyday drivability, especially in light-throttle urban use and steady motorway cruising. The engine suits drivers who want a modern petrol E-Class with reasonable smoothness and decent flexibility. It feels most convincing when it is healthy, quiet, and allowed to use its mid-range rather than being chased for top-end drama.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, turbo boost coordination, and variable camshaft timing logic. This produces a smooth factory calibration when everything is right, but it also means that ignition weakness, cooling drift, timing wear, PCV faults, carbon buildup, and boost irregularities can overlap and reduce refinement without one obvious dramatic symptom. Accurate diagnosis matters because weak examples often feel merely flatter, rougher, or less premium rather than obviously broken. On this engine, small faults add up quickly. The best cars feel cohesive and quietly strong, while poor ones feel more tired than their mileage suggests.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "205-225 hp. Mild gains are possible through careful software optimization, but preserving factory smoothness, timing health, cooling stability, and gearbox quality is usually the smarter ownership priority.",
