    "yearRange": "2016-2019",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In this fleet- and taxi-oriented pre-facelift W213 E 200 form, the M274.920 should be understood as a softer software-calibrated version of the same 2.0-liter turbo petrol hardware rather than a fundamentally different engine. That changes the character of the car more than the badge suggests, because the lower output reduces the sense of reserve torque and makes the drivetrain feel more workmanlike under load. It still retains the core refinement traits of the standard M274.920 package, but with less effortless performance. In healthy condition, it works as a pragmatic professional-use calibration rather than an enthusiast-oriented one.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and Mercedes engine management. Compared with the regular 184 hp E 200 version, this detuned fleet calibration gives up some torque and response in exchange for market positioning, insurance logic, or operating-policy goals on selected European markets. Mechanically it still carries the same strengths and weaknesses of the M274 family, which means maintenance quality matters more than the calmer output figure might suggest. It can still feel smooth and civilized, but it has less surplus performance to hide small faults. This makes condition and correct servicing even more important than on the standard tune.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 340,000 km. The fleet-calibrated M274.920 can cover strong mileage when oil changes are conservative, cooling-system health is monitored, and ignition, breather, carbon, and timing-related symptoms are addressed early. The lower output does not automatically make it worry-free, because the same common ageing patterns still apply. A good example can remain smooth and economically logical for a long time, while a neglected one will still accumulate thermostat faults, oil leaks, carbon buildup, timing wear, misfires, and charge-air-system issues. Proper service history matters more than the detuned power figure.",
    "turboType": "Single turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine. In this reduced-output tune, the engine is calibrated for calm business use rather than strong mid-range urgency, so the W213 feels more subdued and more obviously efficiency-oriented. It remains smoother than many smaller fleet engines in lower market segments, but it also feels more sensitive to load and passenger weight than the standard 184 hp version. The strongest point is civilized light-throttle operation rather than effortless overtaking ability. It works best when the car is used in the way the calibration suggests: measured, regular, and maintenance-conscious operation.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, turbo boost coordination, and variable camshaft timing logic. In this software-limited version, the underlying control structure remains the same, so the same pattern applies: ignition weakness, cooling drift, timing wear, PCV faults, carbon buildup, and boost irregularities can overlap and quietly reduce refinement. Because the engine has less output reserve than the standard E 200, even moderate faults can feel more noticeable in everyday driving. Proper diagnosis still matters more than random parts replacement. The best cars feel calm and professional, while weaker ones feel dull and prematurely worn.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "184-205 hp. Because this version is software-limited, the most meaningful performance headroom usually comes from restoring or approximating the standard E 200 calibration rather than from aggressive tuning.",
