    "valves": 16,
    "compression": "15.5:1",
    "yearRange": "2018-2020",
    "timingType": "Chain. The OM654 utilizes a simplex timing chain located at the REAR of the engine block. While generally more robust than older 4-cylinders, stretching can occur at high mileages if oil changes are neglected, requiring an expensive engine-out or transmission-out repair. Listen closely for a metallic rattle on cold starts.",
    "injectionType": "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI) operating at an immense 2500 bar. Utilizes highly precise Piezo injectors. This engine features incredibly advanced thermal management and an incredibly compact, engine-mounted SCR (AdBlue) and DPF system to meet the strictest global emissions standards rapidly.",
    "engineLifespan": "250 000 - 450 000+ km. The all-aluminum block with Nanoslide cylinder coating is exceptionally durable. However, the true lifespan is heavily dictated by the rigorous maintenance of its highly condensed, heat-soaked emissions systems (DPF, dual-EGR, SCR) and the rear timing chain.",
    "turboType": "Sequential Twin-Turbo (BiTurbo). Features a smaller high-pressure turbocharger for instant response and a larger low-pressure turbo for top-end power. The transition provides excellent torque, masking the fact that it is only a 2.0-liter 4-cylinder in a heavy executive sedan.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MD1. An incredibly advanced engine control unit that manages the fuel injection, twin turbos, and the ultra-strict emissions monitoring. It is heavily locked down but remains receptive to high-end ECU tuning.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6d-TEMP",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "320+ hp. The internal forged rotating assembly is incredibly strong. The block can handle significant boost increases, with the primary limitation being the hypersensitive DPF/SCR monitoring systems and the turbochargers' thermal capacity.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "6.5 - 8.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "4.8 - 5.8 L/100km",
      "mixed": "5.5 - 6.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "8.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.52). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the rear timing chain, the delicate twin-turbos, and prevent DPF clogging.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Rear Timing Chain Stretch: The OM654 locates its timing chain at the rear of the block, making access incredibly difficult. Due to its single-row design, it stretches over high mileages if oil changes are stretched. You will hear a distinct, sharp metallic rattle on cold starts when the tensioner fails.",
      "Camshaft Rocker Arm Wear (OM654 Specific): A critical and highly specific flaw in the OM654 is the rapid wear of the camshaft rocker arms (roller cam followers). If oil changes are neglected, these rockers wear down, causing severe camshaft damage and a loud, rhythmic ticking noise from the top of the engine.",
      "Engine-Mounted DPF/SCR Thermal Stress: To heat up the emissions system quickly, the DPF and SCR catalyst are bolted directly to the engine block. This subjects the surrounding sensors, wiring harnesses, and plastic components to extreme, constant heat soak, accelerating their failure.",
      "Complex AdBlue (SCR) System Failures: The dual-dosing AdBlue system is incredibly complex and prone to sensor failure. The NOx sensors frequently degrade from heat and soot, and the AdBlue injector can crystalize. This triggers dashboard countdowns that will eventually prevent the car from starting.",
      "Switchable Water Pump Issues: The OM654 uses a switchable water pump to bring the engine up to temperature rapidly. The internal shroud mechanism can jam over time due to coolant degradation. This leads to localized overheating or extremely slow warm-up times.",
      "EGR and Intake Manifold Soot Buildup: Modern emissions standards force the engine to ingest a significant amount of exhaust gases via high and low-pressure EGR systems. This soot mixes with oil vapor from the PCV to form a thick sludge inside the intake manifold, restricting airflow."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Engine-Out Timing Chain Repair: Because the timing chain is positioned practically against the firewall, what is a standard maintenance task becomes a massive financial burden. Mechanics often must drop the transmission or the entire engine just to replace the chain. Neglecting the chain stretch will result in pistons destroying the valves.",
      "Catastrophic Valvetrain Failure: If the rocker arm wear is ignored, the camshaft lobes will literally grind themselves flat against the seized roller followers. The metal shavings will circulate through the engine oil, scoring the cylinder walls and destroying the oil pump. A complete camshaft and lifter replacement is a massively expensive, labor-intensive repair.",
      "AdBlue No-Start Countdown: If the ECU detects any fault in the incredibly sensitive SCR system, it initiates a hard countdown. Once the permitted number of miles or starts reaches zero, the ECU completely immobilizes the vehicle. You will be stranded until a dealer replaces the hardware and resets the encrypted software.",
      "DPF Blockage from Urban Commuting: The OM654 relies on extreme exhaust temperatures to actively burn off trapped soot in the DPF. If the car is used strictly for short city trips, these regenerations are constantly aborted. The filter will fatally clog, creating massive backpressure that can destroy the twin turbochargers.",
      "Oil Dilution via Aborted Regenerations: During DPF regeneration, extra diesel is injected late in the combustion cycle. If the regeneration is aborted by shutting the car off, this raw diesel washes down the cylinder walls and dilutes the engine oil. This massively reduces oil viscosity, rapidly accelerating wear on the main bearings and the fragile rocker arms.",
      "Tandem Oil/Vacuum Pump Leaks: The engine uses a combined oil and vacuum pump located at the rear of the block. The internal seals can fail, leading to oil leaking down the bellhousing, often misdiagnosed as a rear main seal leak. It requires significant labor to access and replace."
    ],
