    "yearRange": "2019-2023",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In facelift W213 E 200 form, the M264.920 is the more modern replacement for the earlier M274 generation and gives the car a cleaner, quicker, and more technically polished character. The extra smoothness comes not only from the combustion engine itself, but also from the 48V EQ Boost system that helps fill small response gaps and improves driveline refinement at low load. It still does not deliver classic large-capacity Mercedes richness, yet it feels much more complete and modern than a tax-driven entry engine. In healthy condition, it is one of the more balanced four-cylinder petrol drivetrains in the facelift W213 range.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, twin-scroll boost behavior, adaptive ignition control, and Mercedes engine management integrated with a 48V mild-hybrid support architecture. Compared with the earlier M274-based E 200, this setup feels cleaner in throttle pickup, more progressive in low-speed operation, and more modern in general calibration quality. The EQ Boost assistance does not transform it into a performance engine, but it helps the car feel less strained in everyday use. It is still a compact turbocharged four-cylinder in a large executive sedan, so good cooling, clean oil, healthy ignition, and accurate diagnostics remain essential. When maintained properly, it feels polished and cohesive rather than dramatic.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 360,000 km. The M264.920 can cover strong mileage when oil changes are conservative, cooling-system behavior is monitored, and timing, ignition, breather, and boost-related faults are addressed early. The engine usually stays smooth enough to hide moderate deterioration, which means short test drives often flatter weak examples. A strong one can remain refined and efficient for a long time, while a neglected one tends to accumulate thermostat faults, coolant-pump issues, carbon buildup, oil seepage, timing-control irregularities, and charge-air-system problems. Long-term durability depends far more on service discipline than on the mild-hybrid badge.",
    "turboType": "Single twin-scroll turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine. In the facelift E 200, the turbocharger is well matched to the 2.0-liter displacement and gives the W213 a more natural reserve of torque than the smaller entry engines. The extra support from the EQ Boost system helps the engine feel smoother and less hesitant in transient throttle situations. It is not an especially emotional powertrain, but it is calm, effective, and quite premium when everything is working properly. Its strongest quality is balanced everyday drivability rather than outright excitement.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, turbo boost coordination, variable camshaft timing logic, and 48V mild-hybrid drivetrain integration. This creates a smoother and more modern factory calibration than the older M274 family, but it also means ignition weakness, cooling drift, timing deviations, sensor issues, PCV faults, and boost irregularities can overlap without one single dramatic failure pattern. Accurate diagnosis matters because the engine often feels only slightly duller or rougher rather than obviously broken. On a premium four-cylinder with hybrid assistance, small defects have an outsized effect on perceived quality. The best examples feel quietly strong and very cohesive.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6d",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "225-245 hp. Mild gains are usually possible through careful software optimization, but preserving factory smoothness, thermal stability, timing health, and gearbox quality is generally the smarter ownership priority.",
