    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In this facelift fleet- or taxi-oriented E 200 form, the M264.920 should be understood as a softer calibration of the same newer-generation 2.0-liter mild-hybrid turbo petrol package rather than a fundamentally different engine. The lower output changes the feel of the car more than the badge suggests because it reduces reserve torque and makes the drivetrain more obviously duty-focused under passenger or load pressure. It still keeps the underlying smoothness and modernity of the M264 family, but with a more workmanlike character than the full 197 hp EQ Boost version. In healthy condition, it works as a pragmatic professional-use configuration rather than an enthusiast-oriented one.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and mild-hybrid drivetrain support through the newer M264 control architecture. Compared with the full-output E 200 EQ Boost, this softer configuration trades away some response and effortless feel in exchange for operating-policy or market-positioning goals. Mechanically it still carries the same strengths and weaknesses of the M264 family, which means service discipline matters more than the calmer output figure might suggest. It can still feel smooth and modern, but it has less spare performance to hide moderate faults. That makes condition especially important in professional-use scenarios.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 360,000 km. The detuned M264.920 can cover strong mileage when oil changes are conservative, cooling-system behavior is monitored, and timing, ignition, breather, and boost-related faults are not ignored. The lower output does not make it immune to the same age-related patterns seen elsewhere in the M264 family. A good example can remain smooth and economically logical for years, while a neglected one will still accumulate thermostat faults, coolant-pump issues, carbon buildup, oil seepage, timing-control irregularities, and charge-air-system problems. Proper service history matters far more than the softer calibration.",
    "turboType": "Single twin-scroll turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine. In this reduced-output mild-hybrid tune, the drivetrain is calibrated for calm business use rather than strong mid-range urgency, so the W213 feels more subdued and more obviously efficiency-oriented. It remains smoother and more polished than many lower-segment fleet engines, but it gives away some of the easy flexibility that makes the 197 hp version more naturally suited to the chassis. The strongest point is civilized light-throttle operation rather than generous overtaking reserve. It works best when maintained carefully and used in the measured way the calibration suggests.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, turbo boost coordination, variable camshaft timing logic, and 48V mild-hybrid system integration. In this software-limited version, the underlying control structure remains the same, so ignition weakness, cooling drift, timing deviations, sensor issues, PCV faults, and boost irregularities can still overlap and quietly reduce refinement. Because the engine has less output reserve than the full 197 hp tune, even moderate faults feel more noticeable in everyday driving. Proper diagnosis matters more than random parts replacement. The best cars feel calm and professional, while weaker ones feel dull and prematurely worn.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6d",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "210-230 hp. Because this version is software-limited, the most meaningful headroom usually comes from restoring or approximating the fuller factory E 200 calibration rather than from aggressive tuning.",
