    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In the pre-facelift W213 E 250, the M274.920 represents the stronger factory-tuned middle step between the calmer E 200 and the more assertive E 300. It uses the same basic 2.0-liter architecture, but the extra output gives the W213 a noticeably more natural reserve of torque and a more confident executive character. It still belongs to the modern small-capacity turbo era rather than the old-school big-engine Mercedes tradition, yet it feels convincingly matched to the chassis in daily use. In healthy condition, it is one of the more satisfying non-AMG petrol W213 drivetrains from the early years.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and Mercedes engine management. The M274 DE20 LA layout in this 211 hp tune gives the W213 a more relaxed and flexible feel than the standard E 200 calibration, especially under motorway load and overtaking demand. It remains a modern direct-injection turbo engine, so refinement depends heavily on clean oil, stable cooling, healthy ignition components, and good diagnostic discipline. When well maintained, it feels strong, smooth, and cohesive. When neglected, it becomes expensive through several overlapping medium-size faults rather than one dramatic collapse.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 340,000 km. The M274.920 in E 250 tune can cover strong mileage when oil changes are conservative, cooling-system health is monitored, and ignition, breather, carbon, and timing-related symptoms are addressed early. These engines often continue to feel acceptable even after deterioration has started, which is why service history matters more than first impressions. A strong example can remain refined and muscular for many years, while a neglected one tends to accumulate thermostat problems, oil leaks, carbon buildup, timing wear, misfires, and small boost-related issues. Long-term ownership depends much more on maintenance quality than on the higher output alone.",
    "turboType": "Single twin-scroll turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine. In E 250 form, the M274.920 gives the W213 enough low-end and mid-range torque to feel more complete than the entry-level petrol variants, without trying to imitate a six-cylinder car. Its strongest quality is not drama but easy, usable thrust and a quiet sense of reserve in normal driving. It suits drivers who want a proper petrol executive sedan feel without stepping up to the cost structure of larger engines. When healthy, it feels more naturally premium than the lower-tuned versions built from the same hardware.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, turbo boost coordination, and variable camshaft timing logic. This produces a smooth factory calibration when everything is healthy, but it also means ignition weakness, cooling drift, timing wear, PCV faults, carbon buildup, and boost irregularities can overlap and reduce refinement without one obvious dramatic symptom. Accurate diagnosis matters because weak examples often feel merely flatter, rougher, or less expensive than they should rather than clearly broken. On this engine, small faults add up quickly. The best cars feel cohesive and quietly strong, while poorer ones feel more ordinary than the badge suggests.",
