    "yearRange": "2019-2023",
    "timingType": "Chain. The OM654 utilizes a simplex timing chain located at the REAR of the engine block. While generally more robust than older 4-cylinders, stretching can occur at high mileages if oil changes are neglected, requiring an expensive engine-out or transmission-out repair. Listen closely for a metallic rattle on cold starts.",
    "injectionType": "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI) operating at an immense 2050 bar. Utilizes highly precise Piezo injectors. After dropping the 1.6L, Mercedes returned to the 2.0L block for the E 200 d, heavily detuning it via software. It retains the incredibly compact, engine-mounted SCR (AdBlue) and DPF system to meet Euro 6d standards.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. Because this full 2.0-liter block is artificially detuned to just 160 hp, the internal mechanical stress is practically non-existent. However, the true lifespan is heavily dictated by the rigorous maintenance of its highly condensed, heat-soaked emissions systems (DPF, dual-EGR, SCR) and the rear timing chain.",
    "turboType": "Single Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT). The turbocharger provides excellent low-end response. Because it is mechanically identical to the 194 hp E 220 d, the turbocharger is vastly under-stressed in this 160 hp application.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MD1. An incredibly advanced engine control unit that manages the fuel injection, VNT turbo, and the ultra-strict Euro 6d emissions monitoring. This version features a software cap limiting power, making it incredibly lucrative for ECU tuning.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6d-TEMP / Euro 6d-ISC-FCM",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "280+ hp. The internal forged rotating assembly is incredibly strong. Because the engine is mechanically identical to the higher-output versions, it can safely reach 230+ hp with just a software flash.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "6.0 - 7.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "4.2 - 5.2 L/100km",
      "mixed": "4.8 - 6.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "8.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.52). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the rear timing chain, the delicate rocker arms, and prevent DPF clogging.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Rear Timing Chain Stretch: The OM654 locates its timing chain at the rear of the block, making access incredibly difficult for mechanics. Due to its single-row design, it stretches over high mileages if oil changes are stretched beyond 10,000 km. You will hear a distinct, sharp metallic rattle on cold starts when the tensioner begins to fail.",
      "Camshaft Rocker Arm Wear: A critical and highly specific flaw in the OM654 is the rapid wear of the camshaft rocker arms, also known as roller cam followers. If oil changes are neglected or oil quality drops, these rockers wear down, causing severe camshaft damage. This manifests as a loud, rhythmic ticking noise from the top of the engine and requires immediate attention.",
      "Engine-Mounted DPF/SCR Thermal Stress: To heat up the emissions system quickly, the DPF and SCR catalyst are bolted directly to the engine block. This subjects the surrounding sensors, wiring harnesses, and plastic components to extreme, constant heat soak. This intense thermal environment massively accelerates the failure rate of nearby electronic components.",
      "Complex AdBlue (SCR) System Failures: The dual-dosing AdBlue system is incredibly complex and highly prone to sensor failure. The NOx sensors frequently degrade from extreme heat and soot, while the AdBlue injector itself can crystalize and block. This triggers dashboard countdowns that will eventually prevent the car from starting until the dealer resets the software.",
      "Switchable Water Pump Issues: The OM654 uses a vacuum-actuated switchable water pump to bring the engine up to temperature rapidly. The internal shroud mechanism can jam over time due to coolant degradation or vacuum leaks. This leads to localized overheating or extremely slow warm-up times, directly triggering persistent thermostat faults.",
      "EGR and Intake Manifold Soot Buildup: Modern emissions standards force the engine to ingest a significant amount of exhaust gases via high and low-pressure EGR systems. This soot mixes with oil vapor from the PCV to form a thick, tar-like sludge inside the intake manifold, severely restricting airflow. Over time, this chokes the engine and frequently throws the car into Limp Mode.",
      "Tandem Oil/Vacuum Pump Leaks: The engine utilizes a combined oil and vacuum pump located at the absolute rear of the block. The internal seals can fail over time, leading to engine oil leaking down the bellhousing. This is often misdiagnosed as a rear main seal leak and requires significant labor to access and replace."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Engine-Out Timing Chain Repair: Because the timing chain is positioned practically against the firewall, what is a standard maintenance task becomes a massive financial burden. Mechanics often must drop the transmission or the entire engine just to replace the chain and guides. Neglecting the chain stretch will ultimately result in the pistons striking and destroying the valves.",
      "Catastrophic Valvetrain Failure: If the rocker arm wear is ignored, the camshaft lobes will literally grind themselves flat against the seized roller followers. The resulting metal shavings will circulate through the engine oil, scoring the cylinder walls and destroying the oil pump. A complete camshaft and lifter replacement is a massively expensive, labor-intensive repair that often totals the engine.",
      "AdBlue No-Start Countdown: If the ECU detects any fault in the incredibly sensitive SCR system, it initiates a hard, irreversible countdown timer. Once the permitted number of miles or engine starts reaches zero, the ECU completely immobilizes the vehicle. You will be completely stranded until a dealership replaces the faulty hardware and resets the encrypted emissions software.",
      "DPF Blockage from Urban Commuting: The highly efficient OM654 relies on extreme exhaust temperatures to actively burn off trapped soot in the DPF. If the car is used strictly for short city trips, these crucial regenerations are constantly aborted by the driver shutting off the engine. The filter will fatally clog, creating massive backpressure that can physically destroy the turbocharger.",
      "Oil Dilution via Aborted Regenerations: During a DPF regeneration, extra raw diesel is injected late in the combustion cycle to raise exhaust temperatures. If the regeneration is aborted by shutting the car off, this unburnt diesel washes down the cylinder walls and heavily dilutes the engine oil. This massively reduces oil viscosity, rapidly accelerating wear on the main bearings and the fragile valvetrain.",
      "Tandem Pump Labor Tax: An oil leak from the tandem oil/vacuum pump is common, but because it sits tightly between the engine and firewall, it is notoriously difficult to access. Mechanics often quote massive labor hours just to replace a simple internal O-ring on the pump. Ignoring the leak will coat the transmission bellhousing and the entire undercarriage in thick oil."
    ],
