    "yearRange": "2016-2019",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In pre-facelift W213 E 300 form, the M274.920 is the highest mainstream factory calibration of the early non-hybrid 2.0-liter petrol setup and gives the chassis a much more effortless character than the lower E 200 and E 250 tunes. It still belongs to the modern downsized turbo era rather than the older large-capacity Mercedes tradition, but in day-to-day use it feels strong, flexible, and properly matched to the size of the car. The extra power and torque do not make it a sports sedan, yet they give the drivetrain the reserve it needs to feel convincingly premium. In healthy condition, it is one of the best four-cylinder petrol W213 combinations.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and Mercedes engine management. In E 300 tune, the M274.920 feels more relaxed under load than the softer calibrations because the extra torque is available earlier and more consistently in normal use. It remains a modern direct-injection turbo engine, so smooth operation still depends heavily on clean oil, stable cooling, healthy ignition parts, and accurate diagnostics. When maintained properly, it feels cohesive and quietly fast. When neglected, it becomes expensive through several overlapping medium-size issues rather than one single catastrophic weakness.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 340,000 km. The M274.920 in E 300 tune can cover strong mileage when oil changes are conservative, cooling-system health is monitored, and ignition, breather, carbon, and timing-related symptoms are addressed early. These engines often stay smooth enough to hide moderate deterioration, which is why service history matters more than a quick test drive. A strong example can remain refined and powerful for many years, while a neglected one tends to accumulate thermostat problems, oil leaks, carbon buildup, timing wear, misfires, and small boost-related faults. Long-term durability depends far more on disciplined maintenance than on the higher output figure.",
    "turboType": "Single twin-scroll turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine. In E 300 form, the turbocharger and calibration give the W213 the kind of mid-range torque reserve that makes the car feel naturally expensive rather than merely adequate. The main strength is not drama but effortlessness, especially in overtaking, motorway cruising, and carrying load. It suits drivers who want a genuinely complete petrol executive sedan without stepping into six-cylinder cost territory. When healthy, it feels more substantial and more naturally premium than the lower-tuned M274 variants.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, turbo boost coordination, and variable camshaft timing logic. This gives the engine very smooth factory behavior when everything is right, but it also means ignition weakness, cooling drift, timing wear, PCV faults, carbon buildup, and boost irregularities can overlap and dilute refinement without one dramatic failure pattern. Accurate diagnosis matters because tired examples often feel only slightly flatter, rougher, or less expensive than they should. On the stronger E 300 tune, extra torque can mask developing problems for longer than on the lower-output variants. The best cars feel quietly strong and cohesive, while poor ones feel suspiciously ordinary.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "270-290 hp. Mild gains are possible through careful software optimization, but preserving factory smoothness, timing health, cooling stability, and gearbox behavior is usually the smarter ownership priority.",
