    "yearRange": "2016-2019",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In USA and North American W213 E 300 form, the M274.920 keeps the same broad mechanical character as the main pre-facelift E 300 but with a slightly different market calibration. The result is still a strong and naturally usable executive-sedan drivetrain with very similar real-world flexibility. It remains a modern turbocharged four-cylinder rather than a traditional Mercedes big engine, yet it feels properly matched to the car in everyday driving. In healthy condition, it is a polished and convincing mainstream premium setup.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and Mercedes engine management with market-specific emissions calibration. Compared with the 245 hp version, this North American calibration gives away very little in practical use because the broad torque curve remains the defining trait of the drivetrain. It still depends on clean oil, stable cooling, healthy ignition parts, and accurate diagnostics to feel premium. When maintained properly, it feels smooth, flexible, and quietly strong. When neglected, it suffers from the same layered modern turbo-engine ageing pattern as the rest of the M274 family.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 340,000 km. The M274.920 in North American E 300 tune can cover strong mileage when oil changes are conservative, cooling-system health is monitored, and ignition, breather, carbon, and timing-related symptoms are addressed early. These engines often stay smooth enough to flatter weak examples, so service history matters more than first impressions. A strong example can remain refined and powerful for many years, while a neglected one tends to accumulate thermostat problems, oil leaks, carbon buildup, timing wear, misfires, and boost-related issues. Long-term durability still depends much more on maintenance quality than on the small output difference.",
    "turboType": "Single twin-scroll turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine. In USA E 300 form, the turbocharged 2.0-liter setup gives the W213 enough low-end and mid-range strength to feel convincingly premium in daily use. The strongest quality is easy torque access rather than top-end drama, which suits the character of the chassis very well. It works best for drivers who want a smooth and modern petrol executive sedan without stepping into larger-engine ownership costs. When healthy, it feels much more complete than its cylinder count suggests.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz engine management with direct-injection control, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, turbo boost coordination, variable camshaft timing logic, and market-specific emissions behavior. This creates smooth factory calibration when healthy, but it also means ignition weakness, cooling drift, timing wear, PCV faults, carbon buildup, and boost irregularities can overlap without one dramatic failure point. Accurate diagnosis matters because tired examples often feel merely flatter or rougher rather than clearly broken. Extra torque reserve still helps mask problems longer than on lower-output M274 variants. The best cars feel cohesive and quietly strong, while the bad ones feel more ordinary than the badge suggests.",
    "euroStandard": "Market-specific emissions calibration",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "265-285 hp. Mild gains are possible through careful software optimization, but preserving factory smoothness, timing health, cooling stability, and gearbox behavior is usually the smarter ownership priority.",
