    "timingType": "Double-roller chain. The M276 engine utilizes a robust timing chain system, and by 2018, most factory updates (like secondary check valves) were implemented. However, the hydraulic tensioners themselves still wear at high mileages. Listen closely for a brief, sharp metallic rattle on cold starts, which indicates a loss of hydraulic tension.",
    "injectionType": "Direct Injection (CGI) with Piezo injectors operating at up to 200 bar. This model also integrates a Gasoline Particulate Filter (GPF) in the exhaust to meet Euro 6d-TEMP standards. While direct injection provides immense power, it inherently introduces the risk of severe carbon buildup on the intake valves over time.",
    "engineLifespan": "200 000 - 350 000+ km. The 3.0L BiTurbo V6 block is mechanically exceptionally reliable. The true lifespan of the vehicle is largely dictated by strict adherence to oil change intervals, preventing cylinder scoring, and maintaining the delicate twin-turbo cooling system.",
    "turboType": "Twin Turbochargers (BiTurbo). Nestled tightly alongside the block, they provide monstrous torque with minimal lag. The plastic coolant lines feeding these turbos are extremely fragile and will snap from heat cycling, dumping coolant rapidly.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MED17.7.3. A highly advanced ECU managing direct injection and boost pressure. The E 450 is essentially a software-unlocked E 400, offering 34 more horsepower from the factory. It is incredibly receptive to further aftermarket tuning.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6d-TEMP (With GPF)",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "500+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and crankshaft are heavily over-engineered. The block itself handles increased boost effortlessly, limited only by the thermal capacity of the factory turbos and the transmission.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "11.0 - 13.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "7.5 - 9.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "8.5 - 10.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "14.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.51 or 229.52). Low-SAPS oil is required to protect the delicate GPF filter while protecting the highly stressed timing chain tensioners.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Camshaft Sensor Oil Leaking: The camshaft position sensors are prone to internal seal failure over time due to intense engine heat. Oil pushes through the sensor body directly into the engine wiring harness. If ignored, the oil travels straight to the main engine control unit (ECU).",
      "Turbocharger Coolant Line Brittleness: The twin turbochargers are supplied with coolant via incredibly thin plastic lines routed over the blistering hot exhaust manifolds. These lines bake in the heat, becoming so brittle they can snap simply from engine vibration or light touch during maintenance.",
      "Timing Chain Tensioner Rattle: Even with factory updates, the hydraulic chain tensioners lose their ability to hold oil pressure over high mileages. This causes a terrifying metallic rattle on cold starts as the loose chain slaps the guides. Preventative replacement is critical to stop the chain from physically stretching.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes over the intake valves. Over time, thick, tar-like carbon sludge builds up on the valves, causing misfires and restricting airflow. Professional walnut blasting is required every 100,000 kilometers.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Wear: The camshaft-driven high-pressure fuel pumps work immensely hard to support the boost levels and frequently fail internally. This leads to extended cranking times, severe power loss under heavy acceleration, and potential engine leaning.",
      "GPF Sensor Faults: The Gasoline Particulate Filter sensors can be sensitive to short urban trips, throwing check engine lights if the exhaust doesn't reach high enough temperatures to burn off trapped soot."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: If the leaking camshaft sensors are ignored, capillary action will draw the oil through the wiring harness directly into the Bosch ECU. Once the ECU is flooded with oil, it shorts out and requires complete, highly expensive replacement. Installing preventative pigtail harnesses is mandatory.",
      "Catastrophic Coolant Loss: When the brittle plastic turbo coolant lines inevitably snap, they dump the engine's coolant supply almost instantly onto the hot exhaust. If the driver does not pull over immediately, the aluminum engine block will warp from extreme overheating, destroying the engine.",
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Jump: If the cold-start chain rattle is ignored for too long, the timing chain will physically stretch beyond the tensioner's limits. The chain will eventually jump teeth on the camshaft sprockets, causing the pistons to strike the open valves, necessitating a complete engine replacement.",
      "Fatal Cylinder Wall Scoring: The M276 utilizes a Nanoslide aluminum block coating that is highly susceptible to scoring if a fuel injector fails and washes the lubricating oil away. This results in severe oil consumption, aggressive misfires, and a heavy knocking sound from the bottom end.",
      "Transmission Torque Overload: The massive 500 Nm of factory torque places immense strain on the 9G-Tronic transmission. Bypassing the strict 120,000 km fluid service interval guarantees premature torque converter and clutch pack failure."
    ],
