    "yearRange": "2019-2020",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In facelift W213 E 300 form, the M264.920 is the stronger mainstream petrol four-cylinder before the later M254 transition and gives the car a much more complete executive character than the lower E 200 tune. The 48V EQ Boost layer improves low-speed response, driveline smoothness, and stop-start quality, so the powertrain feels more polished than a conventional downsized turbo setup. It still does not imitate a larger six-cylinder Mercedes engine, but it is refined, flexible, and convincingly premium in daily use. In healthy condition, it is one of the best-balanced facelift four-cylinder petrol W213 drivetrains.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and integrated 48V mild-hybrid support. Compared with the lower-output facelift petrol variants, this E 300 tune feels noticeably more effortless under load and more relaxed at motorway speeds. It remains a modern direct-injection turbo engine, so real-world refinement still depends heavily on clean oil, stable cooling, healthy ignition components, and accurate diagnosis. The EQ Boost layer helps mask small drivability gaps, but it does not eliminate the normal wear patterns of a modern turbo petrol drivetrain. A strong one feels polished and cohesive, while a tired one becomes expensive through several medium-size issues rather than one single dramatic failure.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 360,000 km. The M264.920 can cover strong mileage when oil changes are conservative, the cooling system is monitored, and ignition, breather, carbon, timing, and boost-related symptoms are addressed early. Like many refined modern Mercedes engines, it can continue to feel acceptable even after deterioration has started, which makes service history more important than a brief test drive. A healthy example can remain smooth, fast enough, and very usable for many years, while a neglected one tends to collect thermostat faults, coolant-pump issues, carbon buildup, oil seepage, timing-control irregularities, and mild-hybrid support problems. Long-term durability depends much more on disciplined upkeep than on the factory output itself.",
    "turboType": "Single twin-scroll turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine. In E 300 facelift tune, the turbocharger and calibration give the W213 a broad and relaxed torque delivery that suits the executive chassis very well. The strongest quality is not excitement but ease, especially in part-throttle driving, overtaking, and sustained motorway use. The EQ Boost support also helps smooth transient response and low-rpm loading. When healthy, it feels more naturally premium than many other four-cylinder luxury-car powertrains.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management integrated with 48V mild-hybrid control logic, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, boost coordination, and variable camshaft timing. This gives the engine a very smooth factory character when healthy, but it also means ignition weakness, thermal drift, timing deviations, breather faults, carbon buildup, and boost irregularities can overlap and quietly reduce refinement without one obvious dramatic symptom. Proper diagnosis matters because the mild-hybrid layer can make a weak car feel deceptively smoother at first. On the stronger E 300 calibration, extra torque reserve can hide deterioration longer than on lower-output versions. The best examples feel quietly strong and highly cohesive.",
