    "yearRange": "2021-2023",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In late W213 E 300 form, the M254.920 represents the newer modular-generation petrol four-cylinder with integrated starter-generator technology and a more advanced electrical architecture than the earlier M264. The result is a smoother, cleaner, and slightly stronger-feeling drivetrain with more immediate low-end support and better transient refinement. It still belongs to the small-capacity turbo era rather than the old larger-engine Mercedes tradition, but it feels notably more mature in calibration. In healthy condition, it is arguably the most technically complete mainstream petrol four-cylinder offered in the W213 lifecycle.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and integrated 48V mild-hybrid support via ISG. Compared with the earlier M264-based facelift setup, the M254 package feels slightly sharper in low-speed response and more seamless in general driveline integration. It still depends on clean oil, stable thermal control, good ignition parts, and proper diagnostics, because modern refinement often hides developing issues rather than announcing them clearly. The ISG support improves the premium feel of the drivetrain, but it also makes the system architecture more complex. A strong example feels very polished, while a neglected one becomes expensive through layered electrical, cooling, and combustion-related faults.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 360,000 km. The M254.920 should cover strong mileage when oil service is conservative, thermal management remains stable, and ignition, breather, intake, timing, and hybrid-support issues are addressed early. The engine feels modern enough to flatter tired examples during brief drives, which makes history and diagnostics more important than first impressions. A healthy car can stay refined and very usable for years, while a neglected one may accumulate thermostat faults, cooling-module problems, carbon buildup, sensor drift, oil seepage, boost issues, and 48V integration complaints. Long-term ownership depends much more on disciplined servicing than on the promise of newer-generation engineering.",
    "turboType": "Single twin-scroll turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine. In late E 300 tune, the turbocharger works with the ISG support to give the W213 a broad and very polished torque delivery that suits the platform extremely well. The strongest quality is how effortless and integrated it feels rather than how dramatic it sounds. Low-speed response, mid-range pull, and executive-road smoothness are all improved by the newer-generation control strategy. When healthy, it feels like a highly developed final evolution of the four-cylinder petrol W213 concept.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management integrated with 48V ISG control logic, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, boost coordination, variable camshaft timing, and more advanced hybrid support strategy than the older M264. This creates an impressively polished factory character when healthy, but it also means cooling drift, ignition weakness, timing issues, sensor faults, charge-air leaks, breather faults, and hybrid-support irregularities can overlap without a dramatic single failure mode. Accurate diagnostics are essential because the drivetrain often feels only slightly less smooth or less crisp rather than obviously broken. On the M254, workshop quality matters almost as much as the hardware itself. The best examples feel exceptionally cohesive for a four-cylinder executive powertrain.",
