    "yearRange": "2018-2020",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In E 300 e plug-in hybrid form, the M274.920 works as the combustion half of a much more torque-rich drivetrain than a normal four-cylinder E-Class. The petrol engine itself still has the familiar M274 character, but the electric motor transforms low-speed response, urban refinement, and initial acceleration. It does not feel like a pure performance model, yet the combined output gives the car a stronger and more expensive character than the regular petrol four-cylinder versions. In healthy condition, it is one of the most effortless non-AMG pre-facelift W213 drivetrains.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and plug-in hybrid drivetrain coordination. The petrol engine remains a modern direct-injection turbo unit with all the usual sensitivity to oil quality, cooling stability, ignition condition, and carbon buildup, but here it also has to cooperate smoothly with high-voltage hybrid logic, regenerative braking, charging management, and electric torque blending. When healthy, the result feels smooth, quiet, and surprisingly muscular. When neglected, ownership becomes expensive because ordinary M274 ageing problems overlap with hybrid-system complexity. A good example feels much newer than the engine family itself.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 340,000 km. The M274.920 itself can cover strong mileage when oil changes are conservative, cooling-system health is monitored, and timing, ignition, breather, and carbon-related issues are handled early. In the E 300 e, long-term durability also depends heavily on high-voltage system care, charging hardware condition, battery-cooling stability, and clean hybrid diagnostics. A healthy example can stay refined and powerful for many years, while a neglected one tends to accumulate thermostat faults, timing wear, oil seepage, carbon buildup, charge-system errors, battery-support issues, and layered electronic complaints. The hybrid system does not make the petrol engine weak, but it raises the cost of poor maintenance.",
    "turboType": "Single twin-scroll turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine paired with a plug-in hybrid electric drive unit. In pre-facelift E 300 e form, the turbo engine provides the upper-speed and sustained-load foundation, while the electric motor fills the low-end torque gap and gives the car a much more immediate response than a conventional four-cylinder executive sedan. The strongest quality is not soundtrack or drama but seamless thrust. Around town and during overtaking, the drivetrain feels substantially stronger than its petrol engine alone would suggest. When healthy, it delivers a very complete premium-luxury driving experience.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management integrated with plug-in hybrid control logic, battery management, regenerative braking strategy, charging coordination, adaptive fueling, boost control, and variable camshaft timing. This gives the car very smooth factory behavior when everything is right, but it also means cooling faults, ignition weakness, timing wear, breather issues, boost irregularities, battery-support problems, and charging-side faults can overlap without one obvious dramatic failure point. Proper diagnosis matters because the electric support can mask a petrol-side weakness during a short drive. The best examples feel cohesive and quiet, while poor ones become costly through several medium-size faults at once.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
