    "yearRange": "2023-present",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In W214 E 200 form, the M254.920 is positioned as the entry petrol engine, but in practice it does not behave like a stripped basic drivetrain because the 48V integrated starter-generator gives the whole system a noticeably cleaner and more upscale feel than older entry-level Mercedes petrol engines. The first impression is not raw punch but smoothness: the engine starts unobtrusively, responds more neatly off the line than its output figure alone suggests, and feels much less burdened by the size of the car than a traditional low-output four-cylinder would. The long-stroke 2.0-liter character means it prefers calm, torque-led progress over high-rpm excitement, and that suits the W214 platform very well. In daily use, it feels like a modern luxury-sedan base engine rather than a compromise. When healthy, it gives the car a quiet, mature, and properly premium underlying character, even if it never tries to imitate a six-cylinder Mercedes.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and integrated 48V mild-hybrid support via ISG. In E 200 tune, the drivetrain is clearly calibrated around refinement, emissions control, and real-world drivability rather than around dramatic top-end performance, which means the petrol engine and the 48V system are constantly working together to make the car feel smoother, lighter, and more polished than its curb weight would otherwise suggest. The combustion engine itself still follows the familiar rules of a modern direct-injection turbo setup: it wants clean oil, stable cooling, good spark quality, healthy crankcase ventilation, and accurate sensor behavior if it is going to keep that factory-smooth personality over the long term. What makes this version a little deceptive is that the hybrid support helps cover small lapses in drivability, so a car with early-stage deterioration can still feel superficially acceptable during a short drive. A strong example feels crisp, quiet, and naturally expensive in the way it reacts to throttle inputs. A weak one usually does not fail theatrically; instead, it loses sharpness, smoothness, and cohesion a little at a time until the whole powertrain feels more ordinary than a current E-Class should.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 360,000 km. The M254.920 has the potential to cover strong mileage, but it is one of those modern Mercedes engines where longevity depends much more on maintenance discipline and diagnostic quality than on any simple headline about inherent strength. Conservative oil changes, early attention to cooling-system irregularities, proper ignition upkeep, and careful monitoring of oil level and oil-consumption behavior matter much more here than waiting for obvious symptoms. Because the drivetrain is refined by design, weak examples can remain deceptively convincing for a surprisingly long time, which is why a smooth test drive on its own means much less than documented service history, cold-start behavior, and evidence of sensible ownership. A healthy engine can remain quiet, efficient, and mechanically composed for many years, especially in cars that have not been abused by stretched service intervals or chronic short-trip neglect. A neglected one typically does not die in one dramatic event; it accumulates oil-consumption concerns, carbon buildup, timing noise, cooling-module problems, misfires, sensor drift, boost inconsistencies, and mild-hybrid complaints until ownership becomes expensive through overlap rather than catastrophe.",
    "turboType": "Single turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine paired with 48V mild-hybrid ISG support. In the E 200, the turbocharger is not there to create a dramatic surge or an overtly sporty personality; instead, it works with the long-stroke engine layout and the hybrid assist to produce a broad, accessible band of torque that makes the car feel smoother and less effortful in ordinary driving than the numbers alone imply. The strongest part of the setup is the way it covers low-speed and medium-speed use: urban rolling, light-throttle acceleration, traffic merging, and relaxed motorway cruising all suit it very well because the drivetrain does not need to work hard to feel civilized. It is best understood as an engine designed to disappear into the background of the luxury experience rather than dominate it. When everything is right, it gives the W214 the kind of calm and unobtrusive momentum that people expect from an E-Class. When it is not right, the loss of polish is usually more obvious than any loss of outright speed.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management integrated with 48V ISG control logic, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, boost coordination, and variable camshaft timing. In practice, this means the powertrain is governed by a very layered control environment in which combustion quality, hybrid support, thermal strategy, ignition performance, sensor accuracy, and boost behavior all interact closely with one another, which is excellent for smooth factory behavior but not so forgiving when the car begins to age. One of the defining realities of this engine is that several medium-size issues can overlap and blur into each other: a car may feel slightly flatter, slightly rougher, slightly less eager to restart cleanly, or slightly less crisp under load without presenting one dramatic clue. That is why proper diagnosis matters far more than parts-swapping. On the E 200 calibration, the drivetrain should feel light-footed, clean in its reactions, and almost understated in how neatly it delivers its performance. The best cars feel integrated and mature; tired ones feel merely competent, which is exactly why they can be easy to underestimate.",
