    "compression": "10.0:1",
    "yearRange": "2024-present",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In W214 E 300 form, the M254.920 represents the strongest non-plug-in four-cylinder petrol interpretation of the new E-Class range, and that gives it a noticeably different character from the ordinary E 200 even though the basic engine family is shared. The stronger calibration brings more authority in the mid-range, more ease during overtaking, and a more expensive-feeling reserve of performance in a car that is otherwise focused on comfort and composure. It still does not try to mimic a six-cylinder in soundtrack or silkiness, but it moves the W214 with a confidence that feels much more natural for the size and status of the car. The result is one of those modern Mercedes drivetrains that quietly suits the platform better than the badge might suggest. When healthy, it feels complete, mature, and properly premium rather than merely adequate.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and integrated 48V mild-hybrid support via ISG. In E 300 tune, the system feels substantially more resolved because the extra petrol-side output allows the drivetrain to rely less on clever smoothing tricks and more on genuine performance reserve. The engine still lives by the same rules as any modern Mercedes direct-injection turbo unit: it wants clean oil, stable heat management, accurate ignition behavior, healthy crankcase ventilation, and clean diagnostic integrity if it is going to preserve its polished factory character. What makes the E 300 appealing is that it combines that modern technical smoothness with enough real shove to make the big W214 feel properly effortless rather than just competent. The best examples feel calm and strong at the same time. Neglected ones are dangerous in the opposite way: they can still feel fast enough to impress briefly while underlying thermal, ignition, boost, or oil-consumption issues are already beginning to stack up.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 360,000 km. The M254.920 in E 300 tune should be capable of strong mileage, but the higher calibration does not excuse careless ownership, and in some ways it makes discipline even more important because the drivetrain is expected to operate with more sustained authority. Conservative oil changes, careful monitoring of oil level and oil-consumption trends, strong cooling-system maintenance, early attention to misfire or boost irregularities, and proper 48V system care are all central to long-term ownership quality. A healthy example can remain impressively smooth, muscular, and civilized for many years, especially if the car has not been subjected to long service intervals or indifferent fuel quality. A neglected one rarely fails in a single spectacular way; instead, it accumulates timing concerns, heat-management issues, carbon buildup, support-battery complaints, charge-air weakness, and gradual loss of factory cohesion. Because the car is powerful enough to mask moderate wear, buyers can underestimate tired examples more easily than with the lower-output variants.",
    "turboType": "Single turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine paired with 48V mild-hybrid ISG support. In this E 300 configuration, the turbocharger is working in a much more confidence-building role than in the E 200 because the stronger 258 hp / 400 Nm calibration gives the W214 the sort of easy real-world pace that better matches its size, insulation, and executive-luxury brief. Low-speed response remains clean and polished thanks to the ISG, but the more important difference appears in rolling acceleration and medium-speed load response, where the car feels less like a careful four-cylinder luxury sedan and more like a genuinely effortless modern Mercedes. It still favors refinement over excitement, yet it no longer feels as though the platform is being gently negotiated by the engine. Instead, the drivetrain feels like it has the breathing room to do the car justice. When healthy, it is arguably the sweet spot for buyers who want a non-plug-in petrol W214 with proper composure and reserve.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management integrated with 48V ISG control logic, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, boost coordination, and variable camshaft timing. In E 300 form, the calibration is clearly more assertive, but not in a crude or aggressive way; the software is still tuned around smoothness and premium drivability, just with a deeper torque reserve and more relaxed access to performance. That makes this version highly satisfying when healthy, but also slightly deceptive when evaluating used examples because the engine can continue to feel strong enough even after some of its mechanical sharpness has started to fade. Weak ignition, growing charge-air leakage, cooling drift, oil-consumption changes, or early timing wear may show up first as a loss of polish rather than a loss of speed. As always with modern Mercedes petrol engines, the right way to judge one is by how cleanly it behaves across cold start, low load, repeated heat cycles, and stored fault history, not merely by whether it feels quick on one test drive. A good one feels strong and expensive. A tired one feels strong but suspiciously unrefined around the edges.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6e",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "285-300 hp",
