    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with variable camshaft adjustment. In W214 E 300 e form, the M254.920 is no longer just a modern 2.0-liter petrol engine but the combustion half of a much more advanced luxury plug-in hybrid system. The petrol side supplies smooth, refined long-distance ability and sustained-load competence, while the electric side transforms the way the car reacts at low speed, in traffic, and under part-throttle use. Compared with a normal four-cylinder executive sedan, the drivetrain feels richer, quieter, and more expensive because it can deliver immediate electric torque without needing the petrol engine to work hard at every moment. The result is not a dramatic sports-sedan character, but a highly polished and mature one. When healthy, it feels like one of the most technically complete mainstream W214 drivetrains rather than a compromise powertrain built around economy rules.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, variable camshaft timing, adaptive ignition control, and fourth-generation plug-in hybrid drivetrain coordination. In this application, the petrol engine is constantly working within a much broader control environment than in a conventional E 200 or E 300, because hybrid blending, regenerative braking, battery temperature management, charging behavior, and electric torque delivery all influence how the car feels. The combustion engine still follows the normal rules of a modern Mercedes turbo four-cylinder, so clean oil, stable thermal behavior, healthy ignition, good breather condition, and accurate sensor data remain essential for long-term quality. What makes the E 300 e special is that when everything is right, the car often feels smoother and stronger than the numbers alone suggest, especially in everyday driving. What makes it potentially expensive is that the same refinement can mask early-stage petrol-side weakness or hybrid-side deterioration until several medium-size issues overlap at once.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 360,000 km. The M254.920 itself should be capable of strong mileage when oil service is conservative, heat management remains stable, and ignition, intake, timing, and breather-related issues are dealt with before they become layered faults. In the E 300 e, long-term durability also depends heavily on high-voltage battery condition, cooling-circuit stability, onboard charging health, hybrid software behavior, and proper diagnostics over time. A healthy example can remain quiet, powerful, and highly usable for many years, especially if it has been charged regularly and maintained as a plug-in hybrid rather than merely as a petrol sedan. A neglected one tends to accumulate a wider spread of medium-cost issues: thermostat behavior, electric coolant-pump problems, carbon buildup, charge-port faults, support-battery complaints, HV cooling issues, and occasional drivability oddities that are harder to diagnose than to notice. The hybrid system does not make the M254 weak, but it absolutely raises the cost of careless ownership.",
    "turboType": "Single turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine paired with a transmission-integrated electric motor and plug-in hybrid battery system. In the W214 E 300 e, the turbocharger no longer needs to carry the entire burden of low-speed drivability by itself because the electric motor fills in the first phase of response with much greater immediacy than a conventional turbo petrol drivetrain can offer. That gives the car a more effortless and more premium feel than its four-cylinder layout might suggest. Around town, the drivetrain can feel almost oversized for the task because it moves the car with calm, electrically assisted confidence, while at higher speed the petrol side gives the system the range and sustained cruising ability expected of an E-Class. It is best understood not as a performance gimmick but as a luxury-oriented hybrid powertrain that happens to be strong. When healthy, it offers one of the cleanest blends of electric smoothness and petrol versatility in the W214 lineup.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management integrated with plug-in hybrid control logic, battery management, regenerative braking strategy, charging coordination, adaptive fueling, boost control, and variable camshaft timing. This gives the factory drivetrain a very polished character because the car can continuously balance petrol torque, electric torque, battery state, and thermal demands in a way that feels almost invisible to the driver. The downside is that several issues can overlap without presenting one dramatic mechanical signature: weak ignition, slight charge-air leakage, mild cooling drift, support-battery weakness, charging faults, HV-system temperature issues, or early petrol-side wear can all appear first as a loss of cohesion rather than a clear breakdown. That is why strong diagnostics matter more than a single road test. A good E 300 e feels highly integrated, expensive, and technically mature. A weak one may still feel fast enough, but not clean enough in the details to justify long-term confidence.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6e",
