    "euroStandard": "EPA Bin 20 / SULEV 20",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with CAMTRONIC variable valve control. In North American W214 E 350 form, the M254.920 sits in a very deliberate sweet spot: it is clearly stronger and more naturally suited to the size of the E-Class than a base-output four-cylinder, but it is still calibrated to behave like a refined premium daily drivetrain rather than a dramatic performance engine. The 48V integrated starter-generator smooths the low-speed experience and helps the car feel cleaner and more immediate off the line, which matters in a luxury sedan where the powertrain is expected to disappear into the overall comfort and isolation of the car. The result is a modern executive-sedan character built around calm confidence, quiet reserve, and easy torque delivery rather than excitement for its own sake. When healthy, it feels more complete and more expensive than many rival four-cylinder luxury drivetrains.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with twin-scroll turbocharging, intercooling, adaptive ignition control, and North America-specific engine-management calibration. In the E 350, the system is tuned to deliver polished real-world drivability, emissions-compliant operation, and enough mid-range authority to move the W214 with proper ease, which is why the car often feels more substantial and more effortless than the raw power figure alone suggests. It still follows all the normal rules of a modern Mercedes direct-injection turbo engine: it wants clean oil, stable heat management, healthy ignition components, good crankcase ventilation behavior, and accurate sensor data if it is going to preserve that factory-smooth personality over time. One of the key ownership realities is that the drivetrain can stay superficially convincing even while several medium-size issues are beginning in the background. A strong example feels crisp, quiet, and cohesive, while a weak one usually becomes less sharp and less premium without one single dramatic failure.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 360,000 km. The M254.920 has the potential for strong long-term service life, but like most modern Mercedes turbo petrol engines, its durability depends far more on maintenance quality and diagnostic discipline than on headline output. Conservative oil changes, early attention to cooling irregularities, proper ignition service, and routine monitoring of oil level and oil-consumption behavior matter much more than simply trusting the engine because it feels refined on a short test drive. In the E 350, the extra smoothness added by the 48V system can make tired examples feel better than they really are during brief use, so documented service history and clean cold-start behavior matter a lot. A healthy example can remain quiet, efficient, and very composed for many years, while a neglected one usually ages through overlapping issues such as oil-consumption drift, carbon buildup, timing noise, cooling-module faults, misfire sensitivity, boost inconsistencies, and mild-hybrid support complaints.",
    "turboType": "Twin-scroll turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine with 48V integrated starter-generator support. In the North American E 350, the turbo system is there not to create a dramatic surge but to give the W214 the sort of broad, easy torque delivery that suits a modern executive sedan. The strongest part of the setup is the way it handles medium-speed work: traffic merging, rolling overtakes, urban acceleration, and steady highway cruising all feel relaxed because the engine does not need to strain to move the car cleanly. The mild-hybrid layer then smooths the first step away from rest and helps the drivetrain feel more polished than a conventional turbo four would. This gives the E 350 a calm but properly premium personality. When healthy, it feels quietly confident, and when something is off, the loss of polish is often more noticeable than the loss of outright speed.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management integrated with 48V ISG control logic, adaptive fueling, knock regulation, boost coordination, and variable camshaft timing. In practice, this means the drivetrain is governed by a dense control environment where combustion quality, hybrid support, thermal strategy, ignition performance, and boost behavior all overlap very closely. That is excellent for smooth factory behavior, but it also means several medium-scale problems can blur into one another as the car ages. A weak support battery, slight charge-air leak, minor cooling drift, tired spark plugs, or early timing-related behavior may each present first as a subtle loss of refinement rather than as a dramatic fault event. That is why proper diagnostics matter far more than casual parts-swapping. A good E 350 should feel neat, integrated, and easy in its reactions, while a tired one often still feels respectable without feeling fully right.",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "285-300 hp",
