    "yearRange": "2023-present",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven turbocharged inline-four timing layout with advanced variable valve control. In W214 E 400 e form, the M254.920 is no longer simply a refined two-liter Mercedes petrol engine, but the combustion half of a much more ambitious plug-in hybrid drivetrain that is clearly tuned to deliver a stronger and more premium result than the ordinary plug-in variants. The internal-combustion side is lifted to a higher output level so that it no longer acts merely as a support unit for the electric module during sustained use, but as an equal performance partner that can uphold the character of a serious executive sedan even after the battery assistance becomes less dominant. That gives this drivetrain a noticeably more authoritative personality than a lower-output hybrid setup. The result is a car that can feel calm and luxurious at one moment, then very forceful and composed the next, without becoming harsh or unrefined. When everything is healthy, the timing, boost, and hybrid blending work together in a way that makes the whole powertrain feel dense, expensive, and technically sophisticated rather than merely powerful on paper.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with turbocharging, intercooling, adaptive ignition strategy, variable valve control, and high-level plug-in hybrid coordination. In the E 400 e, the combustion side has more work to do than in a comfort-biased plug-in hybrid because it is expected to support a much stronger total system output while still preserving Mercedes-style smoothness, emissions control, and long-distance usability. That means the engine depends heavily on precise mixture formation, stable oil quality, reliable ignition energy, clean boost control, and accurately functioning sensors if it is going to keep its intended polished feel over the long term. A major ownership reality here is that the electric side can flatter the car for quite a while even when the petrol side is no longer truly sharp, because the system still feels strong and refined in many short driving situations. A healthy example feels crisp, rich, and highly integrated. A weak one often stays deceptively impressive in straight-line pace while losing subtle refinement, consistency, and driveline cleanliness in ways that become more obvious the longer you live with the car.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 340,000 km. The M254.920 can still be a durable engine in this application, but the E 400 e places it inside a much denser and more expensive operating environment than a normal petrol-only E-Class. Long-term survival depends not just on conservative oil changes and proper petrol-engine maintenance, but also on hybrid thermal management, charging behavior, battery condition, restart smoothness, transmission blending quality, and the health of several overlapping cooling and electrical subsystems. The higher-output petrol calibration also means that neglect becomes more expensive, because the drivetrain is designed to deliver strong system performance without feeling strained, and once that reserve begins to erode the loss of factory polish becomes more obvious. A healthy example can remain very smooth, fast, and impressively civilized for a long time, especially when it is maintained as a true plug-in hybrid rather than as a petrol car with a cable. A neglected example tends to age through layers of medium-cost issues rather than one spectacular failure: oil-consumption drift, cooling irregularities, ignition weakness, charge-port faults, battery-related warnings, charge-air leaks, software oddities, and hybrid blending complaints can gradually turn the car from technically brilliant into financially tiring.",
    "turboType": "Single turbocharged direct-injection inline-four petrol engine working alongside a transmission-integrated electric drive and a large high-voltage battery system. In the E 400 e, the turbocharger does more than provide efficiency-oriented mid-range support; it is part of a drivetrain that is expected to feel genuinely strong and properly luxurious in a heavy executive sedan. The electric side handles the first layer of response and low-speed immediacy, but the petrol side has enough output on its own to ensure that the car still feels substantial and well-matched to its platform when road speed rises or repeated heavy acceleration is demanded. That is what separates this version from softer plug-in hybrids that feel excellent in town but less convincing when driven hard or over distance. Here, the hybrid system is not merely compensating for a modest engine; it is amplifying an already substantial combustion side. When healthy, the result is a broad, deep, and unusually mature wave of performance that feels more like a premium grand-touring drivetrain than a compliance-focused eco variant.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management integrated with plug-in hybrid control logic, high-voltage battery management, regenerative braking coordination, transmission blending strategy, adaptive fueling, boost control, and variable camshaft regulation. This is one of those drivetrains where the factory smoothness comes from extremely tight cooperation between many layers at once, which is wonderful when the car is healthy and frustrating when it is not. Small faults in the charging system, battery temperature management, 12V support, ignition side, boost plumbing, or petrol-engine thermal behavior can all overlap in ways that do not immediately point to one simple cause. Because total system output is high, weak examples often continue to feel subjectively fast enough to hide problems from a short test drive. That is why diagnostic depth matters much more than surface impressions. A strong E 400 e feels seamless, urgent, calm, and expensive all at once. A weak one often feels powerful in bursts, but slightly untidy, inconsistent, or suspiciously artificial in how that performance is delivered.",
