    "compression": "10.5:1",
    "yearRange": "2023-present",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-six timing architecture with CAMTRONIC variable valve control. In W214 E 450 form, the M256.930 is the kind of engine that gives the E-Class the depth and authority many buyers still expect from a proper upper-tier executive sedan, because it combines the innate smoothness of a straight-six with the immediacy and polish of a modern electrified support system. Unlike a smaller turbo four-cylinder that must rely on calibration tricks and insulation to feel expensive, the M256 starts from a fundamentally richer mechanical character, so the car feels calmer, fuller, and more substantial even before the mild-hybrid system adds its contribution. The long, linear delivery of the inline-six suits the W214 exceptionally well, because the platform is at its best when it feels effortless rather than dramatic, and this engine has the natural reserve to make that effortlessness feel authentic instead of artificial. The 48V layer refines the first moment of response, smooths restarts, and helps the whole drivetrain feel more cohesive in traffic, but it is the six-cylinder's deep mechanical balance that defines the car's premium personality. When healthy, this engine does not merely move the E-Class strongly; it gives it the kind of cultured, high-grade power delivery that makes the entire vehicle feel like a more serious and more complete luxury car.",
    "injectionType": "Direct petrol injection with twin-scroll turbocharging, intercooling, variable valve control, adaptive ignition strategy, particulate-filter-equipped emissions management, and tightly integrated 48V mild-hybrid coordination. In the E 450, the injection and boost system are not tuned for a peaky or theatrical kind of power; instead, they are calibrated to support broad torque, clean response, stable emissions performance, and a high degree of refinement under changing loads, which is exactly what a modern Mercedes six-cylinder executive sedan is supposed to deliver. The result is a drivetrain that often feels smoother and stronger than the raw numbers alone suggest, because the engine management constantly blends combustion quality, boost response, restart behavior, and hybrid support in ways the driver usually notices only as a sense of polish. That polish, however, depends on very accurate sensor data, healthy injectors, clean intake and charge-air control, proper oil quality, and stable thermal behavior over time. Once those begin to drift, the M256 usually does not become obviously bad all at once; instead, it becomes slightly rougher, slightly less crisp, and slightly less elegant in the way it builds and delivers its performance. That is why well-kept examples feel expensive in a deep mechanical sense, while tired ones often still feel fast enough but no longer feel special enough.",
    "engineLifespan": "240,000 to 380,000 km. The M256.930 has the basic ingredients for very strong long-term durability, because it is a relatively unstressed modern inline-six with strong torque spread, excellent refinement, and a design philosophy more focused on premium road use than on extreme specific output, but in practice its lifespan depends heavily on preventive maintenance and especially on how well the cooling, lubrication, and electrified support systems are looked after. This is not the sort of engine that rewards neglect just because it feels smooth, since modern Mercedes six-cylinders can mask early-stage wear or irregularities for quite a long time before the ownership experience starts to become expensive. Conservative oil service, clean thermal behavior, careful attention to oil-consumption trends, healthy PCV performance, and proper 48V diagnostics all matter if the engine is to preserve the silky, effortless quality that makes it attractive in the first place. A strong example can age very gracefully and remain one of the most satisfying mainstream Mercedes petrol drivetrains of its era, especially for long-distance or high-speed use where the straight-six layout really shows its value. A neglected one usually does not fail in one dramatic step, but instead accumulates layers of medium-cost issues such as oil leaks, cooling-pump faults, charge-air inconsistencies, electrical support faults, carbon buildup, sensor irregularities, and oil-consumption concerns that gradually erode the sense of engineering quality. The difference between a brilliant M256 and a financially tiring one usually comes down to maintenance discipline and diagnostic quality much more than to headline mileage alone.",
    "turboType": "Twin-scroll turbocharged direct-injection inline-six petrol engine with integrated 48V mild-hybrid support. In the W214 E 450, the turbocharger is used exactly the way a premium road car benefits from most: not to create an abrupt wall of boost, but to deepen and widen the engine's already naturally refined delivery so that the car feels muscular without feeling busy. Because the engine starts from a six-cylinder foundation, the turbo system does not need to work overtime to disguise a lack of base character, and that gives the whole drivetrain a cleaner, more mature feel than many smaller turbocharged alternatives. Low and medium speeds feel especially strong because the broad torque band and electrical assistance combine to produce quick but calm forward motion, while higher-speed work still retains the kind of sustained, composed pull that smaller engines often lose once the initial shove is over. This is one of the reasons the E 450 feels like the genuinely premium sweet spot in the W214 range for buyers who still value combustion-engine character. When healthy, the turbocharged M256 does not feel merely powerful; it feels deep, elastic, and dignified in a way that suits the E-Class almost perfectly.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management integrated with 48V ISG control logic, adaptive fueling, knock control, boost regulation, restart coordination, and variable camshaft and valve-control strategy. In real-world ownership, this matters because the drivetrain's impressive smoothness is not coming from one simple mechanical trick, but from a very dense network of combustion, electrical, thermal, and drivability decisions being made continuously in the background. The advantage is that a healthy E 450 feels almost perfectly blended: restarts are neat, low-speed take-up is clean, torque arrives without fuss, and the car rarely seems caught out by sudden throttle changes or urban traffic conditions. The disadvantage is that several medium-size issues can overlap and imitate one another, so a weak 48V support battery, a mild charge-air leak, an early cooling problem, an aging sensor, or developing ignition weakness may all present first as a subtle loss of polish rather than a dramatic warning event. That is why this engine rewards proper diagnostics much more than guesswork or indiscriminate parts replacement. A good M256-powered E 450 should feel expensive in the details, not just in the acceleration figure, and once that detailed cohesion is gone, the engine is telling you something even if the car still feels subjectively quick.",
