    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain. The M271.860 'EVO' replaces the older supercharged engine's dual-row chain with a single-row chain. This simplex chain is notoriously fragile and highly prone to catastrophic stretching, often failing before 100,000 km. The camshaft adjusters (VVT sprockets) also wear out internally. Listen for a loud, metallic rattle on cold starts for 2-5 seconds.",
    "injectionType": "Direct Injection (CGI) operating at up to 140 bar via Piezo injectors. While efficient, the direct-injected nature introduces severe risk of carbon sludge buildup on the intake valves over time.",
    "engineLifespan": "150 000 - 250 000+ km. The core cast aluminum block is durable, but the engine's true lifespan is entirely dictated by the owner's willingness to replace the fragile timing chain system preventatively. If the chain is neglected, the engine will destroy itself.",
    "turboType": "Single Exhaust Gas Turbocharger (IHI). Replaced the older Eaton supercharger for better efficiency. The turbo itself is generally robust, but the electronic wastegate actuator can fail.",
    "ecuType": "Siemens SIM271DE2.0. A complex engine control unit managing direct injection and boost pressure. Responds moderately well to ECU tuning, though limited by the relatively small turbocharger and internal components.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "250+ hp. The internal rotating assembly is adequate for stock power, but pushing significant boost increases is risky without upgrading the inherently weak timing chain system first.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "9.5 - 11.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "6.0 - 7.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "7.5 - 9.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "12.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "5.5L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the fragile timing chain and the turbocharger bearings.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Stretch: The defining flaw of the M271 EVO. The simplex chain stretches rapidly. Combined with a weak hydraulic tensioner, the chain slaps against the plastic guides on cold starts. Ignoring this noise will lead to the chain jumping teeth.",
      "Camshaft Adjuster (VVT) Wear: The variable valve timing sprockets wear out internally, failing to lock into position upon engine shutdown. This exacerbates the cold start rattle and eventually throws timing correlation check engine lights.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes over the intake valves. Thick, tar-like carbon sludge builds up on the valves, causing misfires and restricting airflow. Professional walnut blasting is required.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Failure: The camshaft-driven high-pressure fuel pump works immensely hard and can leak oil internally into the fuel system or fail to build adequate pressure, causing severe hesitation and stalling.",
      "PCV Valve Failure: The crankcase ventilation hoses hidden beneath the intake manifold degrade, crack, and collapse due to heat and oil vapor. This causes massive vacuum leaks, rough idling, and check engine lights.",
      "Thermostat Failure: The electronically mapped thermostat frequently fails in the open position, preventing the engine from reaching its optimal operating temperature. This leads to poor fuel economy and increased emissions."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Valve-to-Piston Collision: If the timing chain stretch and cold start rattle are ignored, the chain will eventually jump teeth on the worn camshaft adjusters. This completely ruins the engine timing, causing the pistons to strike and bend the open valves, necessitating a total engine rebuild or replacement.",
      "Camshaft Gear Destruction: The teeth on the camshaft adjusters literally wear down to sharp points due to chain slap and poor lubrication. Replacing the chain without replacing the worn adjusters guarantees the new chain will fail quickly.",
      "Oil Dilution: If the high-pressure fuel pump fails internally, raw gasoline can seep directly into the crankcase, massively diluting the engine oil. This destroys the oil's viscosity, rapidly accelerating wear on the main bearings and the already fragile timing chain.",
      "Vacuum Leak Limp Mode: Collapsed or cracked PCV hoses hidden under the intake manifold create massive, unmetered air leaks. The ECU cannot compensate for this extra air, resulting in severe misfires, stalling at idle, and Limp Mode."
    ],
