    "yearRange": "2014-2016",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven 72-degree V6 diesel timing layout. In late-facelift C207 and A207 E 350 BlueTEC form, the OM642.838 is the kind of engine that gives the elegant coupe and cabriolet bodies the serious long-distance character that smaller four-cylinder diesels cannot fully replicate, because it combines heavy mid-range torque, premium cruising refinement, and effortless road speed in a way that feels genuinely substantial. This engine is not about sharp throttle theatrics or flashy high-rpm character; its appeal is the calm, deep reserve of power that makes the whole car feel expensive, relaxed, and mechanically mature. The late BlueTEC calibration also gives it a cleaner and more modern identity than the earlier CDI versions, even though it remains rooted in the same fundamentally strong V6 diesel architecture. In everyday use, the engine suits the C207 platform exceptionally well because the coupe and cabriolet both benefit from an easy, quiet, torque-rich power delivery that matches their grand-touring role perfectly. When healthy, the OM642.838 makes the car feel like a proper upper-class diesel GT rather than merely a stylish E-Class with good numbers on paper.",
    "injectionType": "Common-rail direct diesel injection with turbocharging, intercooling, BlueTEC SCR aftertreatment, AdBlue dosing, and emissions-focused Euro 6 calibration. In the E 350 BlueTEC, the fuel system is tuned less for aggressive response and more for broad, clean torque delivery, smooth low-speed flexibility, and long-distance efficiency, which is exactly what this version of the C207 needs in order to feel premium. The result is a drivetrain that usually feels stronger in the real world than its power figure alone suggests, because the torque arrives early and remains broad enough to make the car seem untroubled by speed, load, or incline. At the same time, this later OM642 depends on much more than just the basic mechanical diesel core, since injector quality, boost control, EGR behavior, DPF health, SCR function, AdBlue delivery, and thermal stability all play a major role in how refined and trouble-free the engine remains. A healthy example feels rich, unhurried, and expensive in the way it gathers speed. A weak one often still feels strong enough in simple acceleration, but starts losing the polished consistency that separates a proper Mercedes V6 diesel from an ordinary used executive coupe.",
    "engineLifespan": "260,000 to 420,000 km. The OM642.838 is one of those engines that can cover very high mileage if it is maintained correctly, because the basic V6 diesel architecture is fundamentally robust and was designed for exactly the kind of broad-torque, high-mileage service that suits the E-Class platform. However, the late BlueTEC versions are more dependent on supporting systems than the earlier simpler variants, so long-term durability is no longer just about whether the short block is strong. Oil cooler sealing, swirl-flap condition, charge-air integrity, injector health, cooling-system stability, DPF loading, AdBlue hardware condition, and clean diagnostic history all matter if the engine is to remain both durable and pleasant to own. A good example can still feel muscular, smooth, and impressively relaxed even at higher mileage, especially when used regularly on longer journeys that suit a large diesel best. A neglected one tends to age through a stack of medium-cost and emissions-related faults rather than through one cinematic engine failure, and that accumulation is what makes some OM642 cars feel financially draining despite the engine's reputation for strength. The best way to think about this motor is that the core engine is durable, but the systems surrounding it determine whether ownership remains satisfying.",
    "turboType": "Turbocharged common-rail V6 diesel with intercooling and BlueTEC aftertreatment integration. In the late E 350 BlueTEC, the turbo setup is there to build a thick, broad wall of low- and mid-range torque rather than a dramatic top-end surge, which suits the role of a refined diesel grand tourer perfectly. The engine rarely needs to feel worked hard because the torque comes in early and decisively, and that gives the coupe a calm, expensive kind of pace that is more about effortless progress than excitement. This is one of the reasons the OM642-powered C207 still feels special to many drivers: it gives the car a deep mechanical authority that smaller or more highly stressed engines often cannot imitate. When healthy, the turbo-diesel torque makes the car feel heavy in a good way, as if it is simply leaning into the road and covering distance without effort. When boost leaks, actuator issues, or soot-related problems begin, the engine often loses refinement before it loses strength, and that subtle erosion is often the real warning sign that the car is no longer at its best.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz common-rail diesel engine management with adaptive injection control, turbo regulation, DPF regeneration strategy, EGR management, SCR/AdBlue control, thermal protection logic, and automatic-transmission torque coordination. In ownership terms, this means the late OM642 is not just a strong old-school diesel with some modern emissions hardware attached, but a heavily coordinated drivetrain whose premium feel depends on several systems working smoothly together. The advantage is that a healthy E 350 BlueTEC feels deeply integrated: torque delivery is clean, cruising is quiet, shifts are unobtrusive, and the engine usually seems to have effortless reserve in all normal situations. The drawback is that multiple different subsystems can produce overlapping symptoms, so injector imbalance, EGR contamination, DPF loading, SCR faults, AdBlue issues, weak thermostatic behavior, charge-air leaks, or transmission calibration drift may all show up first as a vague loss of polish rather than an obvious single-cause failure. That is why careful diagnostics matter far more than casual impressions. A good OM642.838 should feel calm, dense, and expensively sorted. A weak one often still feels quick enough, but slightly coarse, slightly uneven, or suspiciously reluctant to deliver that original Mercedes smoothness.",
