    "repairCostEstimate": "1,500 - 3,500 EUR / year (The massive rating accounts for the existential threat of piston cracking via LSPI, timing chain/VVT replacement, PCV failures, and plastic water pump blowouts.)",
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is mandatory to combat oil dilution, protect the delicate VVT phasers, and minimize the risk of LSPI.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "VVT Phaser Rattle: The variable valve timing sprockets on the camshafts suffer from internal wear, failing to lock securely upon engine shutdown. This leads to a distinct, loud metallic rattling noise for two to three seconds upon cold startup. Ignoring this warning sign will ultimately destroy the camshafts and the timing chain mechanism.",
      "Water Pump Failure: The engine-driven coolant pump utilizes a fragile plastic impeller that degrades rapidly from continuous extreme thermal cycling. The plastic housing inevitably cracks or the impeller shatters, leading to a sudden and massive loss of engine coolant. Upgrading to a durable aftermarket metal impeller pump is highly recommended to prevent engine overheating.",
      "PCV Valve Assembly Failure: The crankcase ventilation valve is notoriously prone to failing and passing excessive, unmetered oil vapor into the intake tract. This failure coats the entire intercooler and intake manifold in a thick layer of oil. This exacerbates direct injection carbon buildup and dramatically lowers the effective octane of the fuel.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump Wear: The camshaft-driven high-pressure fuel pump works immensely hard to support the direct injection system and frequently fails internally. This leads to extended cranking times, severe power loss under acceleration, and erratic idling. In worse cases, it can leak raw fuel directly into the crankcase, destroying the engine oil.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes over the intake valves to keep them clean. Thick, tar-like carbon sludge builds up rapidly on the valve stems, causing random misfires and heavily restricting engine airflow. Professional walnut blasting is absolutely required at higher mileages to restore performance.",
      "Thermostat Housing Cracks: The electronically mapped thermostat housing is constructed from plastic that becomes incredibly brittle due to high engine bay temperatures. The housing often cracks along its seams, causing slow, persistent coolant leaks that are difficult to trace. Replacing the entire assembly is required to restore cooling system integrity."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Engine Failure via LSPI: Low-Speed Pre-Ignition (LSPI) is a terrifying phenomenon where fuel detonates prematurely inside the cylinder due to oil vapors or low-octane fuel. This violent, uncontrolled detonation literally shatters the fragile cast aluminum pistons into pieces. A cracked piston instantly scores the cylinder wall and turns the engine into scrap metal.",
      "Severe Oil Dilution: If the high-pressure fuel pump begins to leak internally, raw gasoline pours directly into the crankcase, massively diluting the engine oil. This destroys the oil's critical viscosity, rapidly accelerating wear on the main bearings and the simplex timing chain. Strict oil change intervals are absolutely critical to monitor and prevent this.",
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Jump: If the cold-start chain rattle is ignored for too long, the timing chain will physically stretch beyond the hydraulic tensioner's limits. The loose chain will eventually jump teeth on the camshaft sprockets, completely ruining the engine's mechanical timing. This causes the pistons to strike the open valves, necessitating a massively expensive complete replacement.",
      "Rear Main Seal Blowout: If the PCV system fails entirely and clogs, the resulting crankcase pressure has nowhere to safely vent. This extreme pressure will forcefully blow out the rear main seal, dumping the engine's oil supply between the engine and transmission. Fixing this catastrophic leak requires dropping the transmission, resulting in a massive labor bill.",
      "Subframe Corrosion Collapse: The rear subframe of this vehicle generation is notoriously susceptible to severe internal rusting, especially in regions that use heavy road salt during winter. The corrosion rots the metal from the inside out, often snapping the lower control arm mounting points while driving. This catastrophic structural failure requires a completely new subframe assembly.",
      "Turbocharger Actuator Seizing: The electronic wastegate actuator controlling the turbocharger is highly vulnerable to the extreme heat generated by the exhaust manifold. The internal linkage can seize or the electronic motor can burn out, preventing the turbo from building boost. This instantly throws the vehicle into a heavily restricted Limp Mode, making highway driving dangerous."
    ],
