    "timingType": "Double roller chain. By 2010, the infamous balance shaft sprocket failure that plagued early M272 engines had been fully resolved by the factory. The timing chain system on these later models is highly robust, though the chain can still stretch and the plastic guides can become brittle over extremely high mileages.",
    "injectionType": "Multi-Port Fuel Injection (MPI). Unlike the newer CGI engines, this naturally aspirated block injects fuel into the intake manifold before the intake valves. This completely eliminates the issue of carbon sludge buildup on the intake valves, vastly improving long-term reliability.",
    "engineLifespan": "250 000 - 400 000+ km. The naturally aspirated, port-injected M272 is widely regarded as an incredibly durable workhorse once the early balance shaft issues were ironed out. Its true lifespan depends primarily on fixing minor oil leaks and maintaining the plastic intake manifold flaps.",
    "turboType": "Naturally Aspirated. It entirely lacks the complex forced induction systems of modern engines. This offers a highly linear power delivery, immediate throttle response, and significantly fewer expensive components that can fail.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch ME9.7. A reliable and well-understood engine management unit. Because the engine is naturally aspirated, aftermarket ECU tuning provides very minimal power gains, mostly limited to sharper throttle response.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "300+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and cast aluminum block are heavily over-engineered for the stock 231 horsepower. However, significantly increasing power requires forced induction, which is not economically viable for this specific platform.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "10.5 - 12.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "7.0 - 8.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "8.5 - 10.0 L/100km",
      "sporty": "13.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.0L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Regular 10,000 km oil change intervals are highly recommended to protect the timing chain guides and the complex variable valve timing mechanisms.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Intake Manifold Tumble Flap Failure: The variable intake manifold uses fragile plastic arms to actuate the internal tumble flaps. Due to constant heat cycling and oil vapor exposure, these plastic arms become brittle and snap. This prevents the flaps from moving, severely restricting engine breathing, triggering a check engine light, and causing sluggish performance.",
      "Camshaft Adjuster Magnet Leaks: The four circular camshaft adjuster magnets located at the front of the cylinder heads are notorious for developing internal seal leaks. Engine oil slowly pushes through the seal and drips directly into the electrical connector. This oil can then travel through the wiring harness, creating a massive electrical hazard.",
      "Oil Centrifuge / Separator Leaks: The PCV system utilizes a plastic oil centrifuge cover located at the rear of the right cylinder head. The factory sealant degrades over time, causing engine oil to drip down the back of the engine block. This oil often burns on the hot exhaust piping, creating a distinct burning smell in the cabin.",
      "Thermostat Sticking Open: The engine coolant thermostat is a common wear item that frequently fails mechanically in the open position. This prevents the V6 engine from reaching its optimal 90°C operating temperature, especially during winter months. The resulting over-cooling forces the ECU to run a rich fuel mixture, destroying fuel economy.",
      "Secondary Air Injection Pump Failure: The electric secondary air pump, responsible for pumping air into the exhaust on cold starts to heat the catalytic converters, frequently suffers from internal bearing failure. It will emit a loud, whining vacuum-cleaner noise on cold starts before seizing entirely.",
      "Engine Mount Collapse: The liquid-filled hydraulic engine mounts are subjected to the heavy weight and torque of the 3.0L V6 block. Over 100,000 kilometers, the rubber tears and the internal hydraulic fluid leaks out. This causes a harsh, annoying vibration to resonate through the steering wheel and cabin while idling in gear."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: If the leaking camshaft adjuster magnets are completely ignored, capillary action will draw the engine oil through the wiring harness directly into the main Bosch ECU. Once the oil floods the delicate circuitry of the control unit, it shorts out, requiring a massively expensive ECU replacement and a new engine wiring harness.",
      "Catastrophic Intake Manifold Ingestion: In severe cases of intake manifold degradation, the internal plastic tumble flaps can break apart entirely. If a piece of the broken plastic flap is sucked through the intake port and into the combustion chamber, it will cause catastrophic valve and piston damage.",
      "Conductor Plate Failure (7G-Tronic): The 722.9 automatic transmission houses its Transmission Control Module (TCM/Conductor Plate) inside the extremely hot oil pan. The delicate speed sensors on this plate frequently fail due to intense heat and metallic debris in the fluid. This instantly throws the transmission into Limp Mode, locking it in second gear.",
      "Subframe Corrosion Collapse: The rear subframe of the C207 chassis is highly susceptible to severe internal rusting, particularly in areas heavily treated with winter road salts. The structural metal rots from the inside out, often causing the lower control arms to detach abruptly while driving.",
      "Rear Main Seal Oil Leaks: If the PCV oil centrifuge system becomes clogged with sludge, it creates excessive internal crankcase pressure. This pressure will forcefully blow out the rear main seal, dumping the engine's oil supply between the engine block and the transmission bellhousing.",
      "Catalytic Converter Meltdown: If the thermostat fails open and the engine constantly runs a rich fuel mixture, the unburnt fuel will pass directly into the exhaust system. Over time, this excess fuel ignites inside the catalytic converters, melting the precious metal honeycomb structure and requiring an incredibly expensive exhaust replacement."
    ],
