    "yearRange": "2013-2014",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven 72-degree V6 diesel timing layout. In the early facelift C207 and A207 E 350 BlueTEC, the OM642.838 gives the elegant coupe and cabriolet bodies the kind of deep, expensive-feeling diesel torque that smaller engines simply cannot imitate, because it combines strong low-end pull, quiet long-distance reserve, and an effortlessly mature driving character. This version already lives in the Euro 6 world, so while the underlying OM642 still feels like a traditionally robust Mercedes V6 diesel, it is also more sensitive to healthy emissions control, temperature management, and clean intake behavior than the earlier, less complex CDI variants. That gives the engine an interesting dual nature: mechanically it still feels old-school solid, but operationally it depends on several modern systems behaving properly in the background. In real use, the engine suits the C207 platform extremely well because the car benefits far more from dense mid-range torque and stable motorway composure than from a peaky performance feel. A good example feels calm, weighty, refined, and genuinely premium in the way only a proper Mercedes diesel GT can.",
    "injectionType": "Common-rail direct diesel injection with turbocharging, intercooling, SCR / AdBlue aftertreatment, diesel particulate filter support, and early Euro 6-focused emissions calibration. In this early BlueTEC specification, the OM642.838 is not just a powerful V6 diesel but a carefully managed emissions-era drivetrain where combustion quality, aftertreatment health, and thermal control all play a visible role in how refined the car feels. The torque delivery is usually stronger in practice than the paper figure alone suggests, because the engine develops a thick and steady mid-range that makes the coupe feel unhurried and substantial rather than merely quick. At the same time, injector smoothness, boost accuracy, EGR cleanliness, DPF loading, SCR dosing, and coolant-temperature stability all matter if the engine is to preserve that polished feel over time. A healthy example feels smooth, muscular, and deeply sorted. A tired one often still feels fast enough in a straight line, but gradually loses the creamy consistency and calm authority that define a really good OM642 Mercedes.",
    "engineLifespan": "260,000 to 420,000 km. The basic OM642 architecture is fully capable of high mileage because it was developed for exactly the kind of broad-torque, long-distance diesel service that suits the E-Class family so well. However, this early Euro 6 BlueTEC version is more dependent on its supporting systems than simpler earlier OM642 applications, so long-term durability is no longer only about the strength of the block and rotating assembly. Oil cooler seals, swirl-system wear, thermostat behavior, injector balance, DPF health, SCR / AdBlue reliability, boost integrity, and proper diagnostics all determine whether the engine remains satisfying to own. A well-kept example can still feel impressively strong, clean, and underworked even with significant mileage, especially when it has a history of proper long-distance use. A neglected example usually becomes expensive not because the engine catastrophically collapses, but because several medium-cost diesel and emissions issues begin to stack together. The engine itself is durable, but the total ownership experience depends on how healthy the surrounding systems remain.",
    "turboType": "Turbocharged common-rail V6 diesel with intercooling and BlueTEC aftertreatment integration. In the early facelift E 350 BlueTEC, the turbo system is tuned to create a thick, broad wave of low- and mid-range torque rather than a dramatic high-rpm power rush, which is exactly what suits a refined executive coupe or cabriolet. The engine rarely feels like it needs effort, because even modest throttle inputs produce substantial forward motion, and that gives the whole car a rich and grown-up character. This is part of the OM642 appeal in the C207: it makes the car feel expensive through depth and reserve rather than noise or flash. When everything is healthy, the torque delivery feels dense, linear, and quietly authoritative. When charge-air leaks, actuator issues, or soot-related restrictions begin to appear, the engine often loses refinement and fullness before it becomes obviously slow, and that subtle erosion is usually the real warning sign.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz common-rail diesel engine management with adaptive injection control, turbo regulation, DPF regeneration strategy, EGR management, SCR / AdBlue control, thermal protection logic, and transmission torque coordination. In practical terms, this means the early Euro 6 OM642 is a deeply integrated powertrain whose premium feel depends on multiple subsystems cooperating smoothly rather than on mechanical strength alone. The advantage is that a healthy car feels exceptionally cohesive, with clean torque delivery, quiet cruising, and smooth automatic behavior that suits the chassis beautifully. The disadvantage is that several different problems can overlap and imitate one another, so weak injector balance, DPF loading, EGR contamination, SCR faults, poor thermostat behavior, or boost leakage may all begin as a vague loss of polish rather than one obvious breakdown. That complexity is why guesswork becomes expensive very quickly on these engines. A strong OM642.838 feels calm, complete, and mechanically expensive. A weak one often still feels quick, but no longer genuinely sorted.",
