    "timingType": "Chain. The M278 utilizes an incredibly complex timing chain system. Early iterations are notorious for severe secondary chain tensioner rattle on cold starts. The hydraulic tensioners leak down overnight, causing a loud metallic slap. Installing updated tensioners alongside preventative check valves in the cylinder head is strictly mandatory to prevent terminal chain stretch and engine timing failure.",
    "injectionType": "Direct Injection (CGI) via highly precise piezo-electric injectors operating at up to 200 bar. While providing excellent efficiency and brutal low-end torque for the turbochargers, this introduces the inevitability of thick carbon sludge buildup on the intake valves over time.",
    "engineLifespan": "200 000 - 350 000+ km. The M278 is an absolute powerhouse, but its lifespan is highly controversial due to the terrifying risk of Silitec/Alusil cylinder wall scoring. The true lifespan of the vehicle is largely dictated by incredibly strict adherence to premium oil changes and proactively replacing the fragile plastic turbo coolant lines.",
    "turboType": "Twin Turbochargers (BiTurbo). Nestled tightly alongside the block, they provide monstrous, immediate torque with virtually no lag. However, the plastic coolant lines feeding these turbos bake in the extreme heat of the V8 and will inevitably snap, requiring immediate preventative replacement.",
    "ecuType": "Bosch MED17.7.3. A highly advanced ECU managing direct injection and dual boost pressure control. The M278 is an absolute legend in the tuning community, effortlessly producing well over 500 horsepower with a simple Stage 1 software flash.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5 / Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "650+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and crankshaft are heavily over-engineered, sharing much of their architecture with the AMG M157 engine. The block handles massive boost effortlessly, limited mostly by the transmission torque converter.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "12.0 - 14.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "7.5 - 9.0 L/100km",
      "mixed": "9.5 - 11.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "16.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "8.0L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the delicate twin-turbos, the hydraulic timing chain tensioners, and crucially, the incredibly sensitive cylinder wall coating.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Silitec Cylinder Wall Scoring: The absolute most terrifying flaw of the M278. Poor lubrication, extended oil change intervals, or fuel injector failure can cause the piston rings to severely gouge the aluminum cylinder coating. This causes massive oil consumption and requires a total engine replacement.",
      "Turbocharger Coolant Line Brittleness: The twin turbochargers are supplied with coolant via incredibly thin plastic lines routed directly over the blistering hot exhaust. These lines bake in the heat, becoming so brittle they can snap simply from engine vibration.",
      "Timing Chain Tensioner Rattle: The secondary hydraulic chain tensioners lose their ability to hold oil pressure over high mileages when the engine is off. This causes a terrifying metallic rattle on cold starts as the loose chain slaps the plastic guides.",
      "Camshaft Sensor Oil Leaking: The four camshaft position sensors are highly prone to internal seal failure due to intense ambient engine heat. Engine oil slowly pushes through the sensor body directly into the wiring harness.",
      "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes over the intake valves. Over time, thick, tar-like carbon sludge builds up on the valves, heavily restricting engine airflow.",
      "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Wear: The dual camshaft-driven high-pressure fuel pumps work immensely hard to support the BiTurbo boost levels. They can wear out internally, potentially leaking raw fuel into the oil pan and ruining the oil's viscosity.",
      "Intercooler Pump Heat Soak: The air-to-water intercooler system relies on a small electric pump to circulate coolant. When intake temperatures skyrocket during spirited driving, the ECU pulls engine timing, making the V8 feel sluggish."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Engine Replacement via Scoring: If the cylinder walls become scored, there is no cheap fix. The engine will consume liters of oil and misfire constantly. The only remedy is replacing the entire engine block or having it professionally re-sleeved, which is financially devastating.",
      "Catastrophic Coolant Loss: When the highly brittle plastic turbo coolant lines inevitably snap, they dump the engine's coolant supply almost instantly onto the hot exhaust. If the driver does not pull over immediately, the aluminum engine block will warp from extreme overheating.",
      "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: If the leaking camshaft sensors are ignored, capillary action will draw the oil through the wiring harness directly into the Bosch ECU. Once the ECU is flooded with oil, it shorts out and requires complete, highly expensive replacement.",
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Jump: If the cold-start chain rattle is ignored for an extended period, the timing chain will physically stretch beyond the tensioner's operational limits. The loose chain will eventually jump teeth on the camshaft sprockets, causing the pistons to strike the valves.",
      "Cylinder Scoring from Fuel Dilution: If the high-pressure fuel pumps fail and leak raw gasoline into the crankcase, it instantly destroys the oil's viscosity. This diluted oil cannot protect the cylinder walls, leading directly to fatal scoring.",
      "Transmission Torque Overload: The massive 600 Nm of factory torque, which increases exponentially with tuning, places immense strain on the 7G-Tronic transmission. Bypassing the strict 60,000 km fluid service interval guarantees premature torque converter glazing."
    ],
