    "valves": 24,
    "compression": "15.5:1",
    "yearRange": "2011-2013",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven 72-degree V6 diesel timing layout. In the pre-facelift LS E 350 CDI, the OM642.838 delivers the kind of low-end richness and long-legged torque that made Mercedes V6 diesels so desirable in the first place, because it feels strong without needing to be stressed. This version sits in the earlier, less emissions-heavy stage of the OM642 story, so it keeps more of the original mechanical straightforwardness that many owners associate with the best Mercedes diesel character. The engine suits the C207/A207 chassis very well because the coupe and cabriolet benefit more from deep, flexible torque than from a high-revving personality. In normal road use it feels calm, mature, and capable of covering distance with very little effort, which is exactly what a premium diesel grand tourer should do. The LS variant’s stronger calibration gives the car a more muscular, urgent feel than lower-output OM642 versions, while still remaining fundamentally relaxed in character.",
    "injectionType": "Common-rail direct diesel injection with turbocharging and intercooling, tuned here for stronger factory pressure targets and broader torque delivery. In the pre-facelift LS configuration, the OM642.838 was calibrated to feel especially substantial in the mid-range, so the car responds with a thick wave of diesel torque rather than a sharp or nervous throttle feel. That is one of the reasons the engine works so well in the E-Class coupe and cabriolet body styles, where smooth, effortless acceleration matters more than outright sportiness. The hardware remains broadly robust, but as with all OM642 variants, injector behavior, boost integrity, intake cleanliness, and oil/cooling health have a major influence on how refined the engine feels over time. A healthy example feels dense, clean, and expensive in the way only a good Mercedes V6 diesel can. A neglected one can still feel fast enough, but gradually loses the refined, confident character that defines the best versions of this motor.",
    "engineLifespan": "260,000 to 430,000 km. The OM642 family is capable of very high mileage when maintained properly, and the pre-facelift LS calibration does not fundamentally change that durability potential. The key difference is that this earlier version usually feels a little more mechanically direct and a little less dependent on modern emissions layers than later BlueTEC cars, which can be an advantage in long-term ownership. That said, age-related problems still accumulate if the car is neglected, especially around the oil cooler seals, intake swirl system, cooling circuit, turbo plumbing, and injector-related combustion quality. A well-kept example can remain impressively smooth and strong well past typical executive-car mileage, especially if it sees regular long-distance use. A poor example usually does not fail in one dramatic event, but instead becomes expensive through several medium-sized diesel repairs that slowly erode the ownership experience. The base engine is strong enough; the question is whether the surrounding systems have been kept just as healthy.",
    "turboType": "Turbocharged common-rail V6 diesel with intercooling. In the LS pre-facelift configuration, the turbo system is calibrated to deliver a thick and immediate sense of torque rather than a peaky performance surge, which is exactly what a Mercedes E-Class diesel should feel like. The engine has enough low-end reserve to make the car feel heavier in the good sense: stable, full, and confident rather than frantic. That makes highway driving especially relaxed, because the drivetrain can maintain speed and accelerate without seeming strained. When the boost system is healthy, the engine feels deeper and more expensive than smaller diesels, with a smooth swell of torque that suits the long, elegant body lines of the C207. When boost leaks, actuator wear, or intake restrictions appear, the first symptom is often a loss of density and refinement rather than an obvious loss of outright speed. That makes careful inspection important, because the engine can still feel acceptable long after it has stopped feeling truly right.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz common-rail diesel engine management with adaptive injection control, turbo regulation, thermal protection logic, and automatic-transmission torque coordination. The pre-facelift LS OM642.838 is a relatively mature version of Mercedes diesel control logic, so it feels more straightforward than later Euro 6 BlueTEC systems while still being highly integrated with the gearbox and emissions hardware of the era. A healthy setup gives the car a very cohesive character: clean torque delivery, smooth shifts, and a steady, unforced way of building speed. The drawback is that faults can still overlap, so injector imbalance, boost leakage, thermostat drift, EGR contamination, and transmission wear can all produce similarly vague symptoms. That is why the diagnostic approach matters far more than simple road-test impressions. When everything is right, the engine feels authoritative and quiet; when something starts to go wrong, it can lose its polish before it loses its basic ability to move the car quickly.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "300-330 hp",
