    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain located at the absolute rear of the engine block against the firewall. This heavily stressed single-row chain is notorious for stretching over high mileages, leading to a distinct metallic rattling noise during cold engine starts. Replacing the chain and its tensioners is highly labor-intensive, often requiring the complete removal of the transmission to access the timing cover.",
    "injectionType": "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI). Early iterations of this Euro 5 engine were equipped with highly problematic Delphi piezo-electric injectors that failed frequently. Mercedes-Benz issued a massive global recall to replace these with much more reliable solenoid-type injectors. This specific fleet variant is software-restricted for Belgian tax and European leasing optimization.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The OM651 cast iron block is legendary for its taxi-proven durability and can easily cover half a million kilometers if maintained properly. Because this fleet version produces less power, the internal components are even less stressed. Lifespan is entirely dictated by proactively addressing the rear timing chain.",
    "turboType": "Sequential Twin-Turbochargers (BorgWarner). Utilizes a smaller high-pressure turbo for instant low-end response and a larger low-pressure turbo for top-end power. The hardware is completely identical to the 170 hp variant, meaning the turbos operate with a large safety margin due to the restricted software.",
    "ecuType": "Delphi CRD3. A highly complex engine management unit that tightly controls the sequential turbos. This specific fleet variant features an artificial software restriction capping the output at 163 horsepower. It responds incredibly well to aftermarket ECU tuning, allowing the engine to safely unlock its original design potential.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 5",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "250+ hp. The incredibly robust cast iron block and forged internal components are massively over-engineered. The engine handles significant boost increases effortlessly, with tuning limits being dictated strictly by exhaust gas temperatures and DPF flow capacity.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "6.2 - 7.5 L/100km",
      "highway": "4.2 - 5.2 L/100km",
      "mixed": "5.2 - 6.2 L/100km",
      "sporty": "8.0+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.51). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is absolutely mandatory to protect the fragile rear timing chain and prevent the diesel particulate filter (DPF) from clogging prematurely.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "Delphi Injector Failure: Early models were equipped with highly problematic Delphi piezo-electric injectors that failed in massive numbers. Mercedes issued a global recall to replace them with more reliable solenoid-type injectors. Check the service history to ensure this crucial recall was performed.",
      "Rear Timing Chain Stretch: The OM651 utilizes a single-row timing chain located at the rear of the engine. It frequently stretches over high mileages, causing a sharp metallic rattling noise on cold starts. Ignoring this noise will lead to worn camshaft sprockets and potential engine failure.",
      "Coolant Pump Leaks: The engine-driven water pump is housed in a plastic assembly that degrades from constant thermal cycling. It frequently develops slow leaks, causing coolant to pool in the engine undertray. Immediate replacement is necessary to prevent severe overheating.",
      "Intake Manifold Soot Buildup: The combination of Exhaust Gas Recirculation (EGR) and oil vapor creates a thick, tar-like sludge inside the intake manifold. This soot restricts airflow, jams the swirl flaps, and causes severe hesitation. The intake manifold must be periodically removed and physically cleaned.",
      "Oil Pump Solenoid Wicking: The electrical connector for the variable oil pump solenoid can develop an internal seal leak. Engine oil wicks up through the wiring harness and can travel entirely into the main ECU. A sacrificial pigtail harness must be installed to prevent this catastrophic electrical damage.",
      "Split Intercooler Hoses: The heavy-duty rubber hoses routing compressed air from the twin turbos to the intercooler are subjected to immense pressure and oil mist. They frequently split or tear, creating a massive unmetered boost leak. This results in an immediate loss of power, heavy black smoke, and Limp Mode."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Jump: If the cold-start timing chain rattle is completely ignored, the stretched chain will eventually skip teeth on the camshaft sprockets. This destroys the engine's mechanical timing, causing the pistons to violently smash into the valves. This catastrophic event requires a financially devastating total engine rebuild.",
      "Diesel Engine Runaway: If the internal seals of the turbochargers fail catastrophically, they can dump pressurized engine oil directly into the intake tract. The diesel engine will burn its own oil as fuel, accelerating uncontrollably to the redline until it literally blows apart. This terrifying event cannot be stopped by simply turning off the ignition key.",
      "DPF Fatal Blockage: Using this diesel engine strictly for short, urban commutes prevents the exhaust from reaching temperatures high enough for active DPF regeneration. The diesel particulate filter will become permanently clogged with soot, creating massive backpressure. This backpressure destroys the turbochargers and requires an incredibly expensive DPF replacement.",
      "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: If the leaking oil pump solenoid connector is left unchecked, capillary action draws the oil through the wiring harness directly into the Delphi ECU. Once the delicate circuitry is flooded with engine oil, the entire unit shorts out. This requires a new engine control unit and a heavily involved wiring harness replacement.",
      "Swirl Flap Ingestion: If the heavy soot buildup in the intake manifold jams the plastic swirl flaps, the extreme pressure can cause the fragile plastic flaps to break off. These broken plastic pieces are sucked directly into the combustion chamber. This causes immediate and severe physical damage to the intake valves and pistons.",
      "Injector Hydrolock: If a severely degraded fuel injector becomes stuck in the open position, it will continuously dump raw diesel fuel directly into the cylinder. Because liquid cannot be compressed, the piston will abruptly stop during the compression stroke. This violent hydrolock instantly bends the forged connecting rod and destroys the engine block."
    ],
