    "yearRange": "2009-2011",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven 72-degree V6 diesel timing layout. In the early pre-facelift C207 and A207 E 350 CDI, the OM642.836 delivers the classic Mercedes V6 diesel formula in a very pure form, because it combines strong low-end torque, quiet long-distance composure, and a substantial mechanical feel without the heavier emissions complexity of later BlueTEC versions. This engine belongs to the earlier stage of the OM642 story, and that gives it a slightly more direct and traditional character that many diesel Mercedes enthusiasts still prefer. In the C207 coupe and A207 cabriolet, that character works extremely well because the platform benefits more from dense, flexible torque and quiet authority than from high-revving drama. On the road, the engine feels mature and expensive in the way a proper upper-class Mercedes diesel should. A healthy example has the kind of effortless reserve that makes the whole car feel underworked and deeply competent.",
    "injectionType": "Common-rail direct diesel injection with piezo injectors, turbocharging, and intercooling. In this early OM642.836 form, the injection system gives the engine a smooth and muscular torque delivery that feels especially natural in the E-Class coupe and cabriolet, because the car does not need sharp response as much as it needs clean, broad, and confident thrust. The piezo-injector era also contributes to the engine’s refined combustion feel when everything is working properly, particularly at cruising speeds and part-throttle load. At the same time, injector condition, combustion balance, boost integrity, intake cleanliness, and thermal stability all matter greatly as mileage rises. A strong example feels dense, polished, and consistent in the way good Mercedes diesels often do. A weak one may still feel powerful enough, but it gradually loses the calm mechanical precision that makes this drivetrain genuinely special.",
    "engineLifespan": "250,000 to 420,000 km. The OM642.836 is fully capable of high mileage when serviced correctly, because the base V6 diesel architecture is fundamentally strong and was developed for exactly the kind of long-distance, torque-rich use that suits the E-Class platform. Compared with later BlueTEC versions, this earlier Euro 5-era configuration often feels a little simpler to own, but that does not mean it is free from typical OM642 age-related issues. Oil cooler seals, intake swirl wear, injector behavior, cooling-system stability, boost plumbing condition, and general maintenance discipline still determine whether the engine remains satisfying over time. A properly maintained example can feel strong, quiet, and refined well into high mileage, especially if it has seen regular motorway or long-route use. A neglected example usually becomes expensive through several medium-cost diesel faults rather than through one dramatic mechanical failure. The core engine is durable, but long-term ownership quality depends heavily on the health of the systems around it.",
    "turboType": "Turbocharged common-rail V6 diesel with intercooling. In the early E 350 CDI, the turbo system is tuned to emphasize strong and immediate mid-range torque rather than headline-driven performance theatrics, which is exactly what suits the C207 and A207 body styles best. The result is a car that feels full and expensive rather than frantic, especially at medium speeds where the OM642’s broad torque curve gives the coupe a calm but decisive way of gathering speed. This is one of the reasons the early E 350 CDI still feels so appealing: it gives the car genuine grand-touring character without needing artificial aggressiveness. When the turbo and charge-air system are healthy, the engine feels thick, smooth, and quietly authoritative. When boost leaks, control issues, or intake restrictions begin, the engine often loses refinement and density before it becomes obviously slow, which is why subtle drivability changes matter.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz common-rail diesel engine management with adaptive injection control, turbo regulation, thermal protection logic, and automatic-transmission torque coordination. In the early OM642.836, this control strategy feels relatively mature and cohesive, because it integrates torque delivery and gearbox behavior well while remaining less emissions-complex than the later Euro 6 BlueTEC generations. A healthy drivetrain feels calm, settled, and mechanically expensive, with clean torque delivery and unobtrusive automatic behavior. The drawback is that several different faults can still overlap in confusing ways, so injector imbalance, thermostat drift, EGR contamination, boost leakage, or transmission adaptation issues may all first appear as a vague loss of smoothness rather than a clear single-cause problem. That makes proper diagnostics far more valuable than casual impressions. When everything works correctly, the engine feels like a refined and deeply sorted Mercedes diesel. When it does not, the car often remains usable and quick enough, but no longer truly polished.",
