    "timingType": "Simplex timing chain located at the absolute rear of the engine block against the firewall. This heavily stressed single-row chain is notorious for stretching over high mileages, leading to a distinct metallic rattling noise during cold engine starts. Replacing the chain and its tensioners is highly labor-intensive, often requiring the complete removal of the transmission to access the timing cover.",
    "injectionType": "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI). These late-model Euro 6 engines utilize highly reliable solenoid-type injectors operating at 2000 bar. This BlueTEC version is heavily reliant on an incredibly complex Selective Catalytic Reduction (SCR / AdBlue) system injected directly into the exhaust stream to meet strict emissions standards.",
    "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. The OM651 cast iron block is legendary for its taxi-proven durability and can easily cover half a million kilometers if maintained properly. However, the lifespan and usability of this specific model are heavily threatened by the incredibly complex, failure-prone AdBlue (SCR) system and the rear timing chain.",
    "turboType": "Sequential Twin-Turbochargers (BorgWarner). Utilizes a smaller high-pressure turbo for instant low-end response and a larger low-pressure turbo for top-end power. This complex two-stage setup effectively eliminates turbo lag and maintains the monstrous 500 Nm torque curve.",
    "ecuType": "Delphi CRD3. A highly complex engine management unit that tightly controls the sequential turbos, high-pressure fuel injection, and the intricate AdBlue dosing system. The ECU responds exceptionally well to aftermarket software tuning, easily unlocking massive low-end torque.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6",
    "maxPowerStockInternals": "260+ hp. The incredibly robust cast iron block and forged internal components are massively over-engineered to shrug off the 500 Nm factory rating. The engine handles significant boost increases effortlessly, with tuning limits being dictated strictly by exhaust gas temperatures, DPF flow capacity, and AdBlue injection limits.",
    "fuelConsumption": {
      "city": "6.5 - 8.0 L/100km",
      "highway": "4.5 - 5.5 L/100km",
      "mixed": "5.5 - 6.5 L/100km",
      "sporty": "8.5+ L/100km"
    },
    "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.52). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is absolutely mandatory to protect the fragile rear timing chain, prevent DPF clogging, and protect the advanced emissions systems.",
    "commonIssues": [
      "AdBlue (SCR) System Failures: The most notorious and expensive issue on the BlueTEC models. The AdBlue heater element inside the tank, the dosing pump, and the incredibly sensitive NOx sensors frequently fail. These failures trigger a terrifying dashboard countdown that will eventually prevent the car from starting entirely.",
      "Rear Timing Chain Stretch: The OM651 utilizes a single-row timing chain located at the rear of the engine. It frequently stretches over high mileages, causing a sharp metallic rattling noise on cold starts. Ignoring this noise will lead to worn camshaft sprockets and potential engine failure.",
      "Coolant Pump Leaks: The engine-driven water pump is housed in a plastic assembly that degrades from constant thermal cycling. It frequently develops slow leaks, causing coolant to pool in the engine undertray. Immediate replacement is necessary to prevent severe overheating.",
      "Intake Manifold Soot Buildup: The combination of Exhaust Gas Recirculation (EGR) and crankcase oil vapor creates a thick, tar-like sludge inside the intake manifold. This soot restricts airflow, jams the swirl flaps, and causes severe hesitation. The intake manifold must be periodically removed and physically cleaned.",
      "Oil Pump Solenoid Wicking: The electrical connector for the variable oil pump solenoid can develop an internal seal leak. Engine oil wicks up through the wiring harness and can travel entirely into the main ECU. A sacrificial pigtail harness must be installed to prevent this catastrophic electrical damage.",
      "Split Intercooler Hoses: The heavy-duty rubber hoses routing compressed air from the twin turbos to the intercooler are subjected to immense pressure and oil mist. They frequently split or tear, creating a massive unmetered boost leak. This results in an immediate loss of power, heavy black smoke, and Limp Mode."
    ],
    "vulnerabilities": [
      "AdBlue No-Start Countdown: If the main computer detects any fault within the incredibly complex SCR (AdBlue) system—whether it is a bad heater, a clogged nozzle, or a failing NOx sensor—it initiates a hard countdown. Once the permitted miles or engine starts reach zero, the ECU will completely immobilize the vehicle, leaving you stranded.",
      "Catastrophic Timing Chain Jump: If the cold-start timing chain rattle is completely ignored, the stretched chain will eventually skip teeth on the camshaft sprockets. This destroys the engine's mechanical timing, causing the pistons to violently smash into the valves, requiring a total engine rebuild.",
      "Diesel Engine Runaway: If the internal seals of the turbochargers fail catastrophically, they can dump pressurized engine oil directly into the intake tract. The diesel engine will burn its own oil as fuel, accelerating uncontrollably to the redline until it literally blows apart.",
      "DPF Fatal Blockage: Using this diesel engine strictly for short, urban commutes prevents the exhaust from reaching temperatures high enough for active DPF regeneration. The diesel particulate filter will become permanently clogged with soot, creating massive backpressure that destroys the turbos.",
      "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: If the leaking oil pump solenoid connector is left unchecked, capillary action draws the oil through the wiring harness directly into the Delphi ECU. Once the delicate circuitry is flooded with engine oil, the entire unit shorts out.",
      "Swirl Flap Ingestion: If the heavy soot buildup in the intake manifold jams the plastic swirl flaps, the extreme pressure can cause the fragile plastic flaps to break off. These broken plastic pieces are sucked directly into the combustion chamber, severely damaging the engine internals."
    ],
