    "yearRange": "2017-2019",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-four timing layout. In the pre-facelift C238 and A238 E 200, the M274.920 gives the car a smoother and lighter character than the larger six-cylinder options, because it focuses more on balance, usable mid-range response, and efficiency than on outright drama. This engine suits the coupe and cabriolet surprisingly well if the goal is a refined daily grand-tourer rather than a heavy torque-diesel or more expensive high-output petrol version. On the road, it feels cleaner and more eager than older small turbo Mercedes engines, especially in normal urban and mixed driving. The M274 does not try to overwhelm the chassis with force, but instead gives the E-Class coupe a calm, polished, and modern petrol personality. In healthy form, it feels composed and efficient without making the car feel underpowered for regular use.",
    "injectionType": "Direct-injection turbocharged petrol engine with intercooling and modern combustion management. In the E 200 C238, the M274.920 is tuned to deliver a broad and practical torque band rather than a highly aggressive top-end character, which matches the relaxed premium feel of the platform. The direct-injection setup helps the engine feel responsive at lower rpm, while the turbocharger fills in the mid-range so the car does not need to be worked hard in normal driving. This makes the coupe feel more effortless than its output figure alone might suggest. At the same time, as with many direct-injection turbo petrol engines, long-term refinement depends heavily on oil quality, cooling-system health, intake cleanliness, and timing-system condition. A healthy example feels smooth, quiet, and well integrated with the 9G-TRONIC automatic.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 350,000 km. The M274.920 can deliver solid long-term service life when maintained properly, but it is not the kind of engine that rewards neglect, especially because turbo heat, direct injection, and timing-system wear all place more stress on support systems than on a simpler naturally aspirated petrol engine. In the C238 and A238 E 200, the engine usually ages well when serviced on sensible oil intervals and kept thermally stable. However, ownership quality depends heavily on timing-chain behavior, cam-adjuster condition, turbo plumbing health, coolant-system integrity, and carbon-related intake cleanliness. A strong example remains refined and efficient well into higher mileage, especially if it sees regular mixed or highway use. A weak example tends to lose polish gradually through several medium-sized modern turbo-petrol issues rather than through one single dramatic failure. The engine is fully usable long-term, but maintenance discipline makes a major difference.",
    "turboType": "Turbocharged inline-four petrol engine with intercooling. In this E 200 form, the turbo system is calibrated to give the coupe a flexible and premium feel rather than a sharp or overly sporty one, so the engine responds best when driven smoothly and allowed to build torque progressively. That character suits the C238 platform well because the chassis is more grand-touring oriented than aggressively lightweight or overtly performance-focused. In normal use, the engine feels quieter and more elastic than many smaller turbo fours from the same era. When the boost system is healthy, the car accelerates cleanly and without fuss, which fits the Mercedes character very well. When boost leaks, wastegate issues, or heat-related hose ageing appear, the engine often first feels flatter and less cohesive before it becomes obviously slow. That makes subtle drivability changes worth taking seriously.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management with adaptive fueling, ignition control, turbo regulation, thermal protection logic, and automatic-transmission torque coordination. In the M274.920 E 200, this gives the car a smooth and cohesive response profile, where throttle, boost, and gearbox behavior are meant to work together in a very polished way. A healthy setup produces calm, seamless daily drivability, which is one of the biggest strengths of this engine in the E-Class coupe. The downside is that several different faults can overlap in similarly subtle ways, including timing-related wear, thermostat issues, ignition behavior, boost leakage, and carbon-related drivability deterioration. That is why proper diagnostics matter much more than judging the car only by a brief road test. When the engine is healthy, it feels modern, smooth, and appropriately premium. When it starts to age badly, it often becomes less clean and less settled long before it becomes truly unusable.",
