    "power": "197 hp",
    "torque": "320 Nm",
    "fuelType": "Petrol",
    "cylinders": 4,
    "valves": 16,
    "compression": "10.5:1",
    "yearRange": "2019-2023",
    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-four timing layout. In the facelift C238 and A238 E 200, the M264.920 gives the car a smoother, more modern character than the earlier M274 because the drivetrain feels more integrated, cleaner in response, and better supported at low rpm by the 48V EQ Boost system. This engine suits the coupe and cabriolet well if the goal is refined, efficient daily use rather than dramatic outright performance. On the road it feels calmer and more polished than its output figure alone suggests, particularly in urban and mixed driving where the mild-hybrid support helps the car feel lighter on its feet. The M264 does not try to turn the E-Class coupe into a sports car, but it does give it a very contemporary premium-petrol character. In healthy form, it feels smooth, quiet, and intelligently assisted rather than merely turbocharged.",
    "injectionType": "Direct-injection turbocharged petrol engine with intercooling and EQ Boost 48V mild-hybrid assistance. In the facelift E 200 C238, the M264.920 is calibrated for broader real-world drivability than the older setup, so the engine responds more progressively and feels less strained in normal use. The direct-injection system and turbocharger provide the core torque delivery, while the integrated starter-generator adds subtle low-speed support that makes the car feel more cohesive in traffic and smoother during transitions. This is one of the reasons the facelift E 200 feels more polished than the earlier car in everyday use. At the same time, long-term refinement still depends heavily on oil quality, timing-system condition, cooling-system health, intake cleanliness, and the reliability of the 48V support hardware. A healthy example feels modern and composed, while a weak one usually loses smoothness before it loses basic performance.",
    "engineLifespan": "200,000 to 320,000 km. The M264.920 can deliver strong long-term service life if it is maintained properly, but like many modern direct-injection turbo petrol engines, it depends far more on maintenance discipline than on raw mechanical simplicity. In the facelift C238 and A238 E 200, ownership quality is shaped not only by the engine itself, but also by timing-chain behavior, cylinder-head condition, carbon buildup, cooling-system reliability, and the health of the EQ Boost 48V layer. A strong example remains smooth, efficient, and refined well into higher mileage, especially if it has seen regular mixed or highway use rather than constant short-trip duty. A weaker example usually becomes costly through several medium-sized modern turbo and hybrid-support issues rather than through one immediate catastrophic event. The base concept is good, but the surrounding systems need to stay just as healthy as the core engine.",
    "turboType": "Turbocharged inline-four petrol engine with intercooling and 48V mild-hybrid support. In this facelift E 200 form, the turbo system is tuned for clean, flexible real-world response rather than sharp or aggressive sportiness, which suits the grand-touring nature of the C238 very well. The added EQ Boost layer improves the sense of initial smoothness and helps the drivetrain feel less busy at lower speeds, so the car comes across as more mature and better integrated than a simple small turbo engine might suggest. In normal use, the engine feels elastic and quietly capable rather than urgent. When the boost system is healthy, the car accelerates cleanly and progressively with very little fuss. When boost leaks, wastegate issues, actuator problems, or charge-air weaknesses appear, the engine often first feels flatter and less polished before it becomes obviously slow.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management with adaptive fueling, ignition control, turbo regulation, thermal protection logic, 48V EQ Boost integration, and automatic-transmission torque coordination. In the M264.920 E 200, this creates a more interconnected drivetrain character than the earlier M274 setup because the engine, starter-generator, and gearbox all work together to smooth load changes and improve drivability. A healthy system feels modern, polished, and very well calibrated for everyday premium use. The drawback is that multiple different problems can overlap in subtle ways, including timing-related wear, cylinder-head issues, cooling faults, hybrid-system warnings, boost leaks, and carbon-related drivability decline. That makes proper diagnostics far more important than judging the car only by a short road test. When everything is right, the engine feels smart and seamless; when it begins to age badly, it usually becomes less clean and less settled before it becomes truly unusable.",
    "euroStandard": "Euro 6d-TEMP-EVAP-ISC",
