    "timingType": "Double overhead camshaft, 4-valve-per-cylinder, chain-driven inline-four timing layout. In this reduced-output fleet calibration, the M274.920 keeps the same modern mechanical base as the stronger E 200 versions, but the overall character is calmer and less eager because the software focuses more on tax efficiency, smoother everyday drivability, and easier fleet operation than on performance. In the pre-facelift C238 and A238, this gives the car a more subdued premium-grand-tourer feel. The engine still feels refined enough for daily use, but acceleration is intentionally softer and less urgent than the standard 184 hp version. That makes this version more about image, comfort, and running-cost positioning than about strong coupe performance. In healthy form, it still delivers the polished, restrained petrol character expected from an E-Class coupe.",
    "injectionType": "Direct-injection turbocharged petrol engine with intercooling and modern combustion management. In this fleet-spec E 200, the M274.920 is intentionally calibrated below its better-known 184 hp form, so the engine feels more relaxed and less aggressive in the mid-range. The direct-injection layout still gives clean low-rpm response, while the turbocharger preserves enough flexibility to stop the car from feeling completely flat in normal traffic. This setup works best when driven smoothly rather than with heavy throttle inputs. The lower-output tune can feel more measured than exciting, but it remains well suited to calm commuting and business use. Long-term refinement still depends heavily on oil quality, cooling-system health, intake cleanliness, and timing-system condition.",
    "engineLifespan": "220,000 to 350,000 km. The M274.920 can deliver good long-term service life when maintained properly, and this softer fleet-oriented tune does not remove the known support-system sensitivities of the engine family. In the C238 and A238 E 200, long-term ownership quality still depends heavily on timing-chain behavior, cam-adjuster condition, coolant-system stability, turbo plumbing integrity, and carbon-related intake cleanliness. A strong example stays smooth and efficient at higher mileage, especially when serviced on conservative oil intervals and used regularly on mixed or highway trips. A weak example usually declines through several medium-sized modern turbo-petrol issues rather than one dramatic failure. The lower-output software does not make the engine old-school simple, but it can make daily stress levels slightly easier on the drivetrain when properly maintained.",
    "turboType": "Turbocharged inline-four petrol engine with intercooling. In this fleet E 200 configuration, the turbo system is tuned for smoother and more modest real-world delivery rather than stronger mid-range punch, so the drivetrain feels deliberately restrained. That matches the purpose of this version because it is aimed more at taxation and fleet compliance than at maximizing the coupe's grand-touring potential. In normal driving, the engine still feels cleaner and more flexible than a naturally aspirated unit of similar output. When the boost system is healthy, the car moves with reasonable smoothness and calmness. When boost leaks, wastegate issues, or ageing charge-air hoses appear, the engine tends to feel especially flat because the starting point is already a softer calibration.",
    "ecuType": "Mercedes-Benz direct-injection turbo petrol engine management with adaptive fueling, ignition control, turbo regulation, thermal protection logic, and automatic-transmission torque coordination. In this detuned M274.920 E 200, the software strategy prioritizes smoother low-load behavior and lower-output delivery over headline performance, which gives the car a very measured daily-use character. A healthy setup still feels cohesive and premium enough in light-to-medium use. The downside is that several different faults can remain subtle for a long time, including timing-related wear, thermostat issues, ignition weakness, boost leakage, and carbon-related drivability decline. That means proper diagnosis matters much more than relying on a short test drive. When healthy, the engine feels calm and civilized; when it starts to age badly, it becomes less clean and less settled before it becomes truly problematic.",
