      "popupAnimationName": "",
      "drivingExperience": "Driving the OM654-powered E 220 d is an exercise in extreme, highly refined efficiency. While the 194 horsepower and 400 Nm of torque are not designed to win drag races, the variable geometry turbocharger provides immediate, satisfying thrust for city driving and merging onto highways. The most noticeable improvement over the older OM651 is the sheer acoustic refinement; this all-aluminum engine is incredibly quiet, completely lacking the harsh, industrial clatter of older generations. Mated to the brilliant 9G-Tronic transmission, it keeps RPMs phenomenally low, serving as an unbelievably composed, silent intercontinental cruiser that easily returns fuel economy figures that rival small hatchbacks.",
      "mechanicVerdict": "The OM654 2.0-liter turbocharged diesel in the E 220 d represents a massive technological leap for Mercedes-Benz's four-cylinder lineup. By utilizing an all-aluminum block, advanced steel pistons, and a highly precise fuel injection system, they successfully delivered excellent torque with astonishing fuel economy and acoustic refinement. However, severe manufacturer oversights in its peripheral component design earn this highly advanced powertrain a cautious 6/10 risk rating. The absolute most terrifying and financially devastating flaw is catastrophic camshaft rocker arm wear. If oil quality degrades even slightly, the roller cam followers literally grind themselves flat against the camshaft lobes, destroying the valvetrain and filling the engine with metal shavings. Upgrading these rocker arms preventatively is the smartest thing an owner can do. Secondly, Mercedes retained the incredibly controversial rear-mounted timing chain layout. Over high mileages, the chain inevitably stretches, causing a sharp metallic cold-start rattle. Replacing this rear chain requires massive labor hours, frequently necessitating dropping the entire transmission. The defining headache of this modern diesel, however, is the highly sensitive SCR (AdBlue) system. The AdBlue heater unit, the dosing pumps, and the incredibly fragile NOx sensors fail with terrifying regularity, triggering a hard dashboard countdown that will completely immobilize the vehicle if not expensively repaired at the dealership. Furthermore, the combined vacuum/oil tandem pump located at the rear of the block is infamous for weeping oil down the transmission bellhousing. Do not buy this car for short city commutes, as the DPF will inevitably clog. If you proactively upgrade the rocker arms, address the timing chain tensioner, and maintain a hefty reserve fund for the inevitable AdBlue system failures, the OM654 provides unbelievable efficiency, but it demands uncompromising maintenance."
