        "timingType": "Simplex timing chain located at the absolute rear of the engine block. While chain durability is a significant improvement over the older OM651, stretching can still occur at high mileages if oil changes are neglected. Because of its rearward position against the firewall, replacing it requires dropping the transmission or hoisting the entire engine.",
        "injectionType": "Common-Rail Direct Injection (CDI) operating at an immense 2,050 bar pressure via highly precise Piezo injectors. This extreme pressure ensures phenomenal fuel atomization and power delivery but makes the fuel system highly sensitive to poor-quality diesel or water contamination. This entry-level version utilizes software restrictions to limit output.",
        "engineLifespan": "300 000 - 500 000+ km. Because this specific OM654 variant is artificially software-restricted to a mere 160 hp, the incredibly robust all-aluminum block and forged steel pistons experience virtually zero mechanical stress. The true lifespan is entirely dictated by strict adherence to oil changes to protect the fragile valvetrain and the maintenance of the complex AdBlue systems.",
        "turboType": "Single Variable Nozzle Turbine (VNT) with water cooling. Utilizes the identical highly advanced variable geometry turbocharger found on the more powerful 220d model, meaning it operates well below its maximum thermal and rotational capacity due to the software restriction.",
        "ecuType": "Bosch MD1. An incredibly advanced, highly complex engine management unit. Because the hardware is practically identical to the 194 hp E 220 d, this ECU responds spectacularly to aftermarket software tuning, completely removing the artificial factory restrictions.",
        "euroStandard": "Euro 6d-TEMP / Euro 6d",
        "maxPowerStockInternals": "260+ hp. The incredibly robust aluminum block, combined with heavy-duty forged steel pistons, is massively over-engineered to handle torque well above the factory 360 Nm rating. The engine handles significant boost increases effortlessly.",
        "fuelConsumption": {
          "city": "5.3 - 6.5 L/100km",
          "highway": "3.8 - 4.8 L/100km",
          "mixed": "4.5 - 5.5 L/100km",
          "sporty": "7.0+ L/100km"
        },
        "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-30 Fully Synthetic Low-SAPS (MB 229.52). Absolute adherence to strict 10,000 km oil change intervals is absolutely mandatory to protect the highly stressed rocker arms, prevent DPF clogging, and lubricate the rear timing chain.",
        "commonIssues": [
          "Camshaft Rocker Arm Wear: The absolute most critical flaw of the early OM654 architecture. The roller cam followers (rocker arms) can wear out rapidly, especially if oil quality degrades over time. This causes a distinct ticking noise from the top end, which, if ignored, will eventually destroy the camshaft lobes entirely.",
          "Rear Timing Chain Stretch: Mercedes deliberately located the single-row timing chain at the absolute rear of the engine. While stronger than previous generations, it still stretches over high mileages, causing a sharp metallic rattling noise on cold starts. Replacing it requires a massively expensive engine-out or transmission-out procedure.",
          "Complex SCR/AdBlue System Failures: The highly advanced Euro 6 emissions system heavily relies on continuous AdBlue injection directly into the exhaust. The heater element inside the tank, the dosing pump, and the incredibly sensitive NOx sensors frequently fail from thermal stress, triggering a dashboard countdown that immobilizes the vehicle.",
          "Engine-Mounted DPF/SCR Thermal Stress: To heat up the emissions system quickly, the DPF and dual SCR catalysts are bolted directly to the side of the engine block. This unique packaging subjects surrounding sensors and wiring harnesses to extreme radiant heat, causing premature electrical degradation and sensor failures.",
          "Tandem Pump Oil Leaks: The combined vacuum and oil pump located at the rear of the engine block is known to develop slow weeping oil leaks over tens of thousands of kilometers. Because it is positioned tightly against the firewall, diagnosing and repairing this leak is a highly labor-intensive nightmare.",
          "EGR Valve and Cooler Clogging: The Exhaust Gas Recirculation (EGR) system aggressively routes dirty exhaust back into the intake to lower NOx emissions. This creates thick, tar-like soot that heavily clogs the EGR cooler and the entire intake manifold over time, requiring periodic physical cleaning to prevent hesitation."
        ],
        "vulnerabilities": [
          "Catastrophic Valvetrain Failure: If the notorious rocker arm wear is ignored, the roller bearing will seize, and the camshaft lobe will literally grind the follower flat. This fills the engine oil with fine metallic glitter, destroying the main bearings and requiring a completely new cylinder head and camshaft assembly.",
          "AdBlue No-Start Immobilization Countdown: If the main engine computer detects any fault within the SCR emissions system, it immediately initiates a hard, irreversible dashboard countdown. Once the permitted miles or engine starts reach absolute zero, the ECU completely immobilizes the vehicle until fixed at the dealership.",
          "Engine-Out Timing Chain Replacement Cost: Because the timing chain drive is located at the absolute rear of the engine block, access is virtually impossible while the engine is in the car. If the chain stretches and threatens to jump teeth, mechanics must completely remove the transmission, turning a wear item into a massive labor bill.",
          "DPF Fatal Blockage from Urban Use: The OM654 requires extremely hot exhaust temperatures for extended periods to perform active regeneration cycles for the Diesel Particulate Filter. If the vehicle is used strictly for short city commutes, the DPF will become fatally blocked with dense soot, requiring an incredibly expensive filter replacement.",
          "Injector Hydrolock: The 2050-bar Piezo direct injectors work under immense pressure and can, in rare cases of severe fuel contamination, stick completely open. Raw liquid diesel fuel rapidly fills the combustion chamber, causing a violent hydrolock that instantly bends the forged connecting rod and destroys the block.",
          "Coolant Intrusion via EGR Cooler: The internal passages of the EGR cooler can crack due to intense thermal cycling, allowing engine coolant to slowly leak directly into the exhaust stream or intake tract. This causes mysterious coolant loss and thick white exhaust smoke, eventually leading to severe overheating."
        ],
