            "valves": 24,
            "compression": "10.5:1",
            "yearRange": "2017-2018",
            "timingType": "Double-roller chain. The M276 architecture utilizes independent timing chains for each cylinder bank, driven from the front of the engine. Early iterations were notorious for secondary chain tensioner rattle, but by this 2017 production run, the tensioners and check valves were generally updated from the factory. However, listening for a brief metallic rattle on cold starts remains critical, as high-mileage examples can still suffer from hydraulic leak-down.",
            "injectionType": "Direct Injection (CGI) via highly precise piezo-electric injectors operating at up to 200 bar. This system delivers immense efficiency and perfect atomization for the twin turbochargers, but it inherently guarantees the accumulation of thick carbon sludge on the intake valves over long-term ownership since no fuel washes the ports.",
            "engineLifespan": "250 000 - 450 000+ miles. This 3.0L BiTurbo variant is arguably one of the most bulletproof modern petrol engines ever fitted to the C238 chassis. Because it uses a heavily over-engineered block to generate a highly conservative 329 horsepower, the twin turbochargers operate at incredibly low, completely unstressed boost pressures from the factory.",
            "turboType": "Twin Turbochargers (BiTurbo). Nestled tightly alongside the 60-degree V6 block, they operate at extremely low peak boost to maintain the 329 hp target, providing instantaneous throttle response. The turbos themselves are highly reliable, but the fragile plastic coolant lines feeding them represent a critical, heat-induced failure point.",
            "ecuType": "Bosch MED17.7.3. A highly advanced ECU managing direct injection and dual boost pressure control with specific SAE calibration for the US market. The unhindered exhaust flow means the engine offers astronomical tuning potential, easily bridging the gap to AMG-level performance.",
            "euroStandard": "US EPA Tier 3",
            "maxPowerStockInternals": "500+ hp. The internal forged connecting rods and micro-alloyed crankshaft are heavily over-engineered. The block handles massive boost effortlessly, as this engine is essentially a detuned version of higher-tier AMG-lite (43 AMG) powertrains, limited only by the intercooler's thermal capacity.",
            "fuelConsumption": {
              "city": "11.5 - 13.5 L/100km",
              "highway": "7.5 - 8.8 L/100km",
              "mixed": "9.0 - 10.5 L/100km",
              "sporty": "14.5+ L/100km"
            },
            "oilSpecification": "6.5L 5W-40 Fully Synthetic (MB 229.5). Absolute adherence to strict 5,000 to 7,500-mile oil change intervals is mandatory to protect the delicate twin-turbos, the highly stressed hydraulic timing chain tensioners, and the sensitive Nanoslide cylinder wall coatings.",
            "commonIssues": [
              "Turbocharger Coolant Line Brittleness: The twin turbochargers are supplied with coolant via incredibly thin plastic lines routed directly over the blistering hot exhaust manifolds. Over tens of thousands of miles, these lines literally bake in the heat, becoming so brittle they can snap simply from engine vibration. Replacing them preventatively with billet metal alternatives is absolutely critical.",
              "Camshaft Sensor Oil Leaking: The four camshaft position sensors are highly prone to internal rubber seal failure due to intense ambient engine bay heat. Engine oil slowly pushes through the sensor body directly into the electrical connector and wiring harness. If ignored, this oil travels through the wiring via capillary action and causes major electrical issues.",
              "Timing Chain Tensioner Rattle: Even with late-model factory updates, the secondary hydraulic chain tensioners can eventually lose their ability to hold oil pressure overnight on high-mileage engines. This causes a terrifying metallic rattling noise on cold starts as the loose chain slaps the plastic guides before oil pressure builds. Installing new tensioners and check valves resolves this.",
              "Direct Injection Carbon Buildup: Because fuel is injected directly into the cylinders, it never washes over the intake valves to keep them clean. Over time, thick, tar-like carbon sludge builds up heavily on the valve stems and intake tracts. This causes random misfires, rough idling, and heavily restricts engine airflow, requiring professional walnut blasting.",
              "High-Pressure Fuel Pump (HPFP) Wear: The camshaft-driven high-pressure fuel pumps work immensely hard to support the direct injection and twin-turbo boost levels. They can wear out internally, causing extended cranking times, severe power loss, and potentially leaking raw fuel into the oil pan. This dramatically lowers oil viscosity and threatens the main bearings.",
              "Intercooler Pump Heat Soak: The air-to-water intercooler system relies on a small electric pump to circulate coolant and lower intake temperatures. This factory pump is frequently overwhelmed during spirited driving or harsh summer months. When intake temperatures skyrocket, the ECU aggressively pulls engine timing, making the car feel extremely sluggish."
            ],
            "vulnerabilities": [
              "Catastrophic Coolant Loss: When the highly brittle plastic turbo coolant lines inevitably snap, they dump the engine's coolant supply almost instantly onto the hot exhaust pipes. If the driver does not notice the warning and pull over immediately, the aluminum engine block will warp from extreme overheating. This completely destroys the engine and requires a massive replacement bill.",
              "ECU Destruction via Oil Wicking: If the leaking camshaft position sensors are ignored, capillary action will draw the escaped engine oil through the wiring harness directly into the Bosch ECU. Once the delicate circuitry of the ECU is flooded with oil, it shorts out and requires complete replacement. Installing preventative sacrificial pigtail harnesses is mandatory.",
              "Catastrophic Timing Chain Jump: If the cold-start chain rattle is ignored for an extended period, the timing chain will physically stretch beyond the tensioner's operational limits. The loose chain will eventually jump teeth on the camshaft sprockets, completely ruining the engine's mechanical timing. This causes the pistons to violently strike the open valves.",
              "Fatal Cylinder Wall Scoring: The M276 utilizes a specialized Nanoslide aluminum block coating that is highly susceptible to severe scoring if a fuel injector fails and washes the lubricating oil away. This results in extreme oil consumption, aggressive misfires, and a heavy knocking sound from the bottom end. Repair requires re-sleeving the block or total replacement.",
              "Transmission Torque Overload: The massive 480 Nm of factory torque, which dramatically increases with tuning, places immense, continuous strain on the 9G-Tronic transmission clutches. Bypassing the strict 75,000-mile fluid service interval practically guarantees premature torque converter glazing and severe transmission shuddering.",
              "Subframe Corrosion Collapse: The rear subframe of the C238 chassis is highly susceptible to severe internal rusting, particularly in states using heavy winter road salt. The structural metal rots entirely from the inside out, often causing the lower control arms to detach abruptly while driving at high speeds."
            ],
