                "popupAnimationName": "",
                "drivingExperience": "Driving the mild-hybrid M254-powered E 300 is an absolutely mind-bending exercise in highly compressed, electrified four-cylinder performance. The 258 horsepower and 400 Nm of torque derived from the internal combustion engine are supplemented by an instant 200 Nm electric torque fill from the 2nd-Generation 48V ISG integrated into the transmission. This means turbo lag does not simply feel minimized; it physically does not exist when pulling away from a stoplight. The coupe surges forward from a standstill with sudden, relentless force, seamlessly blending electric and petrol power. The 9G-Tronic transmission shifts invisibly, and the engine start/stop system is so incredibly smooth due to the ISG that it is practically imperceptible. It is a highly competent, phenomenally fast executive cruiser that trades classic V6 character for profound modern efficiency.",
                "mechanicVerdict": "The M254 2.0-liter turbocharged mild-hybrid petrol engine in the late-facelift E 300 is a technological marvel that pushes the absolute boundaries of what a 4-cylinder engine can achieve. By lengthening the stroke and integrating a 2nd-generation 48V Integrated Starter Generator (ISG) directly into the transmission bellhousing, Mercedes successfully extracted V6-levels of torque and staggering efficiency. However, this immense technological complexity earns this highly advanced powertrain a highly cautious 7/10 risk rating. The absolute most terrifying and financially devastating flaw is the 48V ISG system itself. If the 48V battery, inverter, or the transmission-mounted ISG fails, the car loses its starter motor entirely and becomes an immobilized brick, requiring an incredibly expensive trip to the dealership. Mercedes also controversially located the single-row timing chain at the absolute rear of the engine block. Over extremely high mileages, the chain inevitably stretches, causing a sharp metallic cold-start rattle. Replacing this rear chain requires dropping the entire transmission. Furthermore, while the forged steel pistons are incredibly robust, the engine operates at a terrifying 350-bar fuel pressure and remains vulnerable to Low-Speed Pre-Ignition (LSPI) if poor fuel is used or if the PCV system fails. The thermal management system is also astronomically complex, utilizing multiple electric and mechanical water pumps to cool the battery, the turbo, and the block; these fragile plastic lines inevitably weep. You must install an aftermarket oil catch can to minimize the risk of LSPI and strictly adhere to 10,000-kilometer oil changes using premium fuel exclusively. If meticulously maintained, the M254 delivers staggering hybrid-assisted punch, but one minor electrical or maintenance oversight can result in a financially devastating repair."
